"page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"Clone and build"},"47":{"id":"/coder-templates-22","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"What it reuses, and the trust warning"},"48":{"id":"/coder-templates-23","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"From a git URL: `bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace`"},"49":{"id":"/coder-templates-24","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"What it decides"},"50":{"id":"/coder-templates-25","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"Inside the workspace container"},"51":{"id":"/coder-templates-26","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"What the startup script does, in order"},"52":{"id":"/coder-templates-27","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"Editor, terminal, and the metadata tiles"},"53":{"id":"/coder-templates-28","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The inlined code-server fork"},"54":{"id":"/coder-templates-29","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"Editor settings seed"},"55":{"id":"/coder-templates-30","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Terminal and editor defaults"},"56":{"id":"/coder-templates-31","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"Cloning more than one repository"},"57":{"id":"/coder-templates-32","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"GitHub credentials for a workspace"},"58":{"id":"/coder-templates-33","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#scriptsgithub-authsh-the-per-workspace-doctor","content":"`scripts/github-auth.sh`, the per-workspace doctor"},"59":{"id":"/coder-templates-34","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"What the two templates make, and when the `devcontainer` toggle matters"},"60":{"id":"/coder-templates-35","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"How `push-template.sh` ships the source, and the same push by hand"},"61":{"id":"/coder-templates-36","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Every create-form field and template variable, with its default"},"62":{"id":"/coder-templates-37","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Autostop, TTLs, Docker-in-Docker, and the DinD sidecar failure modes"},"63":{"id":"/coder-templates-38","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"},"64":{"id":"/coder-templates-39","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Choosing between the plain container and the devcontainer before you click create"},"65":{"id":"/coder-templates-40","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Reading a failed push or a stuck workspace against what the template actually does"},"66":{"id":"/coder-templates-41","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Changing a template variable without guessing which file owns it"},"67":{"id":"/coder-templates-42","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build,\nhow they get pushed, what each template variable means, and how the create form and the\nin-editor terminal and editor behave."},"68":{"id":"/coder-templates-43","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Related: project-conventions.md (shell and payload conventions),\nSECURITY.md, and\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md\n(the long prose on the tmux profiles and the Grok Build session)."},"69":{"id":"/coder-templates-44","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."},"70":{"id":"/coder-templates-45","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."},"71":{"id":"/coder-templates-46","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`devcontainer` (`templates/devcontainer/`) is the DinD-backed one."},"72":{"id":"/coder-templates-47","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."},"73":{"id":"/coder-templates-48","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`devcontainer` reuses `workspace`'s variables. `repo_url` exists in both; `dind` does\nnot exist in `devcontainer`."},"74":{"id":"/coder-templates-49","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"Each template dir keeps its own independent copy of the `.tftpl` payloads\n(`startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). Change one twin,\nchange the other."},"75":{"id":"/coder-templates-50","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."},"76":{"id":"/coder-templates-51","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the orchestration-protocol seed text the startup scripts\ninline into every workspace's `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` (read with `file()`, chomped)."},"77":{"id":"/coder-templates-52","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."},"78":{"id":"/coder-templates-53","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`notes-setup.sh` is the library behind each clone's private `notes.md`. The\nstartup script inlines it with `file()` the same way, writes it into\n`~/.grok/`, sources it, and calls `notes_materialize` per clone. That call also\nseeds the ignore entries that keep the file uncommitted; see \"What is shared\" in\n`docs/grok-build-and-agents.md` for what it writes into git config."},"79":{"id":"/coder-templates-54","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`, and `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the\nterminal payloads the startup script writes. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."},"80":{"id":"/coder-templates-55","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` and `coder-agents.md` are the Coder Agents\nskill and instruction seeds (chomped `file()`)."},"81":{"id":"/coder-templates-56","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`mcp-cache-proxy.json` is the context7 MCP cache-proxy blob the startup script\nwrites."},"82":{"id":"/coder-templates-57","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` is the shared GitHub-token refresh `coder_script`,\nrendered with `templatefile()` so `${github_auth_id}` and\n`${coder_github_app_install_url}` interpolate."},"83":{"id":"/coder-templates-58","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"Editing the common copy changes both templates; the staged copy overwrites any\nsame-named file the template dir still carries."},"84":{"id":"/coder-templates-59","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"Presets collapse the create form to one click."},"85":{"id":"/coder-templates-60","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."},"86":{"id":"/coder-templates-61","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"`devcontainer`: **Devcontainer default** (the template default) and **Plain clone**."},"87":{"id":"/coder-templates-62","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"Preset keys are parameter *names*; `display_name` is UI-only and would be ignored."},"88":{"id":"/coder-templates-63","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."},"89":{"id":"/coder-templates-64","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It resolves the repo root from `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout\n`bazelisk run` names) ahead of the runfiles copy, so `.env` and the template\nfiles are regular files rather than execroot symlinks."},"90":{"id":"/coder-templates-65","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It resolves the running stack on the Docker **daemon** by container label, never\nthrough a cwd-resolved compose project."},"91":{"id":"/coder-templates-66","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"Per template it checks `main.tf` exists (otherwise \"run this from the repo root\") and\nnotes a missing `.terraform.lock.hcl`."},"92":{"id":"/coder-templates-67","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It rejects a `coder_workspace_preset` description over Coder's 128-**byte** ceiling."},"93":{"id":"/coder-templates-68","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It rejects a `vscode-alt.svg` whose inlined data URL would exceed Coder's\n256-character `workspace_apps.icon` column."},"94":{"id":"/coder-templates-69","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It streams the source as a tar on stdin, with symlinks dereferenced into regular\nfiles. Coder's provisioner unpacks that archive into an empty workdir and does\nnot follow host paths, so a stored runfiles symlink plans as \"No configuration\nfiles\"."},"95":{"id":"/coder-templates-70","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It confirms an **active** version."},"96":{"id":"/coder-templates-71","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It re-sends the template's own description."},"97":{"id":"/coder-templates-72","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It re-pins **Autostop** to *Disabled*."},"98":{"id":"/coder-templates-73","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"No `.terraform.lock.hcl` is committed under `templates/`. Each `main.tf` pins\n`coder/coder` `~> 2.18` and `kreuzwerker/docker` `~> 4.6` in `required_providers`; Coder\npushes with a warning when no lockfile sits beside them. `coder_devcontainer` needs a\nserver `>= 2.21`, which this stack runs (v2.36.6)."},"99":{"id":"/coder-templates-74","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."},"100":{"id":"/coder-templates-75","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."},"101":{"id":"/coder-templates-76","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"Both the preset descriptions and the two routing hints fit the same 128-byte ceiling.\n`coder_parameter` descriptions are **not** capped (some run past 450 characters) and are\nnot checked."},"102":{"id":"/coder-templates-77","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"A failed description edit and a failed autostop pin only add a note; neither blocks the\ntemplate."},"103":{"id":"/coder-templates-78","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"A template whose push or verification failed is counted in the\n`N of M template(s) did not reach an active version` line and makes the run exit\nnon-zero, while every other template still gets its push attempt."},"104":{"id":"/coder-templates-79","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"No arguments pushes every template in `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT` order."},"105":{"id":"/coder-templates-80","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"Arguments are template *names*, and they select which names to push. A name that is not\none of the known names is an error that lists the known names, so a typo cannot quietly\ndo nothing."},"106":{"id":"/coder-templates-81","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"A repeated name is one push, not two, and the push order stays `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT`\norder whatever the argument order is. Naming `devcontainer` first cannot orphan it."},"107":{"id":"/coder-templates-82","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"It is also the manual path after you register an account, and the retry path for one\ntemplate."},"108":{"id":"/coder-templates-83","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Everything Coder-facing runs **inside the coder container**, against\n`http://127.0.0.1:3000`."},"109":{"id":"/coder-templates-84","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."},"110":{"id":"/coder-templates-85","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"`coder login` on the host must reach the API *before* it has a credential, and in\npublic mode the only URL it will use is `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, which Authelia gates."},"111":{"id":"/coder-templates-86","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Its first unauthenticated call (the first-user check) gets the HTML sign-in page back,\nand the CLI reports that as `unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"`."},"112":{"id":"/coder-templates-87","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Loopback inside the container cannot be gated, so the push cannot fail that way."},"113":{"id":"/coder-templates-88","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"It runs the image's own `coder` binary, so client and server are the same build."},"114":{"id":"/coder-templates-89","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"No host `coder` CLI and no `coder login` are involved, and a stale host-side session\ncannot aim the push at some other deployment."},"115":{"id":"/coder-templates-90","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."},"116":{"id":"/coder-templates-91","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Same trust model as `coder reset-password`, which also bypasses the API and talks to\nthe database directly."},"117":{"id":"/coder-templates-92","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."},"118":{"id":"/coder-templates-93","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Its scope has to be `coder:all` with `allow_list` `{*:*}`: a narrowed token fails the\nCLI's opening organization lookups, so least privilege is not available on v2.36."},"119":{"id":"/coder-templates-94","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."},"120":{"id":"/coder-templates-95","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"A push is also the only thing that *registers* a template: Coder stores the source as a\nFilestore row, not as files it scans on disk."},"121":{"id":"/coder-templates-96","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."},"122":{"id":"/coder-templates-97","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."},"123":{"id":"/coder-templates-98","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"Nothing else about GitHub travels in a `--var`."},"124":{"id":"/coder-templates-99","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as      | Condition                           |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `DEV_IMAGE`      | `--var image=` | always; default `evo-t1-dev:latest` |","table":"table-0"},"125":{"id":"/coder-templates-100","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`     | Passed as            | Condition                                                     |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` | `--var litellm_key=` | only when the key is real, never the `change-me*` placeholder |","table":"table-0"},"126":{"id":"/coder-templates-101","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`  | Passed as                | Condition                           |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |\n| `CODER_AGENT_URL` | `--var coder_agent_url=` | only when the key is present at all |","table":"table-0"},"127":{"id":"/coder-templates-102","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`    | Passed as                  | Condition                                                                                        |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `GROK_PROFILE_MODE` | `--var grok_profile_mode=` | when non-empty; the retired `GROK_PROFILE_SHARE` maps (`true` to `shared`, `false` to `private`) |","table":"table-0"},"128":{"id":"/coder-templates-103","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as         | Condition                       |\n| ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `REPO_URL`       | `--var repo_url=` | only when present and non-empty |","table":"table-0"},"129":{"id":"/coder-templates-104","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                | Condition                                                                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GITHUB_MCP`     | `--var github_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` (the github MCP server is on by default; any other value pushes nothing and the template default stands) |","table":"table-0"},"130":{"id":"/coder-templates-105","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                  | Condition                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `CONTEXT7_MCP`   | `--var context7_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` (the cached context7 MCP server is on by default; any other value pushes nothing and the template default stands) |","table":"table-0"},"131":{"id":"/coder-templates-106","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`   | Passed as                    | Condition                                      |\n| ------------------ | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` | `--var context7_api_key=...` | only when non-empty; the value is never logged |","table":"table-0"},"132":{"id":"/coder-templates-107","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                | Condition                      |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ |\n| `SEARCH_MCP`     | `--var search_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` |","table":"table-0"},"133":{"id":"/coder-templates-108","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`    | Passed as                                              | Condition                                                 |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` | `--var searxng_url=http://host.docker.internal:<port>` | always; a blank or non-numeric value falls back to `8181` |","table":"table-0"},"134":{"id":"/coder-templates-109","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`GITHUB_MCP`, `CONTEXT7_MCP` and `SEARCH_MCP` share one polarity: all three MCP blocks\nship from the template default (`true`), so the only value worth setting is `false`,\nand `true`/unset/empty pushes nothing at all."},"135":{"id":"/coder-templates-110","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"A dev stack that moved the search port needs its own template push: `searxng_url`\nis derived from `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` as read from `.env`, so a push made while\n`dev-stack.env` carries `18181` still names `8181` unless the key is set in `.env`\ntoo. A workspace dials the host gateway, where the compose network does not help,\nso the port in the rendered `SEARXNG_URL` has to be the one actually published."},"136":{"id":"/coder-templates-111","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`repo_url` prefills the Git repository field on both create forms. The Terraform\ndefault is still no clone (`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`).\n`.env.sample` sets `REPO_URL` to this stack's GitHub URL, so a normal push prefills\n`https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git` and **VS Code Web** opens\n`~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. A blank `REPO_URL=` line stays unpassed."},"137":{"id":"/coder-templates-112","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."},"138":{"id":"/coder-templates-113","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`coder_agent_url` exists so agents keep dialing the local plain listener when\n`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is a public name."},"139":{"id":"/coder-templates-114","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"`CODER_CONTAINER_SERVICE` (default `coder`), `CODER_DB_SERVICE` (default `db`),\n`CODER_CONTAINER_API_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`), `CODER_PUSH_USER`,\n`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, `DOCKER_BIN`, `PROBE_TIMEOUT` (20s) and `VERIFY_TIMEOUT`\n(60s) are the overrides."},"140":{"id":"/coder-templates-115","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"A missing `docker`, `timeout`, `tar` or `openssl`, a stack that is not running, or no\nactive Coder account is a *skip*: a one-line reason and exit 0. `bootstrap.sh` calls\nthis script, and a fresh clone has none of those prerequisites yet."},"141":{"id":"/coder-templates-116","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."},"142":{"id":"/coder-templates-117","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"A `DEV_IMAGE` that is not in the local image store is a note, not a stop: build it\nfirst with `bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`."},"143":{"id":"/coder-templates-118","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."},"144":{"id":"/coder-templates-119","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."},"145":{"id":"/coder-templates-120","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."},"146":{"id":"/coder-templates-121","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Set a token first. `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN` is any token from the Coder **Tokens** page or\nfrom `coder tokens create`."},"147":{"id":"/coder-templates-122","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"},"148":{"id":"/coder-templates-123","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."},"149":{"id":"/coder-templates-124","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"`coder templates` is the canonical name; `template` is its alias."},"150":{"id":"/coder-templates-125","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."},"151":{"id":"/coder-templates-126","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Autostop is metadata, not Terraform. Re-pin it with `templates edit` after a push made\nfrom the checkout root, or on the dev stack swap `-p evo-t1-coder-stack` for\n`-p evo-t1-dev --env-file dev-stack.env`."},"152":{"id":"/coder-templates-127","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Point the CLI at the in-container plain listener, with a token by name:"},"153":{"id":"/coder-templates-128","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Then re-pin both values:"},"154":{"id":"/coder-templates-129","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Check it in the Coder UI afterwards: **Default autostop** must read *Disabled* (0 h)."},"155":{"id":"/coder-templates-130","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"What a create form shows, in `workspace` order."},"156":{"id":"/coder-templates-131","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."},"157":{"id":"/coder-templates-132","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."},"158":{"id":"/coder-templates-133","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."},"159":{"id":"/coder-templates-134","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."},"160":{"id":"/coder-templates-135","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`).\n`private` gives that one workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume, so one\nworkspace can sit outside the shared profile while its siblings stay inside it.\nSwitching needs a rebuild."},"161":{"id":"/coder-templates-136","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."},"162":{"id":"/coder-templates-137","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is only observably per-workspace while the\nprofile mode is `private`: one shared profile is one file for every workspace that\nowner owns, and the last boot to re-render it wins."},"163":{"id":"/coder-templates-138","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"`devcontainer` asks two questions instead: **Git repository** and **Build repo's dev\ncontainer** (`use_devcontainer`, Yes/No, immutable), plus the same three Grok fields."},"164":{"id":"/coder-templates-139","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace."},"165":{"id":"/coder-templates-140","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable | Default             | Notes                                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `image`  | `evo-t1-dev:latest` | Seeds the **Workspace image** dropdown default. In `devcontainer` it decides nothing about the toolchain inside the editor. |","table":"table-1"},"166":{"id":"/coder-templates-141","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable      | Default                               | Notes                                    |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` | Reached through the Docker host gateway. |","table":"table-1"},"167":{"id":"/coder-templates-142","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable      | Default           | Notes                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `LITELLM_API_KEY` |","table":"table-1"},"168":{"id":"/coder-templates-143","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable             | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_default_model` | `agent` | `agent` is the tool-capable Spark lane (thinking `xhigh` by default, picker `low` / `medium` / `xhigh`); `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` bake the level into the alias. |","table":"table-1"},"169":{"id":"/coder-templates-144","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable        | Default | Notes                                                         |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `docker_socket` | `\"\"`    | Optional Docker socket URI; empty means the provider default. |","table":"table-1"},"170":{"id":"/coder-templates-145","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable          | Default                            | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `coder_agent_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:3002` | The URL the agent dials. Kept on the local plain listener so a public `CODER_ACCESS_URL` does not hairpin agent traffic through the reverse proxy. Empty keeps the provider-rendered access URL. |","table":"table-1"},"171":{"id":"/coder-templates-146","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable | Default | Notes                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dind`   | `false` | Turns on the per-workspace privileged daemon described below. Does not exist in `devcontainer`. |","table":"table-1"},"172":{"id":"/coder-templates-147","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable   | Default                                                                                                                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repo_url` | `\"\"` in `workspace`, `https://github.com/coder/coder` in `devcontainer` (Terraform); `.env.sample` `REPO_URL` overrides both to this stack's GitHub URL | Prefills the **Git repository** field. Both templates clone that URL on the first start; a later start leaves a folder that already holds a `.git` alone. `REPO_URL` in `.env` overrides the default. The sample value is this stack's own repo so **VS Code Web** opens `~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. |","table":"table-1"},"173":{"id":"/coder-templates-148","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable           | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `additional_repos` | `\"\"`    | The **Git repositories (additional)** textarea under the repository field. Extra URLs cloned into their own folder beside `repo_url`. |","table":"table-1"},"174":{"id":"/coder-templates-149","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable            | Default  | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_profile_mode` | `shared` | `shared` mounts the owner's shared `~/.grok` profile volume; `private` gives that workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume. Also the default behind the per-workspace **Grok profile** dropdown; switching a live workspace between the two modes needs a rebuild. |","table":"table-1"},"175":{"id":"/coder-templates-150","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable     | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the `github` MCP HTTP server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. Its `Authorization` header names `$GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, so no token value is ever part of a template variable. Set `GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |","table":"table-1"},"176":{"id":"/coder-templates-151","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable       | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `context7_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the cached `context7` MCP stdio server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. The key is optional, so it ships without one. Set `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |","table":"table-1"},"177":{"id":"/coder-templates-152","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable           | Default           | Notes                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `context7_api_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`. The rendered file names the variable. |","table":"table-1"},"178":{"id":"/coder-templates-153","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable            | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_config_extra` | `\"\"`    | The **Grok config extras** textarea: free-form TOML appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. |","table":"table-1"},"179":{"id":"/coder-templates-154","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"Notes on the two dropdowns that take a custom value:"},"180":{"id":"/coder-templates-155","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `coder`, `coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the\nvariable names another one."},"181":{"id":"/coder-templates-156","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."},"182":{"id":"/coder-templates-157","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"A `github_mcp` of `true` with no GitHub account connected gives that workspace a server\nthat fails its handshake once per session. A `context7_mcp` of `true` with an empty\n`context7_api_key` is the same shape: the block renders, and the server fails its\nhandshake once per session. Both are the pushed default now, so that shape is what a\nfresh deploy gets — `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and nothing in git carries a key.\nPark either one with `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` and re-push."},"183":{"id":"/coder-templates-158","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Autostart, autostop and TTLs are template *metadata*, so they stay out of the Terraform\n(where they would fight the UI) and travel on the CLI after pushing."},"184":{"id":"/coder-templates-159","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"},"185":{"id":"/coder-templates-160","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Coder reads the template's **Default autostop** (its default TTL) when it seeds a\nworkspace. That field also decides whether the create form's **Enable Autostop**\narrives ticked, and it ships at 24 h - which is how a workspace would otherwise start\nwith a shutdown deadline nobody chose."},"186":{"id":"/coder-templates-161","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"A 0 default TTL leaves the checkbox unchecked and leaves `coder create` with nothing to\ninherit, so a workspace runs until its owner stops it."},"187":{"id":"/coder-templates-162","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."},"188":{"id":"/coder-templates-163","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"`push-template.sh` re-sends both after every push, and `new-workspace.sh` names no\n`--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline either."},"189":{"id":"/coder-templates-164","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"A workspace owner can still tick the box for one workspace: `allow_user_autostop`\nstays on, because CE cannot turn it off -- `--allow-user-autostop=false` is marked\n\"can only be disabled in enterprise\"."},"190":{"id":"/coder-templates-165","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Keep the other licensed flags out of the CE stack too. `--failure-ttl` (Failure\ncleanup), `--dormancy-threshold` and `--dormancy-auto-deletion` are all marked \"This\nlicensed feature's default is 0h (off)\" in `templates edit --help` on v2.36.6, and\n`--allow-user-autostart` works the same way. Older notes also list `--inactivityTTL`\namong the rejected names; v2.36.6 has no flag of that name, and `--default-ttl` and\n`--activity-bump` are the closest."},"191":{"id":"/coder-templates-166","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Check it in the Coder UI: the template editor's **Default autostop** reads *Disabled*\n(0 h), and a workspace's **Schedule** tab shows **Enable Autostop** unchecked."},"192":{"id":"/coder-templates-167","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Opt-in in `workspace` (the **Docker-in-docker** Yes/No question, preset *AI workspace +\nDinD*, template variable `dind`). With the toggle off, `docker` in that workspace fails\nthe normal way rather than against a daemon that does not exist."},"193":{"id":"/coder-templates-168","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"With it on, per workspace:"},"194":{"id":"/coder-templates-169","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"One privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on a bridge network private to that\nworkspace (`coder-<wsid>-dind`, network alias `dind`)."},"195":{"id":"/coder-templates-170","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."},"196":{"id":"/coder-templates-171","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` is injected into the agent environment - absent when the\ntoggle is off."},"197":{"id":"/coder-templates-172","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`docker build`, `docker run` and `docker compose` then work inside the workspace. The\n*AI workspace + DinD* preset is the usual way to get this shape, since the client\nbinaries ship only in the golden image."},"198":{"id":"/coder-templates-173","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Details that matter:"},"199":{"id":"/coder-templates-174","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."},"200":{"id":"/coder-templates-175","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Only the literal string `\"true\"` enables it; anything else reads as off."},"201":{"id":"/coder-templates-176","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."},"202":{"id":"/coder-templates-177","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` is deliberately **empty**, so the daemon serves plain\n`tcp://0.0.0.0:2375` with no TLS and no auth. (`DOCKER_TLS_CERTSIZE=0` does not do\nthis: the daemon would listen on 2376 with TLS and the client fails against 2375.)"},"203":{"id":"/coder-templates-178","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Nothing is published to the host, and 2375 is reachable only from that workspace's own\nnetwork - which is also why the network is not `internal`. An internal network gets no\ngateway route or NAT, so `host.docker.internal` would resolve and never answer, and the\nagent would strand. See SECURITY.md."},"204":{"id":"/coder-templates-179","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"},"205":{"id":"/coder-templates-180","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"A missing image fails the workspace build at **plan** time, before anything is\ncreated, with `did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` - see\ntroubleshooting.md."},"206":{"id":"/coder-templates-181","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`cgroupns_mode = \"private\"` and `shm_size = 1024` (MB to the provider, so 1 GiB) are\nboth load-bearing; the sidecar is torn down with the workspace because it is gated on\n`start_count` as well as on the toggle."},"207":{"id":"/coder-templates-182","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and the `docker:29.8.1-dind` tag\nin `templates/workspace/main.tf` and `templates/devcontainer/main.tf` are the same\nengine line (29.8.1). Bump all three together."},"208":{"id":"/coder-templates-183","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-devcontainer-template","content":"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."},"209":{"id":"/coder-templates-184","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"One workspace container from the golden image: launcher and clone target only. It still\nhas to be the golden image, because that is what ships the Docker client the\ndevcontainer flow talks through. The editor's real environment is whatever the repo\nbuilds, so `image` decides nothing about the toolchain inside it."},"210":{"id":"/coder-templates-185","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"An always-on privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on the per-workspace bridge\nnetwork, with `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` injected **unconditionally** and\n`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` empty for the reasons above. `internal` stays false for the same\none reason."},"211":{"id":"/coder-templates-186","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"`dind` is not a variable here. A devcontainer workspace without a daemon is nothing,\nand the plain-clone fallback still wants a daemon its `docker` commands can reach."},"212":{"id":"/coder-templates-187","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"One host bind, `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>`, mounted at that **identical**\npath in both containers."},"213":{"id":"/coder-templates-188","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."},"214":{"id":"/coder-templates-189","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."},"215":{"id":"/coder-templates-190","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Named volumes cannot substitute: a volume is a daemon-local object and does not cross\nto the sidecar's daemon."},"216":{"id":"/coder-templates-191","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Docker creates the missing host directory itself (root-owned) and `startup.sh.tftpl`\nchowns it."},"217":{"id":"/coder-templates-192","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Inside a built dev container the same folder appears under `/workspaces/<repo-name>`,\nthe devcontainer CLI's default mount target and where the dev-container editor opens."},"218":{"id":"/coder-templates-193","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"`main.tf` therefore spells the editor's `folder` per hosting environment\n(`local.editor_folder`): `/workspaces/<repo-name>` inside the dev container,\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>` on the plain container, and\n`/home/coder` when no repository was named."},"219":{"id":"/coder-templates-194","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"One host-side path for all three would open the editor on a folder that exists nowhere\nthe editor runs, and VS Code answers that with\n`Workspace does not exist. Please select another workspace to open.`"},"220":{"id":"/coder-templates-195","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."},"221":{"id":"/coder-templates-196","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."},"222":{"id":"/coder-templates-197","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"A repository that ships no `devcontainer.json` gets no dev container and hence no\neditor with the toggle on. The toggle - or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url>`, which reads the repository and\npicks for you - is how that case is meant to be handled."},"223":{"id":"/coder-templates-198","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."},"224":{"id":"/coder-templates-199","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"With the toggle on, `coder_devcontainer.repo` then builds the repo's own\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` on the sidecar through the Coder agent\n(`@devcontainers/cli` is installed into the parent workspace container by a\n`run_on_start` module, and the golden image does not ship it)."},"225":{"id":"/coder-templates-200","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."},"226":{"id":"/coder-templates-201","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"With the toggle off - or on a repo that carries no `devcontainer.json`, where the build\nhas nothing to build - the editor attaches to the plain workspace agent instead, opened\non the same clone path."},"227":{"id":"/coder-templates-202","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"Either way `folder` is the clone, so code-server and its terminal profiles land on the\nrepository, and the **Web Terminal** button `cd`s there too."},"228":{"id":"/coder-templates-203","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"No `config_path` is set, so the CLI discovers the file the way VS Code does, and a repo\nusing the other legal locations behaves the same."},"229":{"id":"/coder-templates-204","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"`coder_script.dind_bootstrap` is an ordering guard, not a starter: it waits up to 120s\n(180s cap) for the sidecar to answer `docker version`, because the agent runs its\n`run_on_start` scripts before it creates the dev containers."},"230":{"id":"/coder-templates-205","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."},"231":{"id":"/coder-templates-206","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"It carries its own copies of `startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl` and\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so the tmux-default terminal and LiteLLM wiring above apply\nto it too."},"232":{"id":"/coder-templates-207","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."},"233":{"id":"/coder-templates-208","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."},"234":{"id":"/coder-templates-209","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."},"235":{"id":"/coder-templates-210","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Dry run (decision only, creates nothing):"},"236":{"id":"/coder-templates-211","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Create the workspace it picks:"},"237":{"id":"/coder-templates-212","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Force the `workspace` template:"},"238":{"id":"/coder-templates-213","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It shallow-clones (`--depth 1 --no-checkout --single-branch`) into a temporary\ndirectory, from the host, and lists the tracked files."},"239":{"id":"/coder-templates-214","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It looks for a dev container in `.devcontainer/`, `.github/devcontainers/`, or a\n`devcontainer.json`/`devcontainer.yml` anywhere in the tree."},"240":{"id":"/coder-templates-215","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"A tracked `.devcontainer/contributor-env` outranks every other signal: the probe is a\n**miss** even when the same clone also tracks `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`. That is\nhow this repo keeps its own Coder workspaces on `workspace` — the `.devcontainer/` it\nships is the contributor one for a laptop and CI, not a nested toolchain to rebuild\ninside the workspace's own DinD sidecar (see\ncontribute/devcontainer.md). Application repositories do\nnot carry the sentinel, so their dev containers stay a hit."},"241":{"id":"/coder-templates-216","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"Found means `devcontainer`; not found means `workspace`. A probe that fails **entirely**\nis also a `workspace` answer, with a warning."},"242":{"id":"/coder-templates-217","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."},"243":{"id":"/coder-templates-218","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It then assembles `coder create` with the template's parameters: `repo_url=<url>`\nalways, `use_devcontainer=false` whenever `devcontainer` was chosen but no dev\ncontainer was found (forced with `--devcontainer` onto a repo that has none, or a probe\nthat could not check the repo - the cases where leaving the toggle at its default would\nbuild nothing anyway), `dind=true` on `--dind`, and `additional_repos=<list>` whenever\n`--additional-repos` named at least one extra URL."},"244":{"id":"/coder-templates-219","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It names no `--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline."},"245":{"id":"/coder-templates-220","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It either runs the command (`--no-wait`; watch with `coder ls` / `coder logs <name>`)\nor, with no usable `coder` login, prints the same command to run by hand and exits 0."},"246":{"id":"/coder-templates-221","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."},"247":{"id":"/coder-templates-222","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"A workspace name can follow the URL. Coder names start with a letter (40 characters\nmax); the derived default is the repository leaf."},"248":{"id":"/coder-templates-223","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It never mints, reads, or stores a token - unlike `push-template.sh`, which mints its\nown because a template push has no other credential path. With `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN`\nset but `CODER_URL` unset it refuses to guess, and an empty token is a *not yet* state\nrather than an error."},"249":{"id":"/coder-templates-224","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"Each template creates one Docker container per workspace. Extra named volumes\n(`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` at `~/.shared/vscode`, and `grok-profile-<owner-id>`\nat `~/.grok` in shared mode) are chowned to the home owner at the start of\n`startup.sh.tftpl`, because Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root`\n755 and the agent runs as `coder`."},"250":{"id":"/coder-templates-225","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"Image from `images/dev/Dockerfile` (overridable with the `image` variable) carrying:\nNode 22 + 24, Python 3.12 + 3.11, Go 1.24, Bazelisk/buildifier,\nTerraform/tflint/terragrunt, protoc and its Go plugins, kubectl/helm/kubeconform,\nshfmt/shellcheck/bats/ruff/mypy/prettier, the Coder CLI, and the pinned Grok Build CLI\nat `/usr/local/bin/grok`."},"251":{"id":"/coder-templates-226","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."},"252":{"id":"/coder-templates-227","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."},"253":{"id":"/coder-templates-228","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"A persistent per-workspace home volume (`coder-<wsid>-home`)."},"254":{"id":"/coder-templates-229","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`host.docker.internal` mapped to `host-gateway`, so workspaces reach the Coder server\nand LiteLLM."},"255":{"id":"/coder-templates-230","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."},"256":{"id":"/coder-templates-231","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`connection_timeout = 300`."},"257":{"id":"/coder-templates-232","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Installs `tmux` if it is missing, then seeds `~/.tmux.conf` (only while that file is\nabsent or still carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker)."},"258":{"id":"/coder-templates-233","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Ensures the **Grok Build CLI** is on PATH. The golden image ships it, so the\n`https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` download runs only where `grok` is missing from PATH -\nthe `ubuntu:24.04` image option, or a home volume that never received the CLI."},"259":{"id":"/coder-templates-234","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Puts `~/.grok/bin` on PATH (login shells and that process)."},"260":{"id":"/coder-templates-235","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Writes the two terminal-profile tmux helpers (see below)."},"261":{"id":"/coder-templates-236","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Seeds the shared code-server User settings and the Coder Agents context files, only\nfor files that are absent."},"262":{"id":"/coder-templates-237","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Renders `~/.grok/config.toml` (the three gated MCP servers ship by default; the five\noffline stdio ones are not declared)."},"263":{"id":"/coder-templates-238","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind). The\nscript creates that folder first (`mkdir -p` plus a chown to the workspace user) so\nthe editor's wait loop can see it, then clones into it. This is idempotent, runs as\nthe workspace user so their git credentials are consulted, and refuses to touch an\nexisting non-empty non-git folder there. An empty pre-created target still clones."},"264":{"id":"/coder-templates-239","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."},"265":{"id":"/coder-templates-240","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"`display_apps` gives: the **VS Code Desktop** helper (a locally installed VS Code /\nCursor / Windsurf plus the `coder.coder-remote` extension), port forwarding, and the\nSSH helper."},"266":{"id":"/coder-templates-241","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A separate in-browser **code-server** editor is Coder's VS Code fork (no local VS\nCode), labelled **VS Code Web** with `order = 5` so it sorts before GitHub reauth (7),\nInstall GitHub App (8), LiteLLM (10) and Coder UI (11) and reads apart from the\n**VS Code Desktop** helper."},"267":{"id":"/coder-templates-242","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A custom **Web Terminal** command-app at `order = 6` takes the terminal slot. Custom\napps are ordered among themselves; static helper buttons have no `order` or `icon` knob\nin this Coder release and are not reorderable."},"268":{"id":"/coder-templates-243","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A **GitHub reauth** external app (`order = 7`, `/icon/github.svg`) opens Coder's\n`/external-auth/<id>` page in a slim window on the user's Coder session: connect, or\n**Reauthenticate** when the account is already linked. It stays on the main agent so\na failed clone (or a `devcontainer` subagent that never starts) still leaves the\nbutton on the workspace page. Device-flow login is not the App install: with the\ninjected App (id `github`) that page prints \"You must install the GitHub App to\nclone private repositories\" as unlinked plaintext, because Coder omits\n`AppInstallURL` on the default provider."},"269":{"id":"/coder-templates-244","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"An **Install GitHub App** external app (`order = 8`, same icon, opens in a tab)\nis the clickable install path for that default App:\n`https://github.com/apps/coder/installations/new`. It is declared only while\n`github_auth_id` is `github`. After installing, grant the private repos the\nworkspace clones, then restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints."},"270":{"id":"/coder-templates-245","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"Memory (90 percent) and disk (85 percent, `/home/coder`) usage gauges, six resource\nmetadata tiles sampled every 30s - four `coder stat` calls (`cpu --host`,\n`mem --host`, `disk --path /home/coder`, `cpu --host`) and two awk one-liners for swap\nand load average."},"271":{"id":"/coder-templates-246","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A **LiteLLM** tile (`/health/liveliness`, `share = \"owner\"`) that health-checks the\nproxy and is visible only to the workspace owner, plus a **Coder** external app."},"272":{"id":"/coder-templates-247","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"`coder_metadata.workspace_info` shows the image, the cloned repository (in `workspace`,\nor `none` when the Git repository field is empty), the LiteLLM URL, the default Grok\nmodel, whether DinD is `on (tcp://dind:2375)` or `off`, and - redacted - which key is\nwired in. A misconfigured workspace is therefore visible in the UI without shell\naccess. `devcontainer` adds a `devcontainer folder` tile and always reads\n`on (tcp://dind:2375)` for Docker-in-docker."},"273":{"id":"/coder-templates-248","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries an\ninlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`) because that\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. The script body\nlives in a sibling `code-server.sh.tftpl` and is rendered with `templatefile()`\n(workspace: `{ editor_folder = local.editor_folder }`; devcontainer: that plus\n`settings_b64`). Do not wrap that `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"274":{"id":"/coder-templates-249","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when it is missing** from\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the folder and, when\nthe folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for `<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings live on the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume seed, not\nin a per-start merge."},"275":{"id":"/coder-templates-250","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs fresh under `/tmp/code-server` on\nevery start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."},"276":{"id":"/coder-templates-251","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"Both twins' `coder_app` opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`. The\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL, in the Web Terminal's `command`, and as a positional\nargument to the code-server binary with `--ignore-last-opened` (path-based apps\ndrop the query string, and without that flag the last restored workspace wins)."},"277":{"id":"/coder-templates-252","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"`coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count`. `coder/preview` warns\n`Unexpanded count argument on block coder_app.*` when they are: that value is\nnot expanded at workspace-create preview. Scripts, metadata and the container\nstay gated."},"278":{"id":"/coder-templates-253","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."},"279":{"id":"/coder-templates-254","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."},"280":{"id":"/coder-templates-255","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`. Startup chowns\nthat volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume) to the home owner before\nthe write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`. A failed write logs a warning instead of running `jq`/`python`\nagainst a file that was never created."},"281":{"id":"/coder-templates-256","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"code-server's User settings file is a symlink to that path, so every workspace that\nowner has opens the same file, and an edit - in any workspace, or from the host by\nmounting the volume - is not overwritten on the next start. Delete the file to reseed\nfrom the template."},"282":{"id":"/coder-templates-257","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that symlink, so it is the one place\nthe seed is merged instead of linked."},"283":{"id":"/coder-templates-258","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."},"284":{"id":"/coder-templates-259","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."},"285":{"id":"/coder-templates-260","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."},"286":{"id":"/coder-templates-261","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`."},"287":{"id":"/coder-templates-262","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`vscode-terminal-tmux` re-joins or starts one shared session per folder, named\n`vscode-<folder>-<hash>`; with no `tmux` on PATH it falls back to a plain login shell."},"288":{"id":"/coder-templates-263","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`grok-build-terminal` keeps its own session, `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`, keyed off\nthe repository root found with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or the terminal's own\n`$PWD` outside a clone) and runs `grok --cwd <that root>` inside it."},"289":{"id":"/coder-templates-264","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."},"290":{"id":"/coder-templates-265","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"One repository per workspace is the default, and that is what `repo_url` and the\n**Git repository** field mean: one URL, cloned into one folder."},"291":{"id":"/coder-templates-266","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."},"292":{"id":"/coder-templates-267","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"Keep it that way unless a workspace genuinely needs two trees open at once."},"293":{"id":"/coder-templates-268","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed. It joins the list into one `additional_repos`\nparameter, so `\"a , b\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both arrive in the template as `a,b`."},"294":{"id":"/coder-templates-269","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"Without the flag the script passes exactly the parameters it used to pass, and the create\nform's **Git repositories (additional)** textarea stays empty."},"295":{"id":"/coder-templates-270","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."},"296":{"id":"/coder-templates-271","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."},"297":{"id":"/coder-templates-272","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"An extra folder never becomes the folder the tools open on: the editor, the Web\nTerminal button and the tmux helpers all stay on the primary clone."},"298":{"id":"/coder-templates-273","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"A private extra repository still has to be GRANTED to the connected GitHub account, or\nits clone is refused, just like the primary one."},"299":{"id":"/coder-templates-274","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."},"300":{"id":"/coder-templates-275","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"GitHub credentials arrive by one path only: opt-in, and HTTPS-only."},"301":{"id":"/coder-templates-276","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"An `ssh://` or `git@` URL reaches nothing here - this stack provisions no SSH keys."},"302":{"id":"/coder-templates-277","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."},"303":{"id":"/coder-templates-278","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"Fill every `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*` key or omit them all. A defined-but-empty key\ncrash-loops the server, which is why these keys live in `coder.env` and not in `.env`."},"304":{"id":"/coder-templates-279","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."},"305":{"id":"/coder-templates-280","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"Each workspace's agent then injects a `GIT_ASKPASS` helper that mints short-lived\nper-host tokens on demand, alongside a `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair in the agent\nenvironment."},"306":{"id":"/coder-templates-281","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"`gh_token_refresh` (a `coder_script`) re-mints that pair on start and every 6 hours;\nthe minted token expires in about an hour, so between refreshes the gh auth store\ncan hold an expired credential for hours mid-day (check reports that staleness as\nREJECTED). `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the store goes\nstale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script\nprints its one note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App**\nwhen the injected App is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing.\nAfter a reconnect, restart the workspace (or wait for the next `gh_token_refresh`)\nso `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new grant; `GIT_ASKPASS` remints live\nwithout that. The refresh script unsets `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before\n`gh auth login --with-token`, because gh refuses stdin while those names are set."},"307":{"id":"/coder-templates-282","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"A token that authenticates but was never GRANTED the repository still fails clone and\npush, and reads prove nothing about write: a private repository hidden from the\ncredential answers 403 or 404 on both lanes, and a public one answers a read probe\nwith 200 for any authenticated caller, so a read-only credential passes a read test\nwhile every write to that repo is denied.\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check <repo-url>` asks the write question\ndirectly: one GET of\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`, the endpoint `git push` itself\nnegotiates through."},"308":{"id":"/coder-templates-283","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"Nothing long-lived lives in any file, env or template: the rendered `config.toml`\nnames `GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, and the pair stops at the workspace container's edge\n(a dev container built on the sidecar is not handed either key)."},"309":{"id":"/coder-templates-284","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"One connection covers every workspace, and `gh auth status` inside a workspace is how\nyou check it landed."},"310":{"id":"/coder-templates-285","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"`~/.gitconfig` seeding reads that environment: an idempotent\n`credential.https://github.com.helper = !gh auth git-credential` entry (scoped to\n`github.com`) lets `git push`, `gh pr create` and `gh api` authenticate from the minted\ntoken rather than from a token stored in a file. It is skipped when your `gitconfig`\nalready names a helper, because git takes the **last** value it finds."},"311":{"id":"/coder-templates-286","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"The one copy on disk is the per-workspace gh auth store (0600) that `gh_token_refresh`\nmaintains."},"312":{"id":"/coder-templates-287","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#scriptsgithub-authsh-the-per-workspace-doctor","content":"`check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` names the credentials a workspace's\ngit and gh could present and answers \"can this credential WRITE to the repository you\npoint it at?\", by probing the receive-pack ref advertisement that endpoint `git push`\nnegotiates through (full usage:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- --help`). On the host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers with docker, or probes a named container\nalone. Inside a workspace it runs SELF mode with no docker: this environment's own\ncredential chain, one `self:` line per credential, and a named container there is\nrefused with a pointer to the host. `--source=chain` (the default) answers every\ncredential that could cover that repository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or\n`--source=mint` answers one slot and marks the answer shadowed while the env pair\nstands, because git and gh consult that pair first and never reach the store.\n`GH_TOKEN= GITHUB_TOKEN= <command>` is how you make them use the stored credential."},"313":{"id":"/coder-templates-288","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#scriptsgithub-authsh-the-per-workspace-doctor","content":"`auth <container> - < PAT` installs a write-capable PAT into that workspace's gh auth\nstore. That is legitimate in two cases: the owner connected no Coder account, so\nnothing can be minted, or the minted (or GitHub App) credential is revoked, expired,\nor simply was never granted the repository you are pointed at."},"314":{"id":"/coder-templates-289","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#scriptsgithub-authsh-the-per-workspace-doctor","content":"Installing one REPLACES the credential that workspace was presenting. While an\naccount *is* connected the store holds a minted pair and the next `gh_token_refresh`\nrun (start, then every 6 hours) rewrites it, so the PAT is a stand-in, not a second\npermanent credential: rotate or remove it in GitHub -> Settings -> Applications when\nthe work ends. `gh auth logout`, or a rebuild, clears the store either way."},"315":{"id":"/coder-templates-290","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#scriptsgithub-authsh-the-per-workspace-doctor","content":"A PAT is never typed as an argument any more. `auth <container> <PAT>` is refused\nbecause argv is readable through `ps` and lands in shell history, so the script takes\nthe token on stdin only: pipe it in (`echo \"$TOKEN\" | bazelisk run\n//scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> -`) or run `auth <container>` alone and\nanswer the hidden prompt."},"316":{"id":"/config-updates","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"page","content":"Keeping a checkout's config current","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates"},"317":{"id":"/config-updates-0","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"What goes stale after a `git pull`, what `update-configs` refreshes, and what it deliberately will not touch."},"318":{"id":"/config-updates-1","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"What goes stale"},"319":{"id":"/config-updates-2","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"The two passes"},"320":{"id":"/config-updates-3","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"Flags"},"321":{"id":"/config-updates-4","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"Where to run it"},"322":{"id":"/config-updates-5","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"What it will not do"},"323":{"id":"/config-updates-6","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"GitHub external auth and coder.env"},"324":{"id":"/config-updates-7","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"Grok profile mode"},"325":{"id":"/config-updates-8","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Public hosts and the dashboard"},"326":{"id":"/config-updates-9","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"Spark agent model group"},"327":{"id":"/config-updates-10","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Which files go stale and why `git pull` cannot fix them"},"328":{"id":"/config-updates-11","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"},"329":{"id":"/config-updates-12","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"},"330":{"id":"/config-updates-13","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Where the command may run, and where it refuses to"},"331":{"id":"/config-updates-14","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"},"332":{"id":"/config-updates-15","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Bringing an old checkout back to a runnable state with one command"},"333":{"id":"/config-updates-16","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"},"334":{"id":"/config-updates-17","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."},"335":{"id":"/config-updates-18","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."},"336":{"id":"/config-updates-19","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"Run it after you pull, not before: a pull is what changes the templates these\ntools copy from. `bazelisk run` feeds the real checkout (`BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`),\nnot the runfiles copy, so the env pass rewrites the live `.env` beside\n`docker-compose.yml`."},"337":{"id":"/config-updates-20","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two\nsteps: `${VAR}` inside `expose` expands during the shell phase, so compose\nnever sees those variables and a changed value needs a rebuild, and an unset\nvariable collapses to `127.0.0.1:57620:57620/tcp`, which fails to bind. So the\nhost ports and addresses live in a key=value env file - `.env` for the stable\nstack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and each has a checked-in twin beside\nit that carries the keys and explains them in the comment lines above. A pull\nthat renames, adds or retires a key silently changes what the stack runs on."},"338":{"id":"/config-updates-21","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.grok/config.toml`,\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder` and\n`~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots and\nare never revisited after that. A re-pushed template only reaches workspaces\nthat have been **start**ed or **restart**ed since the push - **stop** and\n**start** is what re-runs the provisioning script and therefore re-runs\n`before_config`."},"339":{"id":"/config-updates-22","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`env` reconciles one env file with its twin. It keeps every value you have set,\nfills a key the twin adds with the twin's default (or a reusable value already\nset in a sibling live file - `.env` filling `dev-stack.env`), and moves a key\nthe twin no longer declares into a commented trailer at the end of the file -\nparked, with its value and its explanation still readable, rather than deleted.\nComment lines always come from the twin, even when every value stays as you\nset it, so a pull that only rewrote an explanation still lands in `.env`. The\nrewrite lands in a staging file and replaces the live file only at the end,\nwith mode `600` kept. On a TTY the pass then walks every key that is new this\npull or still a placeholder (`change-me…`, `NOTSET`, `YOUR_LAN_IP`). An empty\nvalue whose example default is also empty is the intended value (LAN-only\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`, a parked MCP flag) and is not asked again. Enter keeps a\nsibling's value or the example default. A missing live file is created from\nthe twin only after a `Y/n` confirm. `--non-interactive` (and `--dry-run`)\nlist those keys and write no answers. A `summary:` line closes the pass."},"340":{"id":"/config-updates-23","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name.\nThe reseed set is the seven home-relative targets the templates seed -\n`~/.tmux.conf`, `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.coder/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`, the two `grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md` copies and\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` - and the same rule applies as in\n`refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it still carries its\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker (or, for the settings file, its\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key), so a config you hand-edit in a terminal is your\nfile and this tool will not move it. `--force` overrides that, per run, and says\nso."},"341":{"id":"/config-updates-24","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`all` runs `env` and then `workspace`."},"342":{"id":"/config-updates-25","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag        | Effect                        |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | print the plan, write nothing |","table":"table-0"},"343":{"id":"/config-updates-26","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                  | Effect                                                                       |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `-i`, `--interactive` | stop and ask about every key that still needs a value (the default on a TTY) |","table":"table-0"},"344":{"id":"/config-updates-27","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                | Effect                                 |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `--non-interactive` | never prompt; list what would be asked |","table":"table-0"},"345":{"id":"/config-updates-28","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag      | Effect                                             |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--force` | reseed workspace files that carry no marker either |","table":"table-0"},"346":{"id":"/config-updates-29","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                                   |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--from <dir>` | read the payloads from that checkout instead of this one |","table":"table-0"},"347":{"id":"/config-updates-30","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                          |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `--home <dir>` | treat that host directory as a workspace `HOME` |","table":"table-0"},"348":{"id":"/config-updates-31","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                 | Effect                        |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--container <name>` | address one container by name |","table":"table-0"},"349":{"id":"/config-updates-32","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"Every flag goes **after** the subcommand, so `update-configs --dry-run env` is a\nusage error and prints the usage rather than guessing."},"350":{"id":"/config-updates-33","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"On a TTY the env pass asks by default (`-i`/`--interactive` is then a no-op;\n`--non-interactive` turns it off). For every key that is new this pull or still\n`change-me…`/`NOTSET`/`YOUR_LAN_IP` it prints the explanation the twin carries\nabove that key and prompts for a value. Enter keeps a reusable value already\nset in a sibling env file, or the example default. A key whose\nname carries `KEY`, `SECRET`, `TOKEN` or `PASSWORD` is echoed nowhere - the\nreport says `set` or `empty` and the prompt does not echo what you type - so a\nscreenful of this output is safe to paste into an issue. With no terminal to\nprompt through the pass warns and leaves those keys alone, which is why\n`--dry-run` / `--non-interactive` is the shape that belongs in a script or CI."},"351":{"id":"/config-updates-34","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\". The pass now prints which\ndaemon it addressed and tells you to use a host shell, or to name the target:"},"352":{"id":"/config-updates-35","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."},"353":{"id":"/config-updates-36","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It never prints an env value that looks like a credential."},"354":{"id":"/config-updates-37","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It never drops a key your env file still sets; retired keys are commented out\nin place."},"355":{"id":"/config-updates-38","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It does not touch `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is rendered *inside* the\nworkspace, and `~/.grok` is one profile volume per owner, so a copy from the\nhost would move the model list, the session-affinity key and the editor keys\nof every workspace sharing the profile. A **start/restart** re-renders it from\nthis checkout, which is the only way a per-workspace value gets in, and the\npass says so after it reseeds."},"356":{"id":"/config-updates-39","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is the one rendered target it does reseed, and\nit re-*renders* rather than copies: `${litellm_url}` and the `$${...}`\ndollar-brace unfoldings are rewritten, and that workspace's own `cline.apiKey`\nand `roo.openaiApiKey` are carried forward."},"357":{"id":"/config-updates-40","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"Inside a running container the same seven targets are `refresh-configs`'s job\ntoo: same reseed set, same marker rule, `--json` for a machine-readable\nanswer, and `--litellm-url <url>` for a stack environment that publishes\nLiteLLM on a port other than 4000 (the reseeded `settings.json` then still\nnames that stack's own model transport). grok-build-and-agents.md covers the profile\nvolume and what seeds on first boot."},"358":{"id":"/config-updates-41","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."},"359":{"id":"/config-updates-42","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop. Do not set\nonly `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_APP_INSTALL_URL` either: it suppresses the injected\nApp like any other key in the set. A custom GitHub App belongs in `coder.env`\nfully filled, including\n`APP_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new`."},"360":{"id":"/config-updates-43","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."},"361":{"id":"/config-updates-44","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."},"362":{"id":"/config-updates-45","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX` is only for telling providers or hosts apart;\nomitted, it matches every `github.com` remote."},"363":{"id":"/config-updates-46","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`push-template.sh` reads `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` from `coder.env` and\nforwards it as `--var github_auth_id=` so the templates declare the same id.\nChanging the id orphans existing GitHub account links."},"364":{"id":"/config-updates-47","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."},"365":{"id":"/config-updates-48","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."},"366":{"id":"/config-updates-49","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints.\nThe grant must include the repos you clone, or a connected credential authenticates\nand is still 403."},"367":{"id":"/config-updates-50","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and the template's `gh_token_refresh` coder_script\nre-mints that pair on start and every 6 hours (`0 0 */6 * * *`) because a minted\ntoken expires first. Check it with `gh auth status` inside the workspace, or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check` for one line per credential slot.\ncoder-templates.md owns\nthe template-side detail."},"368":{"id":"/config-updates-51","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment. That\npath is gone: there is no such template variable and no such key in the twin. A\nworkspace's GitHub credential comes from the External Auth provider alone, so an\nowner who connected nothing has no GitHub credential anywhere - unless they install\none per workspace:"},"369":{"id":"/config-updates-52","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."},"370":{"id":"/config-updates-53","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`GROK_PROFILE_MODE` in the twin is the default for the template variable\n`grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also shows per workspace as the **Grok\nprofile** dropdown. Read once per push, so changing the default here needs\n`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. Overriding it for one workspace happens on\nthat dropdown and needs a rebuild, like `dind`."},"371":{"id":"/config-updates-54","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts that user's shared\ngrok-profile volume nested inside `/home/coder`, so `config.toml`, the MCP warm\ncaches, `memory-v2`, skills and a self-installed CLI are one profile shared by\nthat account's workspaces. It is re-rendered on every start and the\n**last-started workspace wins** for all of them."},"372":{"id":"/config-updates-55","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`private`: no nested mount. Each workspace gets a private `~/.grok` on its own\nhome volume, so its `config.toml` - plus memory, skills and logs - differs per\nworkspace and dies with the workspace."},"373":{"id":"/config-updates-56","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"A workspace moving `shared` -> `private` starts on an empty profile: nothing is\ncopied and the shared volume is left alone."},"374":{"id":"/config-updates-57","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the shared\nprofile holds and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."},"375":{"id":"/config-updates-58","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch. Empty keeps everything below LAN-only and\nevery derived `STACK_*_URL` stays empty; otherwise set the comma-separated public\nDNS names in the hyphenated one-label form the rules below define; `.env.sample`\nshows a worked example."},"376":{"id":"/config-updates-59","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Entries are DNS names, not URLs: no scheme, no port, no path. The entry with no\nprefix is the dashboard."},"377":{"id":"/config-updates-60","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Each service name is **one label** under the zone and carries its prefix with a\n**HYPHEN**: a wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted\ntwo-label form has no certificate at the public edge."},"378":{"id":"/config-updates-61","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` from the prefix on\neach entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` / `hermes-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. Compose sets\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the Chat tab's Host and\nOrigin)."},"379":{"id":"/config-updates-62","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."},"380":{"id":"/config-updates-63","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`proxy/router.conf` picks the service from `$http_host` and listens on\n`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back as `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the four interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."},"381":{"id":"/config-updates-64","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Set the names in `.env`, and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside\nthe stable one, then re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and recreate the\nstack you switched. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is part of that list:\nbootstrap writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`, and the LAN `:9119` listener stays either\nway. Leave the hermes name out and the Homepage card keeps the LAN URL.\npublic-access.md owns the naming rule, the measured\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` on the dotted form, the request path and the\nchecks to run on the Traefik box."},"382":{"id":"/config-updates-65","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"},"383":{"id":"/config-updates-66","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` are the Ollama endpoints behind the `spark-<n>` aliases. The\nplaceholder hostnames are on purpose: replace them with real Ollama URLs or\nleave them as-is, and those aliases simply fail when used."},"384":{"id":"/config-updates-67","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` are the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoints. A workspace pins\nits session to one healthy box; fresh session-id-less traffic fans out by\n`least-busy`, shortest in-flight queue first."},"385":{"id":"/config-updates-68","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box. Empty is not a working value - LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at\nclient construction (`AuthenticationError`, before reaching the box) and `agent`\nfalls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark` is not a secret and is\nignored by a keyless backend."},"386":{"id":"/config-updates-69","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box."},"387":{"id":"/config-updates-70","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`AGENT_MODEL_URL` is the single `/v1/models` URL the \"Agent backend\" card uses\nfor both its browser link and its status probe. All three boxes serve the same\nmodel id, so point it at any reachable one; the per-box health keys are what tell\nyou which box answered."},"388":{"id":"/config-updates-71","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"Leaving all three `SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` keys empty keeps `agent` defined but\nunusable: LiteLLM then falls back to `coder`, the Arc alias. That fallback is what\nthe \"Agent backend\" card exists to catch - Grok Build, Cline and Roo need\nstructured tool calls, which the Arc IPEX-LLM Ollama returns as plain text for the\nQwen2.5-Coder weights. litellm-routing.md owns the alias\ntable, the picker, the pin, and the measured tool-call limitation (including which\naliases do emit real calls)."},"389":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"page","content":"Building with Bazel","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel"},"390":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-0","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Bazelisk as the ingress for tests, lint and the operator CLIs, and what the runfiles layout means for a script you just added."},"391":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-1","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"The contributor path"},"392":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-2","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"Stack-host bootstrap"},"393":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-3","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"Daily commands"},"394":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-4","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"What Bazel brings"},"395":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-5","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"Script targets and runfiles"},"396":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-6","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"},"397":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-7","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"},"398":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-8","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"},"399":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-9","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"What Bazel brings over running the scripts directly"},"400":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-10","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Script targets, runfiles, and why a new `scripts/*.sh` is a target automatically"},"401":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-11","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Running any operator CLI from a foreign working directory with pinned inputs"},"402":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-12","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Adding a script without editing a BUILD file"},"403":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-13","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Getting the same Bazel, Bats, shellcheck and Terraform that CI uses without installing\nany of them"},"404":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-14","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Bazelisk is how this repo runs tests, lint, and the operator CLIs. The Makefile targets call\nthe same commands. test-with-bazel.md covers the Bats harness;\n../branching.md the branch rules that CI guards;\ndevcontainer.md the container that carries the toolchain."},"405":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-15","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"},"406":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-16","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Open the checkout in the contributor dev container (`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nbuilds it) and run the commands below unchanged."},"407":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-17","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Or, from a machine that is not in that container, `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`\ndocker-runs `evo-t1-dev:latest` and runs the gate inside it."},"408":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-18","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Both give you the GNU userland the gate runs on, which a macOS laptop cannot promise\nnatively. Neither replaces the bootstrap below — that one is for the stack host."},"409":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-19","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"},"410":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-20","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point. On Linux it also installs public\nDNS so `github.com` still resolves when Tailscale MagicDNS is the only\nnameserver (`Could not resolve host: github.com` after a compose `up`). `--check`\nreports that lookup and writes nothing."},"411":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-21","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target, including every `scripts/*.sh`. CI repeats the gate from\na clean runner inside the same golden image: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds\n`images/dev` with buildx and the Actions layer cache and docker-runs the two suites below in\nit, so the bazel job does not install the pins onto the runner at all — the image already\ncarries them. The persistent cache on that path is the buildx GHA layer cache, so a pull\nrequest that leaves `images/dev/**` alone replays the image layers."},"412":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-22","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target         | What it runs                                                                             |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:test-fast` | One `sh_test` per `tests/bats/*.bats` file, plus the shell inventory and docstring gates |","table":"table-0"},"413":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-23","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target    | What it runs                                       |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:lint` | ShellCheck, shfmt, buildifier (manual: host tools) |","table":"table-0"},"414":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-24","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target        | What it runs                                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:validate` | test-fast, lint, `docker compose config -q`, `.env.sample` vs `.env.example`, terraform fmt when terraform is installed |","table":"table-0"},"415":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-25","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target   | What it runs                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `//:fix` | buildifier and shfmt write mode |","table":"table-0"},"416":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-26","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target             | What it runs                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:<stem>` | One operator CLI: `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`, e.g. `//scripts:dev-stack -- up`, `//scripts:new-workspace -- --dry-run <url>` |","table":"table-0"},"417":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-27","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target                    | What it runs                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:test-in-image` | The merge gate inside `evo-t1-dev:latest`, for a machine that is not the contributor container |","table":"table-0"},"418":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-28","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target                   | What it runs                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:seed-dev-env` | Materialize a contributor home (Grok config, editor settings, notes, tmux, helpers) from the workspace templates |","table":"table-0"},"419":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-29","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"`make test`/`bats`/`lint`/`fix`/`validate` and the `stack-*`/`prod-stack-*` shims are the\nsame commands;\n`make help` lists them. `//:test-fast` does not start Compose, pull models, or SSH to a Spark\nbox: the suites exercise those scripts against `tests/bats/tool_stubs`. `bazelisk run\n//scripts:<stem> -- <args>` is how a human runs one of them for real."},"420":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-30","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."},"421":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-31","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script probes for its repo root instead of trusting the cwd."},"422":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-32","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."},"423":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-33","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs. CI docker-runs the\ngate with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)`, so those Bazel caches are per-job and Bazel\ncan name a uid the image passwd file does not list; the cache that survives there is\nthe image's buildx GHA cache. No remote cache is configured — the same two flags\nwould take a `--remote_cache=<uri>` endpoint later."},"424":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-34","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"`scripts/BUILD.bazel` declares one `exec_shell` launcher plus one `sh_binary` wrapper per\n`scripts/*.sh`, generated from the directory, so a new CLI is a Bazel target without a second\nedit there. `//:validate` is an alias for `//scripts:validate`; `//:fix` keeps the same\nlauncher pattern at the repo root."},"425":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-35","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"`rules_shell` only symlinks an `sh_test` or `sh_binary` source when that file is executable.\n`//tools:exec_shell.bzl` writes a generated launcher and runs the real `.sh` from runfiles\nwith bash, so a checkout whose scripts are mode `100644` still builds."},"426":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-36","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"Each CLI target also declares the repo files that script reads by path — `//:stack_data`\n(`docker-compose.yml`, both `templates/**` payload trees, `images/dev/**`,\n`.devcontainer/**`, `litellm/**`, `searxng/**`, `homepage/**`, `proxy/**`, the two `.env`\nsamples, `LICENSE`) and\n`//scripts:doc_sources` (every `scripts/**.sh`, so a sourced `scripts/lib/*.sh` resolves\ntoo). `proxy/certs/` is `.bazelignored` (bootstrap issues those on the host), so a\n`bazelisk run` of a stack runner or of `push-template` prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` — the real checkout —\nover the runfiles copy as the compose project directory, the live `.env`, and the template tree (runfiles\n`templates/**` are symlinks that Coder cannot follow after unpack). Without the committed payloads a\nsandboxed run would read a partial copy of the repo and fail for the wrong reason. A new\ntop-level payload directory has to be added to that `glob` by hand: `test-fast` and `lint`\nonly see repo files that travel in runfiles, so an unlisted directory is invisible to the\nharness rather than checked loosely."},"427":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"page","content":"Contributor environment","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer"},"428":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-0","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it is seeded, and which path is the merge gate."},"429":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-1","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"The one image, the one pin file"},"430":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-2","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"Open it on a laptop"},"431":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-3","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"Seeding the contributor home"},"432":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-4","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"GitHub auth in the container"},"433":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-5","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"Running the gate here"},"434":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-6","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"What CI does"},"435":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-7","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"Why this repo's own workspaces stay on `workspace`"},"436":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-8","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"What this is not"},"437":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-9","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"},"438":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-10","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"Opening the repo as a contributor container in VS Code or a compatible editor"},"439":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-11","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"What `seed-dev-env` materializes into that home, and what it refuses to touch"},"440":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-12","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"GitHub auth inside the container, and why `github-auth check` runs SELF there"},"441":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-13","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"},"442":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-14","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"What CI does, and why this repo's own Coder workspaces stay on `workspace`"},"443":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-15","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"Reproducing CI's failure on your own machine with the same image CI runs"},"444":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-16","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"Running the Bazel gate on macOS without maintaining a second, BSD-flavoured toolchain"},"445":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-17","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"},"446":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-18","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"One image — `images/dev/Dockerfile`, built as `evo-t1-dev:latest` — is the contributor and\ntest runtime on macOS, on Linux, and on GitHub. The same Dockerfile serves the Coder\nworkspaces the stack provisions, the contributor dev container on a laptop, and the\ncontainer CI docker-runs its checks inside. Every version in it comes from\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`; ../coder-templates.md covers the\nworkspace side, and CONTRIBUTING.md\nis the checklist the PR follows."},"447":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-19","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"`images/dev/tool-versions.env` is the single source of truth for every tool version:\nBazelisk, buildifier, shfmt, shellcheck (apt), bats, Terraform/tflint/terragrunt, the\nCoder CLI, the Docker *client* trio, Node, Python, Go, protoc and the pinned Grok CLI."},"448":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-20","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"Each `KEY` there is also an `ARG` in `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and the `ARG` default\nmirrors the pin, so a bare `docker build` matches a scripted one."},"449":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-21","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."},"450":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-22","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."},"451":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-23","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"Build it locally when you want the tag in the local store (the Coder docker provider\nresolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from there and never pulls):"},"452":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-24","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."},"453":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-25","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"},"454":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-26","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                                  | Value                          | Why                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `build.context` / `build.dockerfile` | `../images/dev` / `Dockerfile` | The same Dockerfile the workspace and CI use; `ARG` defaults are the pins, so no build args are needed. |","table":"table-0"},"455":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-27","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key          | Value   | Why |\n| ------------ | ------- | --- |\n| `remoteUser` | `coder` |     |","table":"table-0"},"456":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-28","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                          | Value                   | Why                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `containerEnv` / `remoteEnv` | `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` | The marker that says *contributor container*, not Coder workspace. Also carries `SEARXNG_URL`. |","table":"table-0"},"457":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-29","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key      | Value                  | Why                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mounts` | `/var/run/docker.sock` | The container talks to the workstation's own daemon, which is how a `docker run` inside it is a real one. |","table":"table-0"},"458":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-30","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key       | Value                                          | Why                                           |\n| --------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `runArgs` | `--add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway` | `:4000` and SearXNG on `:8181` on the laptop. |","table":"table-0"},"459":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-31","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                 | Value                                         | Why                                                                     |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `postCreateCommand` | `bash scripts/seed-dev-env.sh --devcontainer` | Seeds the contributor home the way a workspace start seeds a workspace. |","table":"table-0"},"460":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-32","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                                | Value | Why                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `customizations.vscode.extensions` |       | shellcheck and shfmt are the two `//:lint` runners, wired into the editor beside the Terraform, Docker and YAML helpers. |","table":"table-0"},"461":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-33","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"`updateRemoteUserUID` is on, so the container's `coder` uid matches the host user and the\nbind-mounted checkout stays writable without a `chmod` dance."},"462":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-34","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker:"},"463":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-35","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                                                                            |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--home <dir>` | Home tree to shape (default `$HOME`; created when absent; an existing non-directory is an error). |","table":"table-1"},"464":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-36","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag                  | Effect                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--litellm-url <url>` | Model-transport address rendered into `settings.json` and `~/.grok/config.toml` (default `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`). |","table":"table-1"},"465":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-37","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag             | Effect                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--devcontainer` | After a successful write, run `gh auth setup-git` when `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` is set and `gh` is on PATH. Best effort, and the token is never an argument. |","table":"table-1"},"466":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-38","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag      | Effect                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `--force` | Re-materialize files a user owns. |","table":"table-1"},"467":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-39","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag        | Effect                                                            |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | Print the plan; write nothing, create no directory, call no `gh`. |","table":"table-1"},"468":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-40","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"What it writes: `~/.tmux.conf`, the shared `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` (with the\ncode-server and desktop VS Code User settings symlinked to it), `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`, the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, the two terminal helpers in\n`~/.grok/bin`, `~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` plus this clone's `notes.md`, and the\n`~/.grok/bin` line in `~/.bashrc`."},"469":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-41","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"},"470":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-42","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"A file carrying the stack marker — `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`, or the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key — is stack-owned and is reseeded when the seed in this repo\ndrifted."},"471":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-43","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"A marker-free file is user-owned and stays untouched unless `--force` says otherwise.\nThe terminal helpers and `notes-setup.sh` are the exception: startup rewrites those on\nevery boot, so this script does too."},"472":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-44","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"It never writes a secret, never writes a GitHub token or a PAT, and never installs the\nGrok CLI — the golden image already carries `/usr/local/bin/grok`. Exit `5` means no\nproven checkout was found (it wants `docker-compose.yml` where it is looking), which is the\nsame repo-root probe every other script in the repo makes."},"473":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-45","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"},"474":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-46","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"SELF mode applies when `CODER_WORKSPACE_ID` is set **or** `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` is. The\ncontainer sets the latter; a Coder workspace sets the former."},"475":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-47","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"SELF means no docker at all and no `DOCKER_HOST`: one probe of this environment's own\ncredential chain, printed as one `self:` line per credential. `docker` in here addresses\nthe workstation daemon mounted in, never the stack host's workspace fleet."},"476":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-48","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"Pass only the repository URL. A named container argument is refused with a pointer to the\nhost shell, because that container lives on a daemon this environment cannot enumerate."},"477":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-49","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"Credentials still arrive by the one supported path: Coder External Auth mints\n`GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN`, and `--devcontainer` hands them to git through\n`gh auth setup-git`. There is no static token and no PAT in this repo's tooling."},"478":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-50","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"},"479":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-51","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"From a machine that is **not** inside the container, ask the image to run them instead of\ninstalling the toolchain onto that machine:"},"480":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-52","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."},"481":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-53","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"Native `bazelisk test` on macOS is a convenience, not a merge path. The suites run there,\nbut the BSD userland differs from the GNU one the image ships (`sed`, `date`, `find`,\n`sort`), so a green Darwin run is not the run that gates the merge and a red one is not\nproof of a defect. The container, or `test-in-image`, is the answer\ntest-with-bazel.md and ../branching.md gate on."},"482":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-54","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit: `//:test-fast`, `//:lint`, `//:validate`, and the `docs-site/` Node checks."},"483":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-55","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"No Actions `container:` key is involved. The job runs on a plain runner and reaches the\ntoolchain through `docker run --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\"` with `HOME=/tmp` and\n`USER=$(id -un)`, so what gates the merge is an image tag, the bind-mounted checkout\nstays writable, permission tests see a non-root identity, and Bazel can name a uid the\nimage passwd file does not list."},"484":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-56","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."},"485":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-57","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"A pull request that does not touch `images/dev/**` replays the cached layers instead of\nrebuilding them, which is what keeps the gate's wall time flat."},"486":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-58","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."},"487":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-59","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file. It is tracked in this\nrepository, and `probe_repo` (in `scripts/lib/workspace.sh`) treats a clone that tracks it\nas a **miss** — exit `3` — even when the same clone also tracks\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`."},"488":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-60","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."},"489":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-61","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."},"490":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-62","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"Do not copy the sentinel into another repository. An application repo's\n`.devcontainer/` is exactly what `new-workspace` is supposed to find, and the sentinel in\nfront of it silently downgrades that workspace to the plain template."},"491":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-63","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."},"492":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-64","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."},"493":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-65","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."},"494":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-66","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."},"495":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-67","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"When the container itself is the suspect, `bazelisk run //:validate` from inside it is the\nfastest answer, and ../troubleshooting.md covers the stack-side\nfailures it rules in or out."},"496":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"page","content":"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel"},"497":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-0","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Hermetic Bats: how a suite is discovered, what the stub PATH gives you, and the two inventory gates a new function has to satisfy."},"498":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-1","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"Layout"},"499":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-2","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"Two gates, one rule"},"500":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-3","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#running-a-suite","content":"Running a suite"},"501":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-4","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"Which runtime gates"},"502":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-5","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-two-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"The two paths a suite may use"},"503":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-6","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"The stub-PATH recipe"},"504":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-7","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"Sandbox-safe: probe for the repo root, then say so"},"505":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-8","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"},"506":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-9","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"},"507":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-10","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Running one suite, from any directory"},"508":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-11","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Which runtime actually gates a merge, and which one is a convenience"},"509":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-12","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"},"510":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-13","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Sandbox-safe repo-root probing"},"511":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-14","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Adding a test that passes in the Bazel sandbox and on a laptop"},"512":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-15","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Making a new function satisfy `//tests:shell_inventory` without guessing"},"513":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-16","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Reproducing a CI failure locally by running the suites in the image CI runs them in"},"514":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-17","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Every suite under `tests/` is hermetic Bats: it runs against stubs and a throwaway tree,\nnever against a daemon, a network, or the operator's real `.env`."},"515":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-18","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."},"516":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-19","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."},"517":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-20","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."},"518":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-21","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."},"519":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-22","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."},"520":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-23","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"},"521":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-24","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"one asserting test in the **one** suite for its module — a `scripts/lib/<mod>.sh` helper\nbelongs to `tests/bats/<mod>.bats`, and a helper that only an entry CLI defines belongs to\n`tests/bats/entry-fns.bats`, which loads single functions with `load_shell_function`;"},"522":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-25","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."},"523":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-26","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"Name it in exactly one suite. A chain test that happens to mention the name also satisfies\nthe inventory, but the owning suite is the one that asserts its behaviour."},"524":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-27","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"},"525":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-28","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#running-a-suite","content":"A suite is one cached action. Its `.bats` file, `test_helper.bash`, `test_helpers.sh`,\n`bats_runner.sh` and the `scripts/**.sh` plus stub/fixture files it declares as `data` are\nits inputs, so editing one script re-runs only the suites that read it, and an unchanged\nsuite replays its cached PASS."},"526":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-29","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"},"527":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-30","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"Inside the contributor dev container (devcontainer.md) the commands\nabove are exactly what CI runs."},"528":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-31","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"On a host that is not that container, `//scripts:test-in-image` docker-runs\n`evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid, with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER` set so\nBazel can name that uid, and runs them there."},"529":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-32","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"A native `bazelisk test` on macOS is convenience only: the BSD userland differs from the\nGNU one in the image (`sed`, `date`, `find`, `sort`), so a red run there is not proof of a\ndefect and a green one is not the run that unlocks the merge button. CI and branch\nprotection gate on the image result either way."},"530":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-33","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-two-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"`REPO_ROOT` — where the checkout is *for this run*. `tests/bats_runner.sh` exports it;\noutside Bazel it is whatever `bats_canonical_repo_root` resolves. Derive it, never\nhardcode a machine path, and treat \"cannot resolve\" as a `skip`, not a failure."},"531":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-34","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-two-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."},"532":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-35","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"Copy this shape; it is the convention every suite already uses (`use_stubs` prints nothing\nand shadows PATH for the current test only, and fails when a stub is missing):"},"533":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-36","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."},"534":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-37","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."},"535":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-38","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."},"536":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-39","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."},"537":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-40","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."},"538":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-41","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."},"539":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-42","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`real_bin <tool>` resolves a tool with the stub directory removed from PATH. That is the\nintegration-tier gate: `if [ -z \"$(real_bin terraform)\" ]; then skip \"terraform not\ninstalled\"; fi` — a test that could talk to a real daemon skips when the tool is absent\ninstead of failing."},"540":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-43","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."},"541":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-44","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"Assertions come from `tests/test_helpers.sh`: `fail`, `assert_eq`, `assert_match`,\n`assert_not_match`, `assert_file_exists`, `assert_file_not_exists`, `load_shell_function`.\n`load_shell_function` is the one harness place that expands a function body (it is how a\nsingle entry-CLI function gets unit-tested); production scripts still never use `eval`."},"542":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-45","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"A script must work when the cwd is **not** the checkout. Under `bazel test` the working\ndirectory is an exec root or a runfiles tree that holds *copies* of whatever the target\ndeclares as `data`; under `bazel run` it is wherever the operator typed `bazelisk`, and\n`bazel run` alone sets `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`. Neither guarantees a working Docker\ndaemon, an `ssh`, or a reachable Spark box — a sandboxed action usually has none of them on\nPATH."},"543":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-46","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails:"},"544":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-47","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."},"545":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-48","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"The file probe (`-f docker-compose.yml`) says \"this is a repo root\"; the `command -v` probe\nof a tool that cannot exist inside the sandbox says \"and it is the real checkout, not a\ncopy\". Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case."},"546":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-49","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"Exit `5` with one `unresolvable repo root` line beats guessing a cwd and half-running a\nfleet check against a copy of the repo."},"547":{"id":"/dashboard","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"page","content":"Dashboard and desktops","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard"},"548":{"id":"/dashboard-0","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."},"549":{"id":"/dashboard-1","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#homepage-dashboard","content":"Homepage dashboard"},"550":{"id":"/dashboard-2","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"What it shows"},"551":{"id":"/dashboard-3","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#login","content":"Login"},"552":{"id":"/dashboard-4","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"Config files"},"553":{"id":"/dashboard-5","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"After a recreate: revalidate once"},"554":{"id":"/dashboard-6","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"Host stats, and the docker socket"},"555":{"id":"/dashboard-7","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#look","content":"Look"},"556":{"id":"/dashboard-8","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Kasm Workspaces"},"557":{"id":"/dashboard-9","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"What each dashboard card shows and which API it reads"},"558":{"id":"/dashboard-10","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput"},"559":{"id":"/dashboard-11","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"},"560":{"id":"/dashboard-12","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"Kasm's wizard-and-UI port pair, and what resetting it destroys"},"561":{"id":"/dashboard-13","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"Reading the box's health from one page without a shell"},"562":{"id":"/dashboard-14","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"Fixing a card that reports down when the service itself is up"},"563":{"id":"/dashboard-15","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."},"564":{"id":"/dashboard-16","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"A card per service, with status pulled from each service's own API over the compose\nnetwork - `http://coder:3000`, `http://litellm:4000`, `http://ollama:11434`,\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status` - so nothing here needs a daemon credential. The\nheader carries boxed tiles for host CPU, memory, CPU temperature, disk and `eth0`\nthroughput, beside the clock, local weather (open-meteo, keyless) and a DuckDuckGo\nsearch."},"565":{"id":"/dashboard-17","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"Card `href`s come from `STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` /\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`, which default to LAN URLs built from `STACK_LAN_HOST` and the\nport variables. Change a port or re-detect the LAN IP and the links follow; set\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` and the cards follow the public names. The Hermes card\nfollows `STACK_HERMES_URL` when bootstrap sets it, and stays on\n`http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` when that key is empty. See hermes.md."},"566":{"id":"/dashboard-18","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"`https://<host>/` serves the dashboard behind a self-signed certificate that\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` issues, so expect a one-time browser warning. Port 80 redirects\nto HTTPS."},"567":{"id":"/dashboard-19","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."},"568":{"id":"/dashboard-20","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"Either form rewrites `.env`, recreates the container and verifies the new value landed.\nThe script's job is keeping the secret out of shell history, `ps` and git, and leaving\n`.env` at mode 600."},"569":{"id":"/dashboard-21","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"`homepage/config/*.yaml` plus `custom.css` are bind-mounted read-only, so `git diff`\nstays the record of dashboard changes, and the directory itself has to stay writable\nbecause the app creates `logs/` inside it. A new config file (`kubernetes.yaml`, ...)\nneeds its own mount line in `docker-compose.yml` before the app sees it."},"570":{"id":"/dashboard-22","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"`custom.css` lives in `/app/config` and is served by the allow-listed\n`/api/config/custom.css` route. It styles the sign-in page too, so keep internal\nhostnames out of it."},"571":{"id":"/dashboard-23","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"},"572":{"id":"/dashboard-24","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"A plain `restart` keeps the generated page, so day-to-day YAML edits need nothing.\nNew asset files do need a recreate (`up -d`), since `restart` keeps the old mounts."},"573":{"id":"/dashboard-25","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"CPU, memory, CPU temperature and disk are host-wide through `/proc`. A network widget\nonly sees the container's own netns (`lo`/`eth0`) - `/sys/class/net` is namespaced, so\nthe host Wi-Fi NIC stays invisible even with `/sys` mounted. That tile reads `eth0`."},"574":{"id":"/dashboard-26","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."},"575":{"id":"/dashboard-27","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#look","content":"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page."},"576":{"id":"/dashboard-28","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."},"577":{"id":"/dashboard-29","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."},"578":{"id":"/dashboard-30","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"`KASM_UI_PORT` has to match on **both** sides of the port mapping. The daemon-side\nlistener listens on that number, so the mapping is `4443:4443`."},"579":{"id":"/dashboard-31","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."},"580":{"id":"/dashboard-32","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Kasm is a privileged Docker-in-Docker container and the only **always-on** privileged\nservice in this stack - the other privileged container is the opt-in workspace DinD\nsidecar. Keep Kasm LAN-only unless you have decided who may reach the public name\n(see SECURITY.md)."},"581":{"id":"/dashboard-33","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Losing the wizard after install is expected: use `:4443`. Resetting Kasm means\nremoving the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config."},"582":{"id":"/getting-started","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"page","content":"Getting started","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started"},"583":{"id":"/getting-started-0","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"First boot on a GMKtec EVO-T1, top to bottom — bootstrap the host, bring the stable stack up, register Coder, push the templates, open a workspace."},"584":{"id":"/getting-started-1","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"Before you start"},"585":{"id":"/getting-started-2","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"First boot"},"586":{"id":"/getting-started-3","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Once Coder answers"},"587":{"id":"/getting-started-4","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"What to read next"},"588":{"id":"/getting-started-5","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"},"589":{"id":"/getting-started-6","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"Bringing the stable stack up and pulling the models"},"590":{"id":"/getting-started-7","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"Registering the first Coder account, pushing both templates, wiring the chat agent"},"591":{"id":"/getting-started-8","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"Creating the first workspace from a git URL"},"592":{"id":"/getting-started-9","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"A working stack on a fresh box without reading the whole README first"},"593":{"id":"/getting-started-10","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"Knowing which step to re-run when a later one says a prerequisite is missing"},"594":{"id":"/getting-started-11","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."},"595":{"id":"/getting-started-12","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"A GMKtec EVO-T1 (or any box with the Arc 140T and enough RAM for the model in\n`OLLAMA_MODELS`) with Docker and Compose v2 installed."},"596":{"id":"/getting-started-13","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."},"597":{"id":"/getting-started-14","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Create the one file that holds real secrets, and lock it down before anything else reads\nit:"},"598":{"id":"/getting-started-15","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Bootstrap the toolchain — the only step that runs before Bazel exists, because it is what\ninstalls Bazelisk:"},"599":{"id":"/getting-started-16","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."},"600":{"id":"/getting-started-17","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Prepare the box: rotate the secrets, set `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, issue the self-signed\ncertificates, build `evo-t1-dev:latest`, pull the DinD image. It is idempotent, so re-run\nit after changing `docker-compose.yml`, `litellm/config.yaml`, `searxng/settings.yml` or\n`proxy/*.conf`."},"601":{"id":"/getting-started-18","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"},"602":{"id":"/getting-started-19","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Register the first account — that account is the site admin. Browsers only expose\n`crypto.randomUUID()` in a secure context, so this points at the TLS listener:"},"603":{"id":"/getting-started-20","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Push both templates (`workspace` first, `devcontainer` last). The source travels as a tar\non stdin and the credential is minted, used and deleted in the same run, so there is no\nhost `coder` CLI and no login here:"},"604":{"id":"/getting-started-21","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Wire the control-plane chat agent — print the intended end state, then apply it:"},"605":{"id":"/getting-started-22","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Create the first workspace. One repository per workspace is the default, and the template\npicks itself from the URL:"},"606":{"id":"/getting-started-23","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."},"607":{"id":"/getting-started-24","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Coder workspace templates — what the two templates build and which\nvariable does what."},"608":{"id":"/getting-started-25","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"LiteLLM model routing — why the answer you got came from the box it\ncame from."},"609":{"id":"/getting-started-26","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."},"610":{"id":"/getting-started-27","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Develop and\nBranching — the gates a change passes on its way back to `main`."},"611":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"page","content":"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents"},"612":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-0","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"What runs inside a workspace and in the Coder control plane: terminal profiles, the shared `~/.grok` profile, and where models come from."},"613":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-1","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Terminal and editor defaults"},"614":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-2","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image"},"615":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-3","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"The rendered `~/.grok` config"},"616":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-4","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-state-one-shared-profile-per-user","content":"Grok Build state: one shared profile per user"},"617":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-5","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"The two modes"},"618":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-6","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"What is shared"},"619":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-7","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Caveats, honestly"},"620":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-8","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Search lane and MCP servers"},"621":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-9","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#coder-agents-control-plane","content":"Coder Agents (control-plane)"},"622":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-10","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"It is always on"},"623":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-11","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Model wiring"},"624":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-12","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"The delegation sketch"},"625":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-13","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"What seeds the agent's context"},"626":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-14","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-this-is-not","content":"What this is not"},"627":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-15","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"},"628":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-16","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"},"629":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-17","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"},"630":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-18","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"One shared profile volume per user, and what last-booted rewrites"},"631":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-19","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"},"632":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-20","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Coder Agents on the control plane and the model config it needs"},"633":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-21","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Changing a model alias in one place and knowing which workspaces see it"},"634":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-22","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Explaining why one workspace's `~/.grok` disagrees with another's"},"635":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-23","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Deciding between the native search lane and the SearXNG fallback"},"636":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-24","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Deep reference for what runs *inside* a workspace and in the Coder control plane: the\nterminal profiles, the per-user `~/.grok` profile, where the CLI and the chat agent get\ntheir models, and what the stack seeds for the agent."},"637":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-25","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the template files themselves),\nlitellm-routing.md (what the aliases route to), and\ntroubleshooting.md."},"638":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-26","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."},"639":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-27","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The `tmux` profile re-joins (or starts) one session per folder, named\n`vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A second terminal window, a browser reload, or\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands in that same session, and a long job\nsurvives closing the tab. `tmux ls` lists the names."},"640":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-28","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root."},"641":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-29","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."},"642":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-30","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."},"643":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-31","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries\nan inlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`), because the\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. `workspace`\ninstalls code-server only when it is missing from `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`\nand starts it with `--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\"\n--ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload. `devcontainer` has no\npersistent prefix to reuse, so it installs fresh under `/tmp/code-server` on every\nstart."},"644":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-32","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."},"645":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-33","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."},"646":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-34","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Baked in at `/usr/local/bin/grok`, with an `agent` link beside it, **outside** the\n`/home/coder` home volume, so a rebuilt volume cannot lose it."},"647":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-35","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Pinned as `GROK_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` (1.0.70 at the time of\nwriting, mirrored as the Dockerfile `ARG` default). The image build unpacks that\npinned `.zst` and runs `grok --version` in two PATH contexts as a build-time smoke\ncheck."},"648":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-36","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Each template's startup script probes `command -v grok` on every start and\nre-downloads from `https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` - bounded, with retries - only\nwhile `grok` is missing from PATH. That installer follows the `stable` pointer, not\nthis pin. It deliberately does **not** fetch on every boot: the bake-in under\n`/usr/local/bin` is the upgrade path, so shipping a new CLI means bumping the pin,\nrebuilding the image (`scripts/build-dev-image.sh`) and re-pushing the templates."},"649":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-37","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"A self-installed CLI under `~/.grok/bin` stays first on the login-shell PATH, so it\nwins over the baked copy."},"650":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-38","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"**`/learn` is a bundled skill of this CLI**, not something the terminal adds: 1.0.70\nships it in its bundled manifest (`public-2026-10-20-r1`) as a user-invocable skill,\nand the pin this image carried before did not include it. Once the image is rebuilt\nand the templates re-pushed it shows up in the Grok Build slash menu, in the\ncode-server web terminal profile named **Grok Build** included, because that\nprofile execs the same `/usr/local/bin/grok` binary. Nothing to install and nothing\nto seed: an older CLI in `~/.grok/bin` is the one case that still hides it."},"651":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-39","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"Rendered per workspace from `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` (and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy of the same payload), and re-rendered idempotently\nwhile the managed block is intact."},"652":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-40","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."},"653":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-41","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"The seven aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field."},"654":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-42","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Under a shared profile the\nlast-booted workspace's key wins for the whole fleet."},"655":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-43","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."},"656":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-44","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server. Grok launches\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch. The proxy config names\n`$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the workspace environment from\nthe sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled by the push from\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth, which is what\nfills `GH_TOKEN` above."},"657":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-45","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."},"658":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-46","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"The managed block is guarded by a `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker, so a\nhand-edited config is left alone."},"659":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-47","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-state-one-shared-profile-per-user","content":"Every workspace gets its own `/home/coder` volume, and `~/.grok` is a **second,\nnested** mount inside it: a Docker volume named `grok-profile-<owner-id>`,\nreferenced by name rather than declared as a Terraform resource, which every\nworkspace of that owner mounts read-write at the same path **while that workspace is\nin `shared` mode**."},"660":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-48","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`shared` (the default here, so a workspace gets the profile unless you turn the\nmode off): configure the CLI once and the rest of that account's workspaces\ninherit it."},"661":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-49","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`private`: that workspace's `~/.grok` - `config.toml` included - lives on its own\nhome volume, differs from its siblings, and dies with the workspace (or survives a\nrebuild on the same volume)."},"662":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-50","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared profile while its siblings stay inside it."},"663":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-51","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Changing the mode needs a **rebuild** - the nested mount either exists or it does\nnot. A workspace that goes from `shared` to `private` starts on an empty profile:\nnothing is copied, and the shared volume is left alone."},"664":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-52","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"In `shared` mode: `config.toml`, `skills/`, `memory-v2/`, `sessions/`, `notes/` (the\ncanonical copy of each repository's private scratch `notes.md`), and a CLI you\ninstalled yourself under `~/.grok/bin`."},"665":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-53","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`. The startup script copies that file here on every start and restores\nit when a recreate leaves the clone without one. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no\nshared volume, so those notes die with that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like\n`config.toml`, the last workspace started for a given user and repository wins."},"666":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-54","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."},"667":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-55","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."},"668":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-56","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."},"669":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-57","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"One shared profile is one `config.toml` for every workspace that owner owns, and\nthe last boot to re-render it wins - for the default model, the affinity key and\nthe config extras alike."},"670":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-58","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."},"671":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-59","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"It is **not** a model transport, so `web_search = \"agent\"` and\n`supports_backend_search = false` stay as they are and the agent's own `web_search`\ntool still digests through the `agent` alias. Native search is the default:\n`disable_web_search = false` keeps the `web_search` tool in the session (it has no\nhosted backend on this box - LiteLLM answers \"tools of type 'web_search' are not\nsupported\" - so the client-side scrape path is what runs). The `searxng` MCP stdio\nserver is the fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. To park native search, set `disable_web_search` to true\nand start a new session. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"},"672":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-60","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"An empty result page and \"no answer\" are different answers, which is why the probe\nreports which one it got."},"673":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-61","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server (the fallback search lane).** Each is a boolean gate in the\ntemplate, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies it\nfleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`.\nThe `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`.\nThe five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."},"674":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-62","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."},"675":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-63","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which rides the owner's shared\nprofile volume in `shared` mode, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs both network and `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`\n(optional - empty means the public rate limit), while an identical lookup inside the\nTTL is answered locally. Two tools, in order -\n`resolve-library-id`, then `query-docs` on the id it returns. Prove it with\n`grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."},"676":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-64","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Cline and Roo/Kilo are pre-wired to the LiteLLM proxy (endpoint from `litellm_url`,\ndefault `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`; model alias `agent`, with\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`/`coder`/`coder-fast`/`chat` listed in\n`__evoT1StackModels`). The seed is\n`settings.json.tftpl`, copied onto the per-owner volume\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` (mounted at `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`) only\nwhile that file is absent, and symlinked from\n`~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`. Startup chowns that volume to\nthe home owner before the first write (empty named volumes are `root:root`\n755). Edits stick, and every workspace\nthat owner has shares the file; delete it to copy the template in again. From the\nhost it is the volume's `settings.json`:"},"677":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-65","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"A built dev container mounts neither the volume nor the symlink, so it is the one\nplace the seed is **merged** instead of linked: the inlined\n`coder_script.code_server` fork bakes a base64 copy of it and writes it over the\ndev container's private `User/settings.json` (or merges over a copy that survived a\nrebuild, when `jq` is on PATH; without `jq` the existing file is kept). Edits\ninside a built dev container therefore survive neither a rebuild nor a look at the\nshared file. Everywhere else no settings map is passed at all, because a merge\nwould replace the symlink."},"678":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-66","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Installing Cline, Roo, the Red Hat YAML extension and Material Icon Theme is a\nmanual step in the Extensions panel (Open VSX by default). If an extension build\nnames its settings differently, set the OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key in its\nUI."},"679":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-67","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#coder-agents-control-plane","content":"Coder v2.36.6 ships *Coder Agents*: a coding agent that lives in the Coder **server** -\na chat UI and an API beside your workspaces, not inside one. A chat runs its tools\n(`read_file`, `edit_files`, `execute`, `spawn_agent`) against a workspace over that\nworkspace's agent connection, and consults two pieces of per-workspace context first:\nan **instructions** file and any **skills** it discovers. The agent itself is upstream\nCoder, not a home-grown agent."},"680":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-68","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."},"681":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-69","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."},"682":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-70","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."},"683":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-71","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"If you know a real experiment you want (`chat-advisor`, `chat-virtual-desktop`,\n...), list it in `.env` and restart the coder service. Empty means no experiments\non."},"684":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-72","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"},"685":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-73","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Apply it (or re-run after a skip):"},"686":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-74","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0.\n`SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap and stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."},"687":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-75","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"A chat turn needs an enabled chat model config - the chat's own pick, else the\ndeployment default, else it errors before the first tool call - and that wiring is\nwhat the script writes. It mints a short-lived admin API token from the stack's own\nPostgres (`token_name 'stack-coder-agents'`, deleted by the trap, never printed) and\ndrives the server's experimental chat API:"},"688":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-76","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"an `openai-compat` AI provider named **`litellm`** (not `litellm-auto`), base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1` - LiteLLM over the compose network, *server-side*,\nauthenticated with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`. The agent dials the in-process AI Gateway\n(aibridge), which forwards to the provider's `base_url` + `/chat/completions`, so\n`/v1` has to be part of the base URL and the key has to match the live proxy: with\n`.env` leaving the key empty, compose still boots the proxy on its\n`change-me-litellm` interpolation default, and that default is what gets sent.\nCreated when absent, drift-patched when an existing provider carries a drifted\n`base_url` or key."},"689":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-77","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"chat model configs `agent` (context limit 262144, set as the default; LiteLLM bakes\n`reasoning_effort` `xhigh`), `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` (same\nlane with a baked thinking level, not default), and `chat` (32768), for models not\nalready registered. `coder` and `coder-fast` stay out on purpose: on this stack's\nIPEX-LLM runtime those weights return tool calls as plain text with no\n`tool_calls`, and an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or edit, so\nregistering them would only add a silently broken model to the pickers.\n`context_limit` is the agent's own compaction budget (a 70 per cent default\ncompaction threshold applies), which is why it is pinned per alias."},"690":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-78","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"pins for the four auxiliary lanes - `general` and `explore` (the two subagent\ntypes) take `agent`, `compaction` and `title_generation` (the helper calls) take\n`chat` - written only for lanes that carry no pin yet, so an admin's own pins stay\nin place."},"691":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-79","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"a three-line system-prompt addendum, appended after Coder's default prompt text\n(`include_default_system_prompt: true`), that keeps the delegation and\nsecret-hygiene rules which must hold even when a chat has no workspace attached."},"692":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-80","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Coder's optional AI Gateway proxy stays off - LiteLLM remains the model transport,\nwhich is why no AI-Gateway keys exist in `docker-compose.yml`."},"693":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-81","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Chat on the Agents page -> if the ask needs more tooling than chat has, Coder Agents\nprovisions or uses a workspace through `execute` -> big multi-file tasks are\n*delegated inside* that workspace: a headless Grok Build CLI run, started backgrounded\nand polled:"},"694":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-82","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Backgrounded and polled because `execute` waits only a short window per tool call\n(about a minute), so a synchronous foreground `grok -p` would die mid-run on any big\ntask. Coder Agents then reviews the delegated diff, runs the project's tests, and\ncommits. Subagents spawned by Coder Agents cannot create workspaces: provisioning\ndecisions stop at the top-level chat."},"695":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-83","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Four files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent:"},"696":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-84","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"},"697":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-85","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"698":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-86","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"},"699":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-87","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"700":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-88","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"1 and 2 are the Coder agent's *default* discovery paths."},"701":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-89","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"3 and 4 are the same text under the Grok Build CLI's convention, and the CLI is\nwhat `execute`-lane delegation drives. The chat agent dedupes skills by name,\nfirst match wins, home scope before a working-dir path, so one agent never loads a\nskill twice: the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."},"702":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-90","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"The text is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced). It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."},"703":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-91","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."},"704":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-92","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Both `main.tf` files deliberately set **no** `CODER_AGENT_EXP_*` env keys on\n`coder_agent.main`: those keys *replace* the agent's discovery defaults, and the\ndefaults already cover exactly the seeded paths - the `~/.coder` roots plus a\nworking-dir-relative `.agents/skills`, which resolves inside the agent's working\ndir (`/home/coder` in `workspace`; in `devcontainer` with the toggle on, the\ncloned folder under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, where a dev container built on\nthe sidecar carries its own home and no seeded path is visible from inside it\nanyway)."},"705":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-93","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Each seeded file carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker - in\n`SKILL.md` directly under the `---` frontmatter, since the agent's skill parser\nrequires frontmatter on line 1 and silently drops anything else. Delete a file to\ntake it over; edit it and the stack leaves it alone. Either way the marker never\nreaches the model: the agent strips HTML comments from injected instruction text."},"706":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-94","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume. They are *not*\non the shared `~/.grok` profile volume above, and seeding does not care whether\nthat mount exists."},"707":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-95","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-this-is-not","content":"Coder Agents is control-plane-native. This stack does not wrap Grok Build: the wiring\nabove *teaches* (skill + instructions) and *equips* (the workspace-side CLI with its\nLiteLLM alias) the native Coder agent to drive Grok Build when work is too big to\nhand-drive. Delegation is a taught habit, not the product."},"708":{"id":"/hermes","page_id":"/hermes","type":"page","content":"Hermes host assistant","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes"},"709":{"id":"/hermes-0","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"The on-host assistant with the Web Dashboard on :9119: how to reach it on the LAN and on its public name, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command."},"710":{"id":"/hermes-1","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#access","content":"Access"},"711":{"id":"/hermes-2","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Auth"},"712":{"id":"/hermes-3","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#models","content":"Models"},"713":{"id":"/hermes-4","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"Mounts and \"be the host\""},"714":{"id":"/hermes-5","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Approvals"},"715":{"id":"/hermes-6","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"Bootstrap and skills"},"716":{"id":"/hermes-7","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Security notes"},"717":{"id":"/hermes-8","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Commands"},"718":{"id":"/hermes-9","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"How to reach the dashboard, on the LAN and on the public name"},"719":{"id":"/hermes-10","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Sign-in: HTTP basic auth, with Authelia in front of the public name"},"720":{"id":"/hermes-11","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"The models it calls through LiteLLM"},"721":{"id":"/hermes-12","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"What the container mounts, and what \"be the host\" costs"},"722":{"id":"/hermes-13","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Approval prompts, bootstrap seeding, and the skills directory"},"723":{"id":"/hermes-14","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Running stack operations by conversation with a plan and an approval first"},"724":{"id":"/hermes-15","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Reaching the assistant on the LAN, and on the public name when `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` includes it"},"725":{"id":"/hermes-16","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Hermes is the Nous Research Hermes Agent running as the interactive assistant **on the\nstack host itself**: not inside a workspace, not in a streamed desktop, but with the\ncheckout, the compose project and the docker socket in reach. It adds an official Web\nDashboard with a Chat tab that carries approval prompts, so the assistant can plan a\nchange, show the plan, and wait for you to approve it before it touches anything."},"726":{"id":"/hermes-17","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."},"727":{"id":"/hermes-18","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"Related: dashboard.md (the card that links here),\nlitellm-routing.md (the aliases Hermes calls),\npublic-access.md (the public name, when `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` sets it),\nSECURITY.md."},"728":{"id":"/hermes-19","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"Dashboard on host port **9119**, from `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PORT`. The dev sandbox shifts\nit to **19119** the same way it shifts every other published port."},"729":{"id":"/hermes-20","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."},"730":{"id":"/hermes-21","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"**Public name, opt-in:** `https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` when that name is\nlisted in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`. Bootstrap classifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and\nwrites `STACK_HERMES_URL`. The path is the same as the other public names: Cloudflare\n(orange cloud, WebSockets on by default), Traefik on the other LAN server (TLS\n`*.overeazy.io`, Authelia), the `router` on this box `:8080`, then\n`http://hermes:9119`, with the original Host preserved. Traefik's backend is\n`:8080`, not `:9119`. See public-access.md."},"731":{"id":"/hermes-22","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"Sign-in is HTTP basic auth from `.env`: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` (default `admin`) and\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`. Bootstrap generates the password; it is **not** the\nHomepage password, and `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` does not rotate it.\nThat basic auth stays on every door. Hermes v0.22.5 refuses to bind `0.0.0.0` with no\nprovider. On the public URL Authelia challenges first and basic auth challenges\nsecond; two logins are expected. LAN `:9119`, and LAN `:8080` with this name's Host\nheader, never see Authelia, so basic auth is the credential on those paths."},"732":{"id":"/hermes-23","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."},"733":{"id":"/hermes-24","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."},"734":{"id":"/hermes-25","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Hermes since the June 2026 hardening refuses a non-loopback bind without a\n`DashboardAuthProvider`. Binding `0.0.0.0` with no provider is a boot error, not a\nwarning."},"735":{"id":"/hermes-26","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a deprecated no-op. Setting it changes nothing and buys\nnothing, and it is not how the public name is opened."},"736":{"id":"/hermes-27","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Compose supplies the provider: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_USERNAME`,\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD` and `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_SECRET`,\ninterpolated from `.env` with the same non-empty fallbacks `.env.sample`\ncarries (`admin` / `change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). An empty\ninterpolation default is a boot error on `0.0.0.0`: the plugin skips\nregistration and the gateway refuses to bind."},"737":{"id":"/hermes-28","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."},"738":{"id":"/hermes-29","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Hermes talks to the LiteLLM proxy the stack already runs: base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1`, model alias **`agent`**. That is the tool-capable Spark lane,\nwhich is what an agent loop needs, and it stays the default."},"739":{"id":"/hermes-30","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`agent` also thinks hard by default: it bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`.\n**`agent-low`**, **`agent-medium`** and **`agent-xhigh`** are the thinking variants of\nit - the same Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on the same three Spark boxes, with real\n`tool_calls` and that level baked into the alias instead. Use one when a run needs a\ndifferent level; Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and `xhigh` and nothing else."},"740":{"id":"/hermes-31","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."},"741":{"id":"/hermes-32","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Fallbacks, in the order worth trying them:"},"742":{"id":"/hermes-33","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`chat` - `qwen2.5:7b` on the Arc, and it returns real `tool_calls`. The reliable\ndegrade path when every Spark box is down."},"743":{"id":"/hermes-34","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`coder` / `coder-fast` - the Qwen2.5-Coder weights on the Arc. Chat is good, but\nthose weights write the tool call into the message body as plain text, so agent\nturns silently lose their tools. See\nlitellm-routing.md."},"744":{"id":"/hermes-35","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Switch it inside the container with `hermes config set model.model chat` (or\n`agent-medium` for a thinking level), which edits the live config under `/opt/data`."},"745":{"id":"/hermes-36","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Nous Portal is an optional upstream, not a default, and this page does not document it.\nNothing here needs an account."},"746":{"id":"/hermes-37","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                        | Inside      | Why                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `hermes-data` (named volume) | `/opt/data` | config, sessions, memory, SQLite |","table":"table-0"},"747":{"id":"/hermes-38","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                   | Inside                           | Why                     |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `${STACK_HOST_PATH:-.}` | `${STACK_HOST_PATH:-/opt/stack}` | identical-path checkout |","table":"table-0"},"748":{"id":"/hermes-39","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount             | Inside                   | Why                |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------ |\n| `./hermes/skills` | `/opt/stack-skills` (ro) | git-tracked skills |","table":"table-0"},"749":{"id":"/hermes-40","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                  | Inside                 | Why                           |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `/var/run/docker.sock` | `/var/run/docker.sock` | compose and container control |","table":"table-0"},"750":{"id":"/hermes-41","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`/opt/data` is a **named volume on purpose**. Hermes keeps a SQLite database there in\nWAL mode, and WAL on a virtiofs bind mount is not safe. Bind-mounting that path is how\nyou corrupt the assistant's own memory."},"751":{"id":"/hermes-42","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find. Empty (CI\n`compose config`) falls back to `.:/opt/stack`. Seeded `terminal.cwd` is\n`${STACK_HOST_PATH}`, expanded from the same env."},"752":{"id":"/hermes-43","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`hermes/skills/` is tracked in git. Add a skill by editing the repo, never by editing\nthe volume; the volume copy is not the record."},"753":{"id":"/hermes-44","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`group_add: ${DOCKER_GID}` plus the socket is the same credential the `coder` service\nalready holds: root-equivalent on the host. Nothing new is exposed here that the Coder\ncontainer does not already have - which is precisely why approvals, below, are the\ncontrol rather than the mount list. A public Hermes name has that same standing as\npublic Coder."},"754":{"id":"/hermes-45","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`HERMES_UID` / `HERMES_GID` are set to the host operator, so a file Hermes writes into\n`$STACK_HOST_PATH` is yours to commit and not owned by uid 10000."},"755":{"id":"/hermes-46","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`/home` is **not** mounted in v1. Hermes cannot see your dotfiles, your SSH keys or\nyour shell history, and it should not need to."},"756":{"id":"/hermes-47","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."},"757":{"id":"/hermes-48","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Leave the mode on `manual`. `off` is `--yolo`: every mutating command executes on the\nhost with no card."},"758":{"id":"/hermes-49","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."},"759":{"id":"/hermes-50","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Working agreement with the assistant: it explains the plan in chat, then waits for the\napproval card in the Chat tab before running anything mutating. If a run looks like it\nis acting without one, stop it and re-read the mode."},"760":{"id":"/hermes-51","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."},"761":{"id":"/hermes-52","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"To reseed deliberately:"},"762":{"id":"/hermes-53","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"`<mount>` is the host directory of the Hermes data volume (what compose mounts at\n`/opt/data`). `--force` is how an existing volume picks up the seeded\n`dashboard.trusted_proxies`. Setting that key with `hermes config set` is the\nin-container alternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is not in the volume;\nrecreate the container and the env applies."},"763":{"id":"/hermes-54","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"A skill is one directory: `hermes/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, with\nagentskills.io frontmatter for the name and description.\nCommit it, and the next `up` has it."},"764":{"id":"/hermes-55","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"A bind-mounted skill needs no recreate when the agent reloads skills from disk; start a\n**new Chat session** and the new skill is offered. Recreate only if it still does not\nshow up, since `restart` keeps the old mounts."},"765":{"id":"/hermes-56","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."},"766":{"id":"/hermes-57","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`). It does not change\nwhat the container can do: local terminal, host mounts and the docker socket still\nmean this agent can affect the real host. Treat its approvals the way you treat a\nroot shell. That is the same standing as public Coder."},"767":{"id":"/hermes-58","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"HTTP basic auth stays, including on the public URL. Authelia is the gate in front of\nthat name only. LAN `:9119` and a matching Host on `:8080` bypass Authelia, so the\nbasic-auth provider is load-bearing. It is a provider, not a toggle."},"768":{"id":"/hermes-59","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` does not remove that requirement. Leave\n`approvals.mode` on `manual`."},"769":{"id":"/hermes-60","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Secrets live in gitignored `.env` and in the `hermes-data` volume. Never commit them,\nand never paste `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` into a chat to prove a route works."},"770":{"id":"/hermes-61","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Prefer structured commands - the existing Bazel scripts and `docker compose` - over\nad-hoc shell. A script the repo owns is reviewable and test-covered; a one-liner the\nmodel invented is neither. See AGENTS.md."},"771":{"id":"/hermes-62","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"No GitHub PAT in Hermes. GitHub auth in this stack is Coder External Auth only, and a\ntoken here would sit on a container that can both write to the checkout and drive the\nhost daemon. See SECURITY.md."},"772":{"id":"/hermes-63","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"},"773":{"id":"/hermes-64","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Bootstrap options, including `--data` and `--force`:"},"774":{"id":"/hermes-65","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Dev sandbox, which puts the dashboard on 19119:"},"775":{"id":"/hermes-66","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."},"776":{"id":"/","page_id":"/","type":"page","content":"evo-t1-coder-stack","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/"},"777":{"id":"/-0","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"One compose stack that turns a GMKtec EVO-T1 into a self-hosted dev-workspace server — Coder, LiteLLM, IPEX-LLM Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes."},"778":{"id":"/-1","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/#start-here","content":"Start here"},"779":{"id":"/-2","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"What runs here"},"780":{"id":"/-3","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"What runs on the box and which door each service answers on"},"781":{"id":"/-4","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"Where to start depending on what you came to do"},"782":{"id":"/-5","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"How the sidebar tabs divide the subjects"},"783":{"id":"/-6","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"Getting oriented in one screen before opening a terminal"},"784":{"id":"/-7","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"Reaching the page that owns a subject from one card, instead of reading four that touch it"},"785":{"id":"/-8","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."},"786":{"id":"/-9","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."},"787":{"id":"/-10","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"},"788":{"id":"/-11","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"},"789":{"id":"/-12","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"},"790":{"id":"/-13","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"},"791":{"id":"/-14","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                     |\n| ------- | --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 80 / 443  | The landing page, and the only thing publishing it |","table":"table-0"},"792":{"id":"/-15","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                             |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3001      | Control plane: one container per workspace |","table":"table-0"},"793":{"id":"/-16","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                        |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 3002      | The plain listener a workspace agent dials via `host.docker.internal` |","table":"table-0"},"794":{"id":"/-17","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                            |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 4000      | The single OpenAI-compatible door in front of every model |","table":"table-0"},"795":{"id":"/-18","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                          |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | —         | The Arc 140T model runtime, never published to the host |","table":"table-0"},"796":{"id":"/-19","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port   | What it is for                                   |\n| ------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3000 → 4443 | Streamed desktops; the wizard only on first boot |","table":"table-0"},"797":{"id":"/-20","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                                            |\n| ------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 9119      | The host assistant. LAN `:9119` stays; the public name is opt-in via `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` |","table":"table-0"},"798":{"id":"/-21","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                               |\n| ------- | --------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 8181      | Keyless metasearch, the fallback search lane |","table":"table-0"},"799":{"id":"/-22","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"Ollama is reachable only from inside the compose network: LiteLLM is the gateway, and it\nis the thing that carries the master key."},"800":{"id":"/-23","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."},"801":{"id":"/learn","page_id":"/learn","type":"page","content":"Learn the stack","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Learn the stack"],"tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn"},"802":{"id":"/learn-0","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."},"803":{"id":"/learn-1","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"How the pieces fit"},"804":{"id":"/learn-2","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-model-lane","content":"The model lane"},"805":{"id":"/learn-3","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-assistant-on-the-host","content":"The assistant on the host"},"806":{"id":"/learn-4","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-rules-a-change-follows","content":"The rules a change follows"},"807":{"id":"/learn-5","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"Which page explains which part of the stack"},"808":{"id":"/learn-6","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"},"809":{"id":"/learn-7","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"},"810":{"id":"/learn-8","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"Knowing which page owns a concept when two of them seem to"},"811":{"id":"/learn-9","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."},"812":{"id":"/learn-10","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"Coder workspace templates owns the templates, the create form and\nthe variables; Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal\nowns what runs inside the container once it is up."},"813":{"id":"/learn-11","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-model-lane","content":"One OpenAI-compatible door, several backends behind it: the Arc 140T through IPEX-LLM, and\noptional Spark boxes. Which one answers is a routing decision made in\nlitellm-routing.md, not in the client — and that page also states the\none thing the local model cannot do."},"814":{"id":"/learn-12","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-assistant-on-the-host","content":"Hermes is the interactive assistant with the checkout, the compose project and\nthe docker socket in reach, gated by approval prompts. Its public name is opt-in through\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`; with that list empty the dashboard stays on the LAN.\npublic-access.md is the page that decides what the box exposes."},"815":{"id":"/learn-13","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-rules-a-change-follows","content":"project-conventions.md is what a script has to look like to be\nmergeable, and branching.md is how it gets to `main`. The Bazel harness they\nboth assume is in the Contribute tab."},"816":{"id":"/litellm-routing","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"page","content":"LiteLLM model routing","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing"},"817":{"id":"/litellm-routing-0","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."},"818":{"id":"/litellm-routing-1","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#one-front-door","content":"One front door"},"819":{"id":"/litellm-routing-2","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Two doors, one key"},"820":{"id":"/litellm-routing-3","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"What an alias is"},"821":{"id":"/litellm-routing-4","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"Config keys that gate all of this"},"822":{"id":"/litellm-routing-5","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"The alias table"},"823":{"id":"/litellm-routing-6","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"Reading the table"},"824":{"id":"/litellm-routing-7","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#tool-calls-the-measured-limitation","content":"Tool calls: the measured limitation"},"825":{"id":"/litellm-routing-8","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"What was measured"},"826":{"id":"/litellm-routing-9","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"What agent mode needs"},"827":{"id":"/litellm-routing-10","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#routing-inside-the-agent-group","content":"Routing inside the `agent` group"},"828":{"id":"/litellm-routing-11","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"One group, three deployments"},"829":{"id":"/litellm-routing-12","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"The picker: least-busy"},"830":{"id":"/litellm-routing-13","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The pin: session_affinity"},"831":{"id":"/litellm-routing-14","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"What carries a session id"},"832":{"id":"/litellm-routing-15","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"What floats freely"},"833":{"id":"/litellm-routing-16","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"When a pinned box cools or goes unhealthy"},"834":{"id":"/litellm-routing-17","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"Fallback: `agent` to `coder`"},"835":{"id":"/litellm-routing-18","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-routing-back","content":"Reading the routing back"},"836":{"id":"/litellm-routing-19","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The Agent backend card"},"837":{"id":"/litellm-routing-20","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"Which box answered"},"838":{"id":"/litellm-routing-21","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"The measured burst"},"839":{"id":"/litellm-routing-22","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Editing an alias"},"840":{"id":"/litellm-routing-23","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"Where an alias edit lands"},"841":{"id":"/litellm-routing-24","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#why-this-shape","content":"Why this shape"},"842":{"id":"/litellm-routing-25","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"IPEX-LLM over vanilla Ollama"},"843":{"id":"/litellm-routing-26","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"MoE over dense"},"844":{"id":"/litellm-routing-27","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"The one-front-door rule"},"845":{"id":"/litellm-routing-28","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"},"846":{"id":"/litellm-routing-29","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"},"847":{"id":"/litellm-routing-30","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"},"848":{"id":"/litellm-routing-31","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"},"849":{"id":"/litellm-routing-32","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Routing inside the `agent` group, and the `agent` to `coder` fallback"},"850":{"id":"/litellm-routing-33","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Reading the routing back, and editing an alias safely"},"851":{"id":"/litellm-routing-34","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Moving a workload between the Arc iGPU and a Spark box without touching a workspace"},"852":{"id":"/litellm-routing-35","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Diagnosing a wrong-backend answer from the routing rather than from the client"},"853":{"id":"/litellm-routing-36","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Deep reference for the model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request\npicks a backend, what a pinned conversation does, and what happens when a Spark box goes\ndown."},"854":{"id":"/litellm-routing-37","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."},"855":{"id":"/litellm-routing-38","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the templates that render\n`~/.grok/config.toml`), grok-build-and-agents.md (the CLI and\nthe control-plane chat), project-conventions.md."},"856":{"id":"/litellm-routing-39","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Workspaces and desktops always talk to one base URL,\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, and pick a model alias. That is the\nhost-gateway door."},"857":{"id":"/litellm-routing-40","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."},"858":{"id":"/litellm-routing-41","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Both doors authenticate with the same `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` value."},"859":{"id":"/litellm-routing-42","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Inside a workspace the key travels as the `LITELLM_API_KEY` environment variable, which\n`~/.grok/config.toml` references by `env_key`, so no key byte lands in a file."},"860":{"id":"/litellm-routing-43","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"An alias is a `model_name:` entry in `litellm/config.yaml`, not a model."},"861":{"id":"/litellm-routing-44","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"Every alias is a stable name. A workspace setting never has to change when a backend\nmoves behind one."},"862":{"id":"/litellm-routing-45","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"A rename or deletion is the edit that can reach workspaces: both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins render the alias list into\n`~/.grok/config.toml`, a running workspace keeps the copy it rendered at start, and\nthe next start re-renders it."},"863":{"id":"/litellm-routing-46","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."},"864":{"id":"/litellm-routing-47","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."},"865":{"id":"/litellm-routing-48","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes."},"866":{"id":"/litellm-routing-49","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."},"867":{"id":"/litellm-routing-50","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 30` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 5`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot."},"868":{"id":"/litellm-routing-51","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` matches the CLI's own streaming idle\nallowance, so a slow call may take a full twenty minutes before either end gives up."},"869":{"id":"/litellm-routing-52","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model                | Backend                                                                                | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `agent` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` (TensorFold on :8888 - one deployment per Spark box, optional) | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"870":{"id":"/litellm-routing-53","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias       | Model                | Backend                                                                          | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ----------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-low` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `low` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"871":{"id":"/litellm-routing-54","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias          | Model                | Backend                                                                             | Structured tool calls                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-medium` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `medium` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"872":{"id":"/litellm-routing-55","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model                | Backend                                                                            | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-xhigh` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"873":{"id":"/litellm-routing-56","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"874":{"id":"/litellm-routing-57","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias        | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder-fast` | `qwen2.5-coder:14b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"875":{"id":"/litellm-routing-58","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias  | Model        | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------ | ------------ | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` |         | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"876":{"id":"/litellm-routing-59","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model               | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `SPARK1_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |","table":"table-0"},"877":{"id":"/litellm-routing-60","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias        | Model        | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------ | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` | `SPARK2_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |","table":"table-0"},"878":{"id":"/litellm-routing-61","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."},"879":{"id":"/litellm-routing-62","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The three `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter."},"880":{"id":"/litellm-routing-63","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."},"881":{"id":"/litellm-routing-64","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"Those three groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not three\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."},"882":{"id":"/litellm-routing-65","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `spark-*` rows are a different thing: the same Qwen2.5-Coder and Qwen2.5\nweights, served over Ollama on the Spark boxes instead of on the Arc."},"883":{"id":"/litellm-routing-66","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`unverified` on the `spark-*` rows means exactly that: this box never ran them\nagainst a live Spark, so the column records the absence of a measurement, not a\n\"no\"."},"884":{"id":"/litellm-routing-67","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."},"885":{"id":"/litellm-routing-68","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."},"886":{"id":"/litellm-routing-69","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."},"887":{"id":"/litellm-routing-70","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"Measured against this box's IPEX-LLM Ollama, whose bundled `ollama` is 0.9.3."},"888":{"id":"/litellm-routing-71","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"A request carrying `tools` for `qwen2.5-coder:32b` or `:14b` comes back as plain text,\n`finish_reason: \"stop\"`, and no `tool_calls` field at all."},"889":{"id":"/litellm-routing-72","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"It behaves that way on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`."},"890":{"id":"/litellm-routing-73","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."},"891":{"id":"/litellm-routing-74","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."},"892":{"id":"/litellm-routing-75","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."},"893":{"id":"/litellm-routing-76","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Agent mode therefore needs either the Spark `agent` lane or the small `chat` model."},"894":{"id":"/litellm-routing-77","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Grok Build, Cline, Roo and Kilo all default to `agent`."},"895":{"id":"/litellm-routing-78","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (default),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. The four Spark aliases all drive tools, so all four are registerable - the\nthinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that picker."},"896":{"id":"/litellm-routing-79","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."},"897":{"id":"/litellm-routing-80","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."},"898":{"id":"/litellm-routing-81","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"One endpoint per box, one box per endpoint. Never point two deployments, or two\naliases, at the same physical machine: the proxy counts deployments, not machines."},"899":{"id":"/litellm-routing-82","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."},"900":{"id":"/litellm-routing-83","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Its counters are per deployment and expire after a hardcoded 3600 s; there is no\n`routing_strategy_args` block to set, and `ttl` there would be a paste from newer docs."},"901":{"id":"/litellm-routing-84","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"It decides only for unpinned traffic: the affinity pin below resolves first, and\n`least-busy` runs when nothing is pinned, when the pinned deployment is unhealthy or in\ncooldown, and for any request with no session id."},"902":{"id":"/litellm-routing-85","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Health checks, cooldowns and those counters all attribute per deployment through\n`model_info.id`, which is why each box carries a unique id: `agent-spark-1`,\n`agent-spark-2`, `agent-spark-3`. Each thinking group has its own triple -\n`agent-low-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-medium-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-xhigh-spark-1/2/3` - so the\nsame physical box is a separate deployment, with its own health state and its own\nin-flight counter, inside every group it belongs to."},"903":{"id":"/litellm-routing-86","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"A cooled box therefore stays visibly out of `healthy_deployments` instead of blending\ninto the group's aggregate stats."},"904":{"id":"/litellm-routing-87","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."},"905":{"id":"/litellm-routing-88","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"One conversation from one client sticks to the box that answered its first turn, for as\nlong as that box stays healthy."},"906":{"id":"/litellm-routing-89","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."},"907":{"id":"/litellm-routing-90","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A conversation that goes quiet for longer than the TTL may move."},"908":{"id":"/litellm-routing-91","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A busy pinned box sheds its own overflow in its own scheduler instead of queueing\ninside the proxy."},"909":{"id":"/litellm-routing-92","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."},"910":{"id":"/litellm-routing-93","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"A value has to look like an id to count: alphanumerics, hyphens or underscores, 8\ncharacters or more. The sources, in order, are `x-litellm-trace-id`, then\n`x-litellm-session-id`, then any `x-<vendor>-session-id`, then\n`session-id`/`session_id`/`thread-id`/`conversation_id` (Codex style), then W3C\ntraceparent and baggage, plus `metadata.session_id` in the body."},"911":{"id":"/litellm-routing-94","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."},"912":{"id":"/litellm-routing-95","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"Both workspace templates render `x-litellm-session-id = \"ws-<workspace-uuid>\"` under\n`[models]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`, so one key covers every conversation of a\nworkspace, subagent calls included."},"913":{"id":"/litellm-routing-96","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"In `shared` profile mode that is one file per user, re-rendered by the last-booted\nworkspace, which then shares that key across that user's workspaces."},"914":{"id":"/litellm-routing-97","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Cline and Roo editor traffic carries no session id: the extension settings have no\ncustom-header field."},"915":{"id":"/litellm-routing-98","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Coder Agents chats carry none either. Coder's aibridge forwards model calls with the\nprovider key only and adds no per-conversation session id."},"916":{"id":"/litellm-routing-99","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Both float across the healthy boxes by load."},"917":{"id":"/litellm-routing-100","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."},"918":{"id":"/litellm-routing-101","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The pin itself is kept."},"919":{"id":"/litellm-routing-102","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."},"920":{"id":"/litellm-routing-103","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."},"921":{"id":"/litellm-routing-104","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"Chat stays usable while agent mode quietly loses its tools. That is the point of the\nhop, not an accident."},"922":{"id":"/litellm-routing-105","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"Each thinking alias carries the same hop of its own - `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`, and the\nsame for `agent-medium` and `agent-xhigh`. Groups are independent, so none of them\ninherits `agent`'s entry, and all four go down with exactly the same boxes."},"923":{"id":"/litellm-routing-106","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"It used to be a chain, `agent -> agent-fast -> coder`. `agent-fast` was a second alias\nfor the same model on a second box with a lower output ceiling; one group of three equal\ndeployments already covers every reachable box, so the chain collapsed to one acyclic\nhop."},"924":{"id":"/litellm-routing-107","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"If a concurrency cap is ever reintroduced, its pairing is `fallbacks` plus\n`context_window_fallbacks`. Both, not one."},"925":{"id":"/litellm-routing-108","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."},"926":{"id":"/litellm-routing-109","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."},"927":{"id":"/litellm-routing-110","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"Its per-box widgets go to error state while `agent` still answers, because `agent`\nanswers from the Arc while every Spark is down."},"928":{"id":"/litellm-routing-111","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, so they follow the LAN, and they answer without a key."},"929":{"id":"/litellm-routing-112","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."},"930":{"id":"/litellm-routing-113","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."},"931":{"id":"/litellm-routing-114","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."},"932":{"id":"/litellm-routing-115","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"A wrong model id does not crash the proxy: 1.102.1 builds the Router with\n`ignore_invalid_deployments` forced to True, so a bad id drops that deployment at boot\nwith one log line and later surfaces as \"No deployments available\"."},"933":{"id":"/litellm-routing-116","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"Measured on a harness boot of this config against the live Spark boxes, before\n`session_affinity` existed."},"934":{"id":"/litellm-routing-117","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."},"935":{"id":"/litellm-routing-118","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"It spread 12/10/8 across the three deployments and returned 30x 200."},"936":{"id":"/litellm-routing-119","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."},"937":{"id":"/litellm-routing-120","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"A single conversation pinned to one box now keeps its streams on that box while it\nstays healthy - that is the behaviour the burst predates."},"938":{"id":"/litellm-routing-121","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Edit `litellm/config.yaml`."},"939":{"id":"/litellm-routing-122","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Restart the proxy on the stack you changed, so it re-reads that file."},"940":{"id":"/litellm-routing-123","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Check that `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY` and `SPARK_3_API_KEY` are still\nnon-empty. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy, before it ever reaches\nthe box."},"941":{"id":"/litellm-routing-124","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Neither stack runner has a `restart` subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`\nare the whole set), so the proxy restart stays a direct compose call. Stable stack:"},"942":{"id":"/litellm-routing-125","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"},"943":{"id":"/litellm-routing-126","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (default),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`)."},"944":{"id":"/litellm-routing-127","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."},"945":{"id":"/litellm-routing-128","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."},"946":{"id":"/litellm-routing-129","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."},"947":{"id":"/litellm-routing-130","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."},"948":{"id":"/litellm-routing-131","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."},"949":{"id":"/litellm-routing-132","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."},"950":{"id":"/litellm-routing-133","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"On a memory-rich but bandwidth- and latency-constrained iGPU that is the sweet spot,\nbecause an equivalent dense model would move every parameter per token and run much\nslower at the same footprint."},"951":{"id":"/litellm-routing-134","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"Workspaces see a stable OpenAI-compatible URL and stable alias names."},"952":{"id":"/litellm-routing-135","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."},"953":{"id":"/operate","page_id":"/operate","type":"page","content":"Operate the box","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Operate the box"],"tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate"},"954":{"id":"/operate-0","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Day-to-day operation — the dashboard and desktops, what to refresh after a git pull, the public shape, and the symptom-first fix list."},"955":{"id":"/operate-1","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"The two commands"},"956":{"id":"/operate-2","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"Where to look"},"957":{"id":"/operate-3","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"Before you reach for a hammer"},"958":{"id":"/operate-4","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Which page answers which operational question"},"959":{"id":"/operate-5","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"The two commands an operator runs most"},"960":{"id":"/operate-6","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Getting to the right runbook in one click instead of searching the README"},"961":{"id":"/operate-7","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Refreshing a stale checkout before diagnosing what it looks like"},"962":{"id":"/operate-8","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"Everything else is a target those two feed:"},"963":{"id":"/operate-9","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."},"964":{"id":"/operate-10","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page         |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| A card is red, or the dashboard will not load | dashboard.md |","table":"table-0"},"965":{"id":"/operate-11","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Something fails, and you have an error string | troubleshooting.md |","table":"table-0"},"966":{"id":"/operate-12","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page              |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| A `git pull` landed and the ports moved | config-updates.md |","table":"table-0"},"967":{"id":"/operate-13","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A model answered from the wrong backend | litellm-routing.md |","table":"table-0"},"968":{"id":"/operate-14","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                    | Page               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A workspace is stuck, or its editor will not start | coder-templates.md |","table":"table-0"},"969":{"id":"/operate-15","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                   | Page             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| A public name does not resolve or redirects wrong | public-access.md |","table":"table-0"},"970":{"id":"/operate-16","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                | Page      |\n| ------------------------------ | --------- |\n| The assistant will not sign in | hermes.md |","table":"table-0"},"971":{"id":"/operate-17","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."},"972":{"id":"/project-conventions","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"page","content":"Project conventions","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions"},"973":{"id":"/project-conventions-0","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."},"974":{"id":"/project-conventions-1","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"File header"},"975":{"id":"/project-conventions-2","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Functions"},"976":{"id":"/project-conventions-3","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Tests"},"977":{"id":"/project-conventions-4","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Ingress"},"978":{"id":"/project-conventions-5","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Scripts"},"979":{"id":"/project-conventions-6","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Templates"},"980":{"id":"/project-conventions-7","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Names and flags"},"981":{"id":"/project-conventions-8","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"},"982":{"id":"/project-conventions-9","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"},"983":{"id":"/project-conventions-10","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"},"984":{"id":"/project-conventions-11","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"},"985":{"id":"/project-conventions-12","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"Writing a script that passes `//:lint` and `//tests:doc_coverage` first try"},"986":{"id":"/project-conventions-13","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"},"987":{"id":"/project-conventions-14","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."},"988":{"id":"/project-conventions-15","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."},"989":{"id":"/project-conventions-16","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"},"990":{"id":"/project-conventions-17","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."},"991":{"id":"/project-conventions-18","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live). `tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is\nmissing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a `# @function` block is missing or incomplete.\nTests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."},"992":{"id":"/project-conventions-19","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`. On a laptop you do not run it:\nthe contributor container or `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned\ntoolchain in from `images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md). `--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run.\nOn Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."},"993":{"id":"/project-conventions-20","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target:"},"994":{"id":"/project-conventions-21","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."},"995":{"id":"/project-conventions-22","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."},"996":{"id":"/project-conventions-23","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`. Keep\n1–3 line awk, usage heredocs, Dockerfiles, YAML data, docs fences, and\n`python3 -m webbrowser`. Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."},"997":{"id":"/project-conventions-24","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping: `section` prints `== name ==`, `phase` prints `-> text`, `ok`\nprints `ok: text`, `summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed` plus\n`  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`. `JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr.\nCLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source. Keep prefixes `warning:` /\n`note:` / `error:`."},"998":{"id":"/project-conventions-25","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root from `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` and then\nprobes for it (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy."},"999":{"id":"/project-conventions-26","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."},"1000":{"id":"/project-conventions-27","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."},"1001":{"id":"/project-conventions-28","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `coder-agents.md`,\n`mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown and JSON are\nread with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must not\nre-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."},"1002":{"id":"/project-conventions-29","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers. A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$`\nas an escaped `$` everywhere (`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic\nalone. An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`;\na lone `$` is already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar.\n`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"1003":{"id":"/project-conventions-30","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."},"1004":{"id":"/project-conventions-31","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."},"1005":{"id":"/project-conventions-32","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."},"1006":{"id":"/project-conventions-33","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."},"1007":{"id":"/project-conventions-34","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."},"1008":{"id":"/project-conventions-35","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."},"1009":{"id":"/project-conventions-36","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."},"1010":{"id":"/public-access","page_id":"/public-access","type":"page","content":"Public subdomains","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access"},"1011":{"id":"/public-access-0","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Serving the same five services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."},"1012":{"id":"/public-access-1","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The path a public request takes"},"1013":{"id":"/public-access-2","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"The five names"},"1014":{"id":"/public-access-3","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"Getting the naming right"},"1015":{"id":"/public-access-4","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"},"1016":{"id":"/public-access-5","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Traefik configuration"},"1017":{"id":"/public-access-6","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Checking the other machine"},"1018":{"id":"/public-access-7","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Authelia"},"1019":{"id":"/public-access-8","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Two follow-ups, both by hand"},"1020":{"id":"/public-access-9","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"},"1021":{"id":"/public-access-10","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"The five names, and how to choose them at bootstrap time"},"1022":{"id":"/public-access-11","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"},"1023":{"id":"/public-access-12","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"},"1024":{"id":"/public-access-13","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"},"1025":{"id":"/public-access-14","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"},"1026":{"id":"/public-access-15","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"},"1027":{"id":"/public-access-16","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Putting the five routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"},"1028":{"id":"/public-access-17","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"The default shape is LAN-only. Setting `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` additionally serves the same\nfive services on public names over real TLS. It is purely additive: every LAN route\nkeeps working."},"1029":{"id":"/public-access-18","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."},"1030":{"id":"/public-access-19","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."},"1031":{"id":"/public-access-20","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."},"1032":{"id":"/public-access-21","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."},"1033":{"id":"/public-access-22","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."},"1034":{"id":"/public-access-23","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"A workspace **app** link (the **LiteLLM** and **Open VS Code Web** buttons) rides the\ncoder row of that table. Both `coder_app` blocks set `subdomain = false`, so their\nURL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path form and goes to `coder:3000`\non its way to `host.docker.internal:4000`. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure\nand `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took no part in it."},"1035":{"id":"/public-access-24","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."},"1036":{"id":"/public-access-25","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"| Name                      | `proxy/router.conf` upstream |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `homepage:3000`              |","table":"table-0"},"1037":{"id":"/public-access-26","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"| Name                            | `proxy/router.conf` upstream  |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - plain listener |","table":"table-0"},"1038":{"id":"/public-access-27","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"| Name                           | `proxy/router.conf` upstream                 |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `https://kasm:4443` (`proxy_ssl_verify off`) |","table":"table-0"},"1039":{"id":"/public-access-28","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"| Name                              | `proxy/router.conf` upstream |\n| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `litellm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `litellm:4000`               |","table":"table-0"},"1040":{"id":"/public-access-29","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"| Name                             | `proxy/router.conf` upstream |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `hermes:9119`                |","table":"table-0"},"1041":{"id":"/public-access-30","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-five-names","content":"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`. The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays.\nHTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays too; Authelia is the extra public gate.\nSee hermes.md."},"1042":{"id":"/public-access-31","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"Each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service prefix joins it with a\n**hyphen**."},"1043":{"id":"/public-access-32","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted two-label form\n(`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge: the browser aborts with\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the request. Measured on\nthis box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake with the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40 and presents\nno certificate at all."},"1044":{"id":"/public-access-33","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"},"1045":{"id":"/public-access-34","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"},"1046":{"id":"/public-access-35","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL`,\nthe card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills `STACK_HERMES_URL`.\nCompose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the Host and Origin\nthe Chat tab checks;"},"1047":{"id":"/public-access-36","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"},"1048":{"id":"/public-access-37","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"},"1049":{"id":"/public-access-38","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones."},"1050":{"id":"/public-access-39","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"Set the five names in `.env` (`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`; `.env.sample` lists them, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`), and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs\nbeside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap` and recreate the stack you switched. The\ncommands are in the README."},"1051":{"id":"/public-access-40","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart. The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the\nentryPoint name, the certificate and the middleware name have to match what that box\nalready defines, so treat those three as the values to align, not as new requirements."},"1052":{"id":"/public-access-41","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."},"1053":{"id":"/public-access-42","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The four interactive names carry it; LiteLLM must not."},"1054":{"id":"/public-access-43","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."},"1055":{"id":"/public-access-44","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."},"1056":{"id":"/public-access-45","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder and hermes 300s, LiteLLM 600s, Kasm 3600s - so\nTraefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all five at `3600s` is the\nsimple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier separately,\ndeclare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` at `300s`, `evo-t1-litellm` at `600s`,\n`evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a session\ndies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."},"1057":{"id":"/public-access-46","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."},"1058":{"id":"/public-access-47","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."},"1059":{"id":"/public-access-48","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend is `:8080` with the original Host,\nthe same as the other four - not `:9119`. A router aimed at `:9119` skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."},"1060":{"id":"/public-access-49","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."},"1061":{"id":"/public-access-50","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names. The sidecar forwards what it receives and\nauthenticates nothing, so a name whose router skips the auth middleware reaches\nits container the same way the LAN does. The app path is behind that policy\nlike the rest of its name, so an expired session surfaces there too."},"1062":{"id":"/public-access-51","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"DNS records for all five names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy."},"1063":{"id":"/public-access-52","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."},"1064":{"id":"/public-access-53","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and Hermes are interactive\nsessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected."},"1065":{"id":"/public-access-54","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."},"1066":{"id":"/public-access-55","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."},"1067":{"id":"/public-access-56","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."},"1068":{"id":"/public-access-57","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."},"1069":{"id":"/troubleshooting","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting"},"1070":{"id":"/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."},"1071":{"id":"/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#stack-bring-up","content":"Stack bring-up"},"1072":{"id":"/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"},"1073":{"id":"/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"},"1074":{"id":"/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"},"1075":{"id":"/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#model-lane-spark-litellm-the-agent-backends","content":"Model lane: Spark, LiteLLM, the agent backends"},"1076":{"id":"/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"How do I know the IPEX-LLM server came up? -> look for `runners=[ipex_llm]`"},"1077":{"id":"/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Grok agent mode makes no tool calls (it narrates and stops) -> an Arc alias served it"},"1078":{"id":"/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"Coder Agents chat refuses every prompt, or its model dropdown is empty -> no model config"},"1079":{"id":"/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"Workspace cannot reach LiteLLM -> `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, or `host.docker.internal`"},"1080":{"id":"/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"Search never answers (or answers empty) -> prove the search lane before blaming the MCP hop"},"1081":{"id":"/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"Context7 or GitHub tools missing in Grok Build -> parked MCP block, or a handshake with nothing to authenticate"},"1082":{"id":"/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A workspace app link answers `Bad Gateway` -> find who answered"},"1083":{"id":"/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspaces-startup-images-the-toolchain-inside-them","content":"Workspaces: startup, images, the toolchain inside them"},"1084":{"id":"/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-stuck-in-starting---startup-is-blocking","content":"Workspace stuck in *starting* -> startup is blocking"},"1085":{"id":"/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Image missing tools, or the build aborts mid-layer -> `curl: (22)`"},"1086":{"id":"/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-