e-router","content":"Public `hermes-gmktecbeast` answers `421` -> no vhost, a rewritten Host, or Traefik aimed past the router"},"1112":{"id":"/troubleshooting-42","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Chat tab never connects, or the socket closes `4403` -> public URL, trusted proxies, or a dropped Upgrade"},"1113":{"id":"/troubleshooting-43","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"Kasm wizard gone after install -> expected"},"1114":{"id":"/troubleshooting-44","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"Coder login problems -> the admin account, and `CODER_ACCESS_URL`"},"1115":{"id":"/troubleshooting-45","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"},"1116":{"id":"/troubleshooting-46","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"},"1117":{"id":"/troubleshooting-47","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Workspaces: startup, the golden image, Grok Build (`/learn`, MCP), the toolchain"},"1118":{"id":"/troubleshooting-48","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Docker-in-Docker, Terraform, and the host `coder` CLI"},"1119":{"id":"/troubleshooting-49","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Editor, credentials, the clone, UI logins, and the public names (`421`, Chat `4403`, Traefik)"},"1120":{"id":"/troubleshooting-50","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"},"1121":{"id":"/troubleshooting-51","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Knowing the cost of a fix before running it, including what it destroys"},"1122":{"id":"/troubleshooting-52","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Deep, symptom-first list. The operational `dev-stack`/`prod-stack`, `search-check` and\n`spark-verify` targets are described in README.md and\nproject-conventions.md."},"1123":{"id":"/troubleshooting-53","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Each fence below is one copy-paste action. A heading quotes the error string where there\nis one, so start with `grep -n` on the text you saw."},"1124":{"id":"/troubleshooting-54","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Placeholders: `<wsid>` is a workspace id and `<ws>` a workspace name. The workspace\ncontainer is `coder-<owner>-<ws>` (so `coder-alx-self-coder`); the `<wsid>` form names\nthe DinD pieces around it - the sidecar `coder-<wsid>-dind-sidecar`, on the network\n`coder-<wsid>-dind`, with its own `coder-<wsid>-dind-lib` volume. What runs \"from the\nstack host\" needs a shell there: inside a workspace `docker` addresses that workspace's\nown private daemon through `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375`, so a `docker inspect` aimed at\nthe stack's `coder` or `router` container and run from inside a workspace queries the\nwrong daemon and answers `no such container`. `bazelisk run //scripts:...` also runs\nfrom the checkout."},"1125":{"id":"/troubleshooting-55","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"Cause: `docker compose` resolved `./proxy/homepage.conf` (or another file bind\nmount) next to a compose file that did not have that file. Under `bazelisk run`\nthat file used to be the runfiles copy, which only holds `//:stack_data`. Docker\nthen created a directory at the source path and failed mounting it onto nginx's\n`/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf`, which is a file in the image."},"1126":{"id":"/troubleshooting-56","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"Fix: `homepage/**` and `proxy/*.conf` belong in `//:stack_data`, and the stack\nrunners prefer `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout) so relative\nmounts and bootstrap-issued `proxy/certs/` resolve there. Re-run from the stack\nhost:"},"1127":{"id":"/troubleshooting-57","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."},"1128":{"id":"/troubleshooting-58","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"A failed bind from an older runfiles compose file can also leave a **directory**\n(or a root-owned file) at `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` inside\n`bazel-out/.../prod-stack.runfiles`. The next `bazelisk run` then dies before\nthe stack script starts; that spelling is the next heading."},"1129":{"id":"/troubleshooting-59","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"Cause: an older `bazelisk run` used the runfiles copy of `docker-compose.yml`, so\ncompose mounted `./homepage/assets/icons` from `bazel-out`. Docker then created a\ndirectory where `evo-t1.svg` belongs, or the homepage container chowned those\nfiles to root. Bazel cannot recreate `prod-stack.runfiles` over that leftover,\nand the stack script never starts, so a repair inside `prod-stack` cannot help."},"1130":{"id":"/troubleshooting-60","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"Fix: recover from a shell, then re-run. `--repair` uses sudo when chmod is not\nenough:"},"1131":{"id":"/troubleshooting-61","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."},"1132":{"id":"/troubleshooting-62","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"Cause: `/etc/resolv.conf` names only Tailscale MagicDNS (`100.100.100.100`).\nDocker's iptables, and Kasm's privileged nested dockerd, can make that stub\nunreachable. When it is, **every** lookup dies, including `github.com`, so\n`git pull` over HTTPS fails. This is host DNS, not GitHub auth."},"1133":{"id":"/troubleshooting-63","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"On Linux, `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --repair` writes\n`/etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/evo-t1-public-dns.conf` (`DNS=1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8`),\nruns `sudo tailscale set --accept-dns=false`, and keeps MagicDNS for `ts.net`\nvia `resolvectl` split-DNS. `--check` still writes nothing."},"1134":{"id":"/troubleshooting-64","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"A checkout that does not yet contain that `--repair` (the usual case: `git pull`\nis already failing) needs the same commands by hand, then pull:"},"1135":{"id":"/troubleshooting-65","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves.\nOther ways to keep a public resolver: add `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` as a global\nnameserver in the Tailscale admin DNS page."},"1136":{"id":"/troubleshooting-66","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."},"1137":{"id":"/troubleshooting-67","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."},"1138":{"id":"/troubleshooting-68","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in its logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."},"1139":{"id":"/troubleshooting-69","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"},"1140":{"id":"/troubleshooting-70","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"},"1141":{"id":"/troubleshooting-71","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"Flags are accepted before or after `logs`; `--project-name` and `--tail` move either\nway. Only `logs` takes trailing service names, and here that trailing word is the\nservice to read."},"1142":{"id":"/troubleshooting-72","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Cause: no Spark box was reachable, so the Arc answered, and the Arc path returns tool\ncalls as plain text."},"1143":{"id":"/troubleshooting-73","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Look at the dashboard's **Agent backend** card first: its per-box widgets go to error\nstate while `agent` still answers."},"1144":{"id":"/troubleshooting-74","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Probe that endpoint (the card reads the same URL, `AGENT_MODEL_URL` in `.env`):"},"1145":{"id":"/troubleshooting-75","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Or check the whole fleet at once, from the checkout. `spark-verify.sh` takes `--json`\nand `--no-tests`; `--no-tests` skips the recipe's own probes (a 195k-token needle plus\na 1/2/4/5-client sweep, minutes per box) and leaves the quick read-only check:"},"1146":{"id":"/troubleshooting-76","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Its host list comes from the `SPARK*_URL` keys in `.env` (`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` and\n`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL`), which is why the default run needs no arguments. Export\n`SPARK_HOSTS=\"host1 host2 ...\"` in the process environment only to check a fleet that\n`.env` does not name. A host that does not answer is a skip, not a failure."},"1147":{"id":"/troubleshooting-77","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Fix, one of these:"},"1148":{"id":"/troubleshooting-78","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Bring the TensorFold server up on the box. `<host>` is what `.env` names, and `--apply`\nwrites the per-host override file that server runs:"},"1149":{"id":"/troubleshooting-79","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Or repoint `SPARK1_OPENAI_URL`, `SPARK2_OPENAI_URL` and `SPARK3_OPENAI_URL` in `.env`\nat a tool-capable OpenAI-compatible endpoint, then restart the proxy so it re-reads\nthem. The stack runners have no `restart` subcommand, so this one stays a direct\ncompose call:"},"1150":{"id":"/troubleshooting-80","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Either way keep `SPARK_n_API_KEY` non-empty: `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY`,\n`SPARK_3_API_KEY`. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy before it ever\nreaches the box."},"1151":{"id":"/troubleshooting-81","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."},"1152":{"id":"/troubleshooting-82","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"Cause: that chat has no model config on this deployment, so there is nothing to route to."},"1153":{"id":"/troubleshooting-83","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"First run this to see the plan, then run it again without the flag to apply it:"},"1154":{"id":"/troubleshooting-84","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"It skips while the coder or db container is down, or nobody has registered yet, and\nre-running it repairs a provider that drifted."},"1155":{"id":"/troubleshooting-85","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"A chat that starts but narrates instead of calling tools is the no-Spark case above."},"1156":{"id":"/troubleshooting-86","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"Two lanes reach that port and they share nothing but the destination, so a probe on one\nanswers nothing about the other."},"1157":{"id":"/troubleshooting-87","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."},"1158":{"id":"/troubleshooting-88","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The **app lane**: the **LiteLLM** button in the Coder dashboard, at\n`https://<coder name>/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/litellm`. `coder_app.litellm` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so that path rides the *coder* name and `coder:3000`, and\n`litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted. See the next heading."},"1159":{"id":"/troubleshooting-89","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The checks that apply to either lane:"},"1160":{"id":"/troubleshooting-90","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` must be an `https://` URL: a LAN IP, or the public coder name in\npublic mode. Never `localhost` and never `127.0.0.1`."},"1161":{"id":"/troubleshooting-91","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."},"1162":{"id":"/troubleshooting-92","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"Agent traffic ignores that URL and dials `host.docker.internal:3002`; see the\n`coder_agent_url` template variable."},"1163":{"id":"/troubleshooting-93","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"Search rides Grok's native `web_search` tool by default (`disable_web_search =\nfalse`). The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the fallback, declared by\ndefault (`search_mcp` defaults to true). Two probes split a SearXNG fault in half:"},"1164":{"id":"/troubleshooting-94","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`. It separates engines that\nanswered, engines that were unresponsive, and an empty result page."},"1165":{"id":"/troubleshooting-95","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"`grok mcp doctor searxng` inside the workspace probes the MCP hop. A healthy\nsearch-check plus a failed handshake points at the server, the URL or the port."},"1166":{"id":"/troubleshooting-96","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"If the SearXNG lane stays down, native search is already on. Park the failing\nserver with `search_mcp = false` on the next template push (`SEARCH_MCP=false` in\n`.env` applies it to every new workspace). To park native search instead, set\n`disable_web_search = true` in the workspace's `~/.grok/config.toml` and start a\nnew session."},"1167":{"id":"/troubleshooting-97","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default. Park one\nwith `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` / `SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` and\na template re-push. `grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared\nservers and their handshake."},"1168":{"id":"/troubleshooting-98","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A missing `[mcp_servers.github]` or `[mcp_servers.context7]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml` means the last push passed `--var …=false`, or the\nworkspace started from an older template. Re-push and restart the workspace\nso the managed `config.toml` re-renders."},"1169":{"id":"/troubleshooting-99","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A declared github server that fails handshake: the owner has not connected\nGitHub External Auth, so `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. Use the **GitHub reauth** app,\nthen start a new Grok session."},"1170":{"id":"/troubleshooting-100","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A declared context7 server that fails handshake: empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is\nthe supported public-rate-limit shape. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills\nthe env; the block still renders with no key."},"1171":{"id":"/troubleshooting-101","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"Discover live tool names with `search_tool`, then call `use_tool` with that\nschema. Do not hardcode MCP tool names from memory."},"1172":{"id":"/troubleshooting-102","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"The **LiteLLM** and **Open VS Code Web** buttons are Coder *apps*: their URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` on the coder hostname, because both `coder_app`\nblocks set `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane failure, and a 200\nfrom `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."},"1173":{"id":"/troubleshooting-103","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"},"1174":{"id":"/troubleshooting-104","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A hit in the first means Coder tried the upstream and gave up, so the fault sits on\nthis box: check the devcontainer or workspace container first, then `DOCKER_HOST`."},"1175":{"id":"/troubleshooting-105","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."},"1176":{"id":"/troubleshooting-106","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."},"1177":{"id":"/troubleshooting-107","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Neither header, and a `Server: cloudflare` with a `cf-ray` instead: Traefik or\nCloudflare answered, and there is nothing on this box to read."},"1178":{"id":"/troubleshooting-108","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."},"1179":{"id":"/troubleshooting-109","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."},"1180":{"id":"/troubleshooting-110","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`000` on the first probe means nothing listened on 4000. A `200 application/json`\nmeans LiteLLM answered, so the model lane is healthy and the app lane failed\nsomewhere else."},"1181":{"id":"/troubleshooting-111","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`000` on the second probe means the private lane is down as well, so the public\nfailure is not a cloud-side one. `421` means the Host header matched no\n`server_name` here, which is what a rewritten Host looks like."},"1182":{"id":"/troubleshooting-112","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A `404` is what this stack answers for an unknown app slug or an agent that is not\nConnected; the agent is the text after the first `.` in `<ws>.<agent>`."},"1183":{"id":"/troubleshooting-113","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."},"1184":{"id":"/troubleshooting-114","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-stuck-in-starting---startup-is-blocking","content":"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."},"1185":{"id":"/troubleshooting-115","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Cause: a pinned download returned an error. The layer prints `curl: (22)` and stops\nrather than shipping a partial image."},"1186":{"id":"/troubleshooting-116","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Fix: check that version in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` against the upstream\nrelease, then rebuild the image."},"1187":{"id":"/troubleshooting-117","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Cause: that container started before the CLI was baked into the image, or its\nstart-time download failed."},"1188":{"id":"/troubleshooting-118","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Both of these answer nothing in that case; the first is the one to type:"},"1189":{"id":"/troubleshooting-119","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."},"1190":{"id":"/troubleshooting-120","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"`/tmp/coder-startup-script.log` holds exactly one `curl: (28)` line when the\nstart-time download was what failed."},"1191":{"id":"/troubleshooting-121","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"One-off fix, from the stack host:"},"1192":{"id":"/troubleshooting-122","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."},"1193":{"id":"/troubleshooting-123","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Permanent fix: rebuild the image and the workspace, which then starts from the\nimage's own copy. See the next two entries for the command."},"1194":{"id":"/troubleshooting-124","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#learn-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-golden-image-still-pins-an-older-cli","content":"Cause: `/learn` is a bundled user-invocable skill of Grok Build 1.0.70, not a\nterminal slash-command this repo seeds. Workspaces still running the previous\npin (1.0.44) have no bundled learn, and a self-installed `~/.grok/bin/grok` that\nsits first on PATH hides the image's copy."},"1195":{"id":"/troubleshooting-125","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#learn-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-golden-image-still-pins-an-older-cli","content":"`1.0.70` (or later) at `/usr/local/bin/grok` is the image that ships `/learn`.\nRebuild `images/dev` after the pin bump (`scripts/build-dev-image.sh`), re-push\nthe templates, and start a new workspace (or restart one so the **Grok Build**\nterminal profile execs the new binary)."},"1196":{"id":"/troubleshooting-126","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#learn-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-golden-image-still-pins-an-older-cli","content":"`~/.grok/bin/grok` first on PATH is a leftover installer copy: move it aside\nso `/usr/local/bin/grok` wins."},"1197":{"id":"/troubleshooting-127","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#learn-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-golden-image-still-pins-an-older-cli","content":"Pin notes: grok-build-and-agents.md."},"1198":{"id":"/troubleshooting-128","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#docker---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---an-older-image-or-the-wrong-preset","content":"Cause: that container was built from an image predating the Docker client toolchain,\nor from the bare-Ubuntu *Minimal shell* preset."},"1199":{"id":"/troubleshooting-129","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#docker---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---an-older-image-or-the-wrong-preset","content":"Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace; the image is fixed at create time."},"1200":{"id":"/troubleshooting-130","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#permission-denied--jq-error-could-not-open-file-on-sharedvscodesettingsjson---empty-named-volume-is-root-owned","content":"Cause: Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root` 755. The\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume is empty on first use (the image has no\nfiles there to copy in), so `coder` cannot write `settings.json`. `jq` and\n`python` then fail because the file was never created. `already cloned, skipping`\non the next line is informational: the clone already exists."},"1201":{"id":"/troubleshooting-131","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#permission-denied--jq-error-could-not-open-file-on-sharedvscodesettingsjson---empty-named-volume-is-root-owned","content":"Fix: the template chowns that volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume)\nto the home owner before the seed, and skips `jq`/`python` when the write\nfails. Push the templates and restart the workspace; there is nothing to delete."},"1202":{"id":"/troubleshooting-132","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-profile-missing-in-a-fresh-workspace---startup-already-finished-so-read-the-log","content":"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."},"1203":{"id":"/troubleshooting-133","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-profile-missing-in-a-fresh-workspace---startup-already-finished-so-read-the-log","content":"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."},"1204":{"id":"/troubleshooting-134","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"Cause: the sidecar is unhealthy, its image was never pulled, or the workspace was\ncreated with DinD off."},"1205":{"id":"/troubleshooting-135","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"`DOCKER_HOST` is injected per template, not per toggle. `templates/workspace` sets\nit only while the toggle is on; `templates/devcontainer` sets it always, because\nthat template always provisions the sidecar and the `devcontainer` CLI has to\nreach its daemon either way. So a set `DOCKER_HOST` tells you the template; the\n*docker-in-docker* metadata tile tells you the state (the devcontainer template\nalways reads `on (tcp://dind:2375)`, the workspace one reads `off` when unset)."},"1206":{"id":"/troubleshooting-136","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"},"1207":{"id":"/troubleshooting-137","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."},"1208":{"id":"/troubleshooting-138","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"Cause: that image is not in the host's local image store, and Terraform never pulls\nit. The `docker_image` data source only inspects the store."},"1209":{"id":"/troubleshooting-139","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."},"1210":{"id":"/troubleshooting-140","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"Fix with the `docker pull` above, or turn DinD off for that workspace."},"1211":{"id":"/troubleshooting-141","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."},"1212":{"id":"/troubleshooting-142","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Cause: provisionerd runs `terraform init` inside the `coder` container.\nDocker's embedded DNS (`127.0.0.11`) forwards names it does not know to\nthe host resolver. When that resolver is Tailscale MagicDNS only, or\nDocker/Kasm iptables has made `100.100.100.100` unreachable from a\ncontainer, every public lookup SERVFAILs. The host CLI is fine; only the\nprovisioner job dies. The lockfile reuse lines (`Reusing previous version\nfrom the dependency lock file`) do not skip the registry: Terraform still\nfetches the discovery document."},"1213":{"id":"/troubleshooting-143","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Fix: `docker-compose.yml` sets `dns: [1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8]` on the `coder`\nservice (embedded DNS still answers `db` and other compose names). Both\ntemplates pin the same resolvers on the workspace and DinD containers so\na later `git clone` of `github.com` does not hit the same SERVFAIL. Recreate\n`coder` so the new upstreams apply, then push:"},"1214":{"id":"/troubleshooting-144","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Host `git pull` still needs a public resolver beside MagicDNS when the host\nitself is MagicDNS-only (heading above; `setup-host --repair` on Linux).\nExisting workspaces pick up the container DNS on the next start after this\ntemplate version is active."},"1215":{"id":"/troubleshooting-145","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."},"1216":{"id":"/troubleshooting-146","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Cause: both templates inline `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` URL on `coder_app.code_server`. Coder v2.36 stores\n`workspace_apps.icon` as `varchar(256)`. A multi-kilobyte SVG produces a data\nURL thousands of characters long, and the insert fails. The provisioner\nreports it at `insert agent app: upsert app`."},"1217":{"id":"/troubleshooting-147","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Fix: the SVG is a compact ribbon whose data URL fits the column, and\n`push-template` rejects a too-large copy locally before talking to the server.\nPull this commit onto the stack host and re-run:"},"1218":{"id":"/troubleshooting-148","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."},"1219":{"id":"/troubleshooting-149","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Cause: `bazelisk run` executes from the runfiles tree, where `templates/**` are\nsymlinks into the execroot. A tar that stores those links unpacks in the\nprovisioner as dangling paths, so Terraform sees no `.tf` files. The two\n`templates/common/` payloads still land (copied with `cp`, which follows\nlinks), which is why the archive is small rather than empty."},"1220":{"id":"/troubleshooting-150","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Fix: `push-template` now prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the live\ncheckout) and stages with `tar -h` so the archive holds regular files. Pull\nthis commit onto the stack host and re-run:"},"1221":{"id":"/troubleshooting-151","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#terraformlockhcl-is-missing---a-note-not-a-failure","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."},"1222":{"id":"/troubleshooting-152","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#terraformlockhcl-is-missing---a-note-not-a-failure","content":"Pin provider versions locally only if you want to, per template directory. The push\ngoes ahead either way, and `workspace` and `devcontainer` are the two names:"},"1223":{"id":"/troubleshooting-153","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"},"1224":{"id":"/troubleshooting-154","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"Cause: the CLI is talking to a public subdomain, and Authelia answers its API calls\nwith the sign-in page."},"1225":{"id":"/troubleshooting-155","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."},"1226":{"id":"/troubleshooting-156","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"Fix: point the CLI at the container's loopback listener, `http://127.0.0.1:3000`,\nwhich nothing can gate. See \"Coder workspace templates\" in README.md."},"1227":{"id":"/troubleshooting-157","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` itself stays the public name: browsers need it, and only CLI\ntraffic should not use it."},"1228":{"id":"/troubleshooting-158","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"Cause: an upstream pin. The Coder image ships its own provisioner binary at\n`/usr/local/bin/terraform` (1.15.5 in `coder:v2.36.6`) and Coder caps the version it\nwas tested against."},"1229":{"id":"/troubleshooting-159","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"`coder server` logs it; the host `coder` CLI does not."},"1230":{"id":"/troubleshooting-160","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"Builds still succeed, so verify with a workspace build before acting on it. Nothing\nin this repo's compose file sets the provisioner's Terraform."},"1231":{"id":"/troubleshooting-161","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpanded-count-argument-on-block-coder_appcoder_ui---apps-were-gated-on-start_count","content":"Cause: `coder/preview` warns when a `coder_app` `count` is not expanded at\nworkspace-create preview. `start_count` is that value. The default is then\nassumed to be 1."},"1232":{"id":"/troubleshooting-162","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpanded-count-argument-on-block-coder_appcoder_ui---apps-were-gated-on-start_count","content":"Fix: `coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count` (scripts, metadata\nand the container still are). Push the templates; new creates stop showing the\nwarning. Existing workspaces pick it up on the next update to the new template\nversion."},"1233":{"id":"/troubleshooting-163","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#bash--c-line-32-syntax-error-near-unexpected-token--after-running-code-server-in-the-background---hcl--in-the-editor-wait-loop","content":"Cause: Coder runs every `coder_script` as `bash -c`. HCL heredocs in `main.tf` only\nescape `$${name}` to `${name}`; `$$((` is two literal dollars. The editor wait loop\nthen becomes `waited=<pid>((waited + 1))`, which is the syntax error. The echo\nbefore the loop still prints. `already cloned, skipping` on the next line is the\nconcurrent clone stage, not the failure."},"1234":{"id":"/troubleshooting-164","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#bash--c-line-32-syntax-error-near-unexpected-token--after-running-code-server-in-the-background---hcl--in-the-editor-wait-loop","content":"Fix: the increment is `waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar). Push the templates and\nrestart so the editor script relaunches."},"1235":{"id":"/troubleshooting-165","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-opens-homecoder-instead-of-the-cloned-repository---last-workspace-won","content":"Cause: path-based Coder apps drop the `?folder=` query before the browser sees\nit, and code-server restores the last opened workspace (often `/home/coder`).\nThe clone itself is fine (`already cloned, skipping` is not a failure)."},"1236":{"id":"/troubleshooting-166","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-opens-homecoder-instead-of-the-cloned-repository---last-workspace-won","content":"Fix: the inlined fork starts code-server with `--ignore-last-opened` and the\nclone folder as a positional argument (`/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` on the\n`workspace` template). Push the templates and restart so the editor process\nrelaunches."},"1237":{"id":"/troubleshooting-167","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 1: the code-server install script's log is green in the workspace's Scripts/Log\ntab."},"1238":{"id":"/troubleshooting-168","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 2: this answers from inside the workspace:"},"1239":{"id":"/troubleshooting-169","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 3: the browser is on `https://` with a trusted cert. Plain `http://` is an\ninsecure context, which is what breaks `crypto.randomUUID()`; see \"Public subdomains\n(optional)\" in README.md."},"1240":{"id":"/troubleshooting-170","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"On `devcontainer`:"},"1241":{"id":"/troubleshooting-171","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."},"1242":{"id":"/troubleshooting-172","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"A repo that ships none wants the **Plain clone** preset, or the dev-container toggle\nset to **No** at create time. Either one runs code-server on the plain workspace\ncontainer and opens the clone there."},"1243":{"id":"/troubleshooting-173","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"},"1244":{"id":"/troubleshooting-174","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"On the workspace page, click **GitHub reauth**. Coder opens `/external-auth/<id>`\n(device flow for the injected App, OAuth for a custom app in `coder.env`). If the\npage says you are already authenticated, click **Reauthenticate**."},"1245":{"id":"/troubleshooting-175","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"If that page says \"You must install the GitHub App to clone private repositories\"\nand lists no accounts, the device-flow login already succeeded. Click **Install\nGitHub App** on the workspace (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant the private repos, then restart. Coder's injected provider omits\n`AppInstallURL`, so the warning on `/external-auth/github` is unlinked plaintext."},"1246":{"id":"/troubleshooting-176","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"Restart the workspace afterwards so `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new\ngrant. `git` via `GIT_ASKPASS` remints without that restart."},"1247":{"id":"/troubleshooting-177","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"Existing workspaces pick up the buttons after `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`\nand a workspace update to the new template version."},"1248":{"id":"/troubleshooting-178","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"The refresh script inherits `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` from the agent. `gh auth\nlogin --with-token` refuses stdin while those names are set. Current templates\nunset the pair, log out, then log in with `--git-protocol https`, and print gh's\nstderr (token redacted) if that still fails."},"1249":{"id":"/troubleshooting-179","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"Inside the workspace, run `GH_TOKEN= GITHUB_TOKEN= gh auth status`. Then click\n**GitHub reauth**, finish device-code login, install the Coder GitHub App if the\nreauth page says to, and restart."},"1250":{"id":"/troubleshooting-180","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The denial is about the **grant**, not the credential format. A\nconnected-but-unscoped credential authenticates and is still refused: 403, or 404\nwhere GitHub hides a private repo's existence (a repo hidden from the credential\nanswers 404, or 403, on the read lane and the write lane alike). And reads prove\nnothing about push: a public repo answers read probes for any authenticated\ncaller, so a credential that READS a repo can still be refused every WRITE to it."},"1251":{"id":"/troubleshooting-181","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Check the GitHub side first. The grant behind the credential - the GitHub App\ninstallation that the External Auth provider (id `github` by default) minted its token from -\nhas to include the repository the workspace clones or pushes. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is the usual surprise\nhere: it means no App is installed under your account, so there is no **Configure** link\nto click and nothing to grant, while the provider itself stays configured and keeps\nminting tokens that authenticate. Install Coder's App from the workspace **Install\nGitHub App** button, or at\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new,\ngrant the repo, then restart. A private repository is never visible to a credential\nthat was never granted it."},"1252":{"id":"/troubleshooting-182","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Then check the URL scheme. That credential covers `https://github.com/...` remotes\nonly, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets neither helper nor askpass token\nand needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this stack provisions nowhere."},"1253":{"id":"/troubleshooting-183","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The doctor, from a host shell or from inside a workspace, names the credentials a\nworkspace's git and gh could present and answers \"can this credential WRITE to the repo you\npass?\". It probes the endpoint `git push` negotiates through: one GET of\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`. A REFUSED-GRANT answer means the\ncredential authenticates while every write to that repo is denied; NOT VISIBLE\nmeans the repo is hidden from the credential (or absent); REJECTED means github.com\nrefuses the credential outright, as it does an expired mint:"},"1254":{"id":"/troubleshooting-184","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"On the host it enumerates the labeled workspace containers with docker and probes\neach (a named container checks just that one). Inside a workspace the docker CLI\nreaches only that workspace's DinD sidecar, so the same command runs SELF mode\nwith no docker: one probe of this environment's own credential chain, printed as\none `self:` line per credential. Pass only the repo URL there; a named container is\nrefused and pointed at the host shell. The repo URL may be the clone URL or the\nbrowser URL, with or without a trailing slash and `.git`; a path below `owner/repo`\nis refused rather than probed, because it names no repository. Full usage:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- --help`."},"1255":{"id":"/troubleshooting-185","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and\nlabels the answer **shadowed** when the env pair outranks it. That label matters:\nwhile `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` carry a token, git and gh consult that token and\nnever reach the gh auth store, so a PAT you pasted into the store is not the\ncredential a push would use. To check or use the stored credential anyway, clear\nthe pair for that one command:"},"1256":{"id":"/troubleshooting-186","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Or install a write-capable PAT into one workspace's gh auth store, on stdin:"},"1257":{"id":"/troubleshooting-187","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"A manual PAT is legitimate in two cases: the workspace owner connected no Coder\naccount, so nothing can be minted, or the minted/App credential is missing the\nrepository - revoked or expired counts the same way, because the answer that proves\neither is NOT VISIBLE or REJECTED, not a passing read. It REPLACES the per-start\nminted credential for that workspace, so treat it as a stand-in and rotate or remove\nit in GitHub -> Applications when the work ends."},"1258":{"id":"/troubleshooting-188","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"A PAT is never typed as an argument any more: argv is readable through `ps` and the\ntoken lands in shell history. Pipe it in (`echo \"$TOKEN\" | bazelisk run\n//scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> -`) or run `auth <container>` alone and\nanswer the hidden prompt. In the line above, `-` is the only argument after the\ncontainer and `< PAT` is a shell redirect, not an argument."},"1259":{"id":"/troubleshooting-189","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Cause: Hermes v0.22.5 refuses a non-loopback bind when no\n`DashboardAuthProvider` is registered. The bundled basic-auth plugin skips\nregistration when the password is empty. Compose interpolation\n`${HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD:-}` (empty default) did that after a pull that\nadded the Hermes keys: `.env` never gained them, so `prod-stack up` without\n`bootstrap` / `update-configs` crash-looped the gateway. The logs repeat\n\"no auth providers are registered\" and do not mention `plugins.disabled`."},"1260":{"id":"/troubleshooting-190","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Fix: compose interpolation defaults now match `.env.sample`\n(`change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). Recreate the service:"},"1261":{"id":"/troubleshooting-191","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Sign-in is `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.env`\n(default `admin` / `change-me-hermes` until bootstrap rotates the\nplaceholders). To fill missing keys and replace those placeholders:"},"1262":{"id":"/troubleshooting-192","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a no-op. Logs:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs hermes`."},"1263":{"id":"/troubleshooting-193","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."},"1264":{"id":"/troubleshooting-194","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Cause: this box's `router` answers `421` when the Host matches no `server_name`.\nThe hermes vhost lives in `proxy/router.conf` (hardcoded, like the other public\nnames). Traefik pointed at `:9119` never hits that file. A rewritten Host also\nmisses every block."},"1265":{"id":"/troubleshooting-195","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"},"1266":{"id":"/troubleshooting-196","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"`421` means this box has no vhost for that Host. Recreate `router` so it\nmounts the conf that lists `server_name hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`."},"1267":{"id":"/troubleshooting-197","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Anything else means the router matched (Hermes basic auth is `401` until you\nsend credentials). A `421` only on the public URL, with this probe succeeding,\nis a rewritten Host or a Traefik backend that is not `:8080`."},"1268":{"id":"/troubleshooting-198","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from\n`proxy/router.conf`."},"1269":{"id":"/troubleshooting-199","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."},"1270":{"id":"/troubleshooting-200","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Cause: the Chat tab is a WebSocket on `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`. Hermes sends a\nkeepalive every 20 seconds. Close code `4403` is the Host / Origin / peer guard."},"1271":{"id":"/troubleshooting-201","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` has to be the public origin. Bootstrap writes\n`STACK_HERMES_URL` from a `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry, and compose copies\nthat into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`. The variable is environment: recreate\nthe container after bootstrap."},"1272":{"id":"/troubleshooting-202","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"},"1273":{"id":"/troubleshooting-203","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"`hermes config set` on `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is the in-container\nalternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` does not need that reseed."},"1274":{"id":"/troubleshooting-204","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."},"1275":{"id":"/troubleshooting-205","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."},"1276":{"id":"/troubleshooting-206","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."},"1277":{"id":"/troubleshooting-207","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"Reset Kasm by removing the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config. Stop\nthe stack first, and use the name your stack owns: `evo-t1-coder-stack_kasm-data` for\nthe stable stack, `evo-t1-dev_kasm-data` for the dev one."},"1278":{"id":"/troubleshooting-208","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"The first registered account is the site admin."},"1279":{"id":"/troubleshooting-209","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"If the UI is unreachable, check that `CODER_ACCESS_URL` matches the address you are\nbrowsing from: the LAN IP by default, or the public coder name in public mode."}},"count":1279},"sorting":{"language":"multilingual","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":463,"2":1182,"3":763,"4":1267,"5":513,"6":901,"7":1206,"8":680,"9":1243,"10":1216,"11":780,"12":749,"13":1183,"14":167,"15":797,"16":548,"17":137,"18":776,"19":450,"20":514,"21":462,"22":595,"23":351,"24":529,"25":555,"26":1234,"27":1000,"28":853,"29":676,"30":1223,"31":434,"32":1245,"33":546,"34":54,"35":747,"36":1113,"37":846,"38":544,"39":955,"40":440,"41":567,"42":994,"43":921,"44":1256,"45":1147,"46":517,"47":1224,"48":642,"49":1222,"50":698,"51":1233,"52":589,"53":1057,"54":586,"55":959,"56":518,"57":655,"58":199,"59":1235,"60":675,"61":599,"62":442,"63":961,"64":512,"65":868,"66":497,"67":554,"68":880,"69":1193,"70":250,"71":118,"72":252,"73":121,"74":579,"75":227,"76":143,"77":246,"78":174,"79":241,"80":146,"81":170,"82":139,"83":585,"84":854,"85":255,"86":122,"87":852,"88":92,"89":723,"90":724,"91":841,"92":721,"93":722,"94":729,"95":706,"96":720,"97":719,"98":786,"99":232,"100":226,"101":459,"102":370,"103":401,"104":790,"105":435,"106":389,"107":711,"108":605,"109":974,"110":99,"111":733,"112":761,"113":725,"114":792,"115":1114,"116":898,"117":1135,"118":736,"119":954,"120":387,"121":1130,"122":1161,"123":794,"124":308,"125":299,"