242,"1103":84,"1104":1202,"1105":467,"1106":657,"1107":650,"1108":855,"1109":1196,"1110":189,"1111":859,"1112":502,"1113":740,"1114":526,"1115":939,"1116":1072,"1117":1276,"1118":569,"1119":588,"1120":944,"1121":748,"1122":558,"1123":574,"1124":848,"1125":476,"1126":626,"1127":1110,"1128":369,"1129":478,"1130":631,"1131":90,"1132":472,"1133":806,"1134":361,"1135":42,"1136":1111,"1137":998,"1138":194,"1139":937,"1140":1109,"1141":638,"1142":484,"1143":760,"1144":856,"1145":836,"1146":734,"1147":623,"1148":464,"1149":838,"1150":592,"1151":987,"1152":487,"1153":621,"1154":727,"1155":359,"1156":1190,"1157":982,"1158":979,"1159":1014,"1160":103,"1161":1151,"1162":419,"1163":908,"1164":93,"1165":142,"1166":685,"1167":1133,"1168":376,"1169":367,"1170":366,"1171":563,"1172":981,"1173":1188,"1174":373,"1175":256,"1176":257,"1177":782,"1178":1175,"1179":46,"1180":71,"1181":72,"1182":349,"1183":927,"1184":219,"1185":477,"1186":628,"1187":488,"1188":458,"1189":1112,"1190":62,"1191":828,"1192":1157,"1193":842,"1194":473,"1195":73,"1196":69,"1197":843,"1198":489,"1199":873,"1200":482,"1201":635,"1202":220,"1203":1160,"1204":493,"1205":129,"1206":1172,"1207":977,"1208":490,"1209":932,"1210":622,"1211":948,"1212":486,"1213":625,"1214":673,"1215":949,"1216":480,"1217":634,"1218":950,"1219":474,"1220":627,"1221":91,"1222":844,"1223":1148,"1224":492,"1225":1166,"1226":630,"1227":102,"1228":479,"1229":100,"1230":470,"1231":475,"1232":624,"1233":481,"1234":632,"1235":485,"1236":633,"1237":503,"1238":504,"1239":505,"1240":802,"1241":1031,"1242":390,"1243":89,"1244":808,"1245":684,"1246":887,"1247":610,"1248":1102,"1249":699,"1250":1030,"1251":509,"1252":1152,"1253":1037,"1254":807,"1255":49,"1256":837,"1257":374,"1258":380,"1259":483,"1260":629,"1261":924,"1262":153,"1263":1013,"1264":494,"1265":1099,"1266":74,"1267":430,"1268":216,"1269":1089,"1270":491,"1271":154,"1272":1117,"1273":150,"1274":1178,"1275":986,"1276":1149,"1277":885,"1278":1044,"1279":686},"orderedDocs":[[650,"**`/learn` is a bundled skill of this CLI**, not something the terminal adds: 1.0.70\nships it in its bundled manifest (`public-2026-10-20-r1`) as a user-invocable skill,\nand the pin this image carried before did not include it. Once the image is rebuilt\nand the templates re-pushed it shows up in the Grok Build slash menu, in the\ncode-server web terminal profile named **Grok Build** included, because that\nprofile execs the same `/usr/local/bin/grok` binary. Nothing to install and nothing\nto seed: an older CLI in `~/.grok/bin` is the one case that still hides it."],[537,"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."],[536,"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."],[359,"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop. Do not set\nonly `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_APP_INSTALL_URL` either: it suppresses the injected\nApp like any other key in the set. A custom GitHub App belongs in `coder.env`\nfully filled, including\n`APP_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new`."],[422,"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."],[577,"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."],[184,"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"],[221,"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."],[263,"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind). The\nscript creates that folder first (`mkdir -p` plus a chown to the workspace user) so\nthe editor's wait loop can see it, then clones into it. This is idempotent, runs as\nthe workspace user so their git credentials are consulted, and refuses to touch an\nexisting non-empty non-git folder there. An empty pre-created target still clones."],[361,"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."],[366,"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints.\nThe grant must include the repos you clone, or a connected credential authenticates\nand is still 403."],[157,"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."],[576,"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."],[159,"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."],[158,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."],[220,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."],[640,"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root."],[162,"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is only observably per-workspace while the\nprofile mode is `private`: one shared profile is one file for every workspace that\nowner owns, and the last boot to re-render it wins."],[161,"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."],[160,"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`).\n`private` gives that one workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume, so one\nworkspace can sit outside the shared profile while its siblings stay inside it.\nSwitching needs a rebuild."],[421,"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script probes for its repo root instead of trusting the cwd."],[367,"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and the template's `gh_token_refresh` coder_script\nre-mints that pair on start and every 6 hours (`0 0 */6 * * *`) because a minted\ntoken expires first. Check it with `gh auth status` inside the workspace, or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check` for one line per credential slot.\ncoder-templates.md owns\nthe template-side detail."],[729,"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."],[360,"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."],[423,"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs. CI docker-runs the\ngate with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)`, so those Bazel caches are per-job and Bazel\ncan name a uid the image passwd file does not list; the cache that survives there is\nthe image's buildx GHA cache. No remote cache is configured — the same two flags\nwould take a `--remote_cache=<uri>` endpoint later."],[673,"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server (the fallback search lane).** Each is a boolean gate in the\ntemplate, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies it\nfleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`.\nThe `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`.\nThe five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."],[368,"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment. That\npath is gone: there is no such template variable and no such key in the twin. A\nworkspace's GitHub credential comes from the External Auth provider alone, so an\nowner who connected nothing has no GitHub credential anywhere - unless they install\none per workspace:"],[494,"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."],[493,"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."],[492,"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."],[491,"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."],[535,"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."],[730,"**Public name, opt-in:** `https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` when that name is\nlisted in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`. Bootstrap classifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and\nwrites `STACK_HERMES_URL`. The path is the same as the other public names: Cloudflare\n(orange cloud, WebSockets on by default), Traefik on the other LAN server (TLS\n`*.overeazy.io`, Authelia), the `router` on this box `:8080`, then\n`http://hermes:9119`, with the original Host preserved. Traefik's backend is\n`:8080`, not `:9119`. See public-access.md."],[337,"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two\nsteps: `${VAR}` inside `expose` expands during the shell phase, so compose\nnever sees those variables and a changed value needs a rebuild, and an unset\nvariable collapses to `127.0.0.1:57620:57620/tcp`, which fails to bind. So the\nhost ports and addresses live in a key=value env file - `.env` for the stable\nstack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and each has a checked-in twin beside\nit that carries the keys and explains them in the comment lines above. A pull\nthat renames, adds or retires a key silently changes what the stack runs on."],[338,"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.grok/config.toml`,\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder` and\n`~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots and\nare never revisited after that. A re-pushed template only reaches workspaces\nthat have been **start**ed or **restart**ed since the push - **stop** and\n**start** is what re-runs the provisioning script and therefore re-runs\n`before_config`."],[1056,"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder and hermes 300s, LiteLLM 600s, Kasm 3600s - so\nTraefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all five at `3600s` is the\nsimple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier separately,\ndeclare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` at `300s`, `evo-t1-litellm` at `600s`,\n`evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a session\ndies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."],[232,"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."],[1055,"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."],[156,"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."],[187,"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."],[293,"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed. It joins the list into one `additional_repos`\nparameter, so `\"a , b\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both arrive in the template as `a,b`."],[1007,"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."],[1135,"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves.\nOther ways to keep a public resolver: add `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` as a global\nnameserver in the Tailscale admin DNS page."],[246,"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."],[299,"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."],[352,"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."],[1179,"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."],[1006,"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."],[410,"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point. On Linux it also installs public\nDNS so `github.com` still resolves when Tailscale MagicDNS is the only\nnameserver (`Could not resolve host: github.com` after a compose `up`). `--check`\nreports that lookup and writes nothing."],[1255,"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and\nlabels the answer **shadowed** when the env pair outranks it. That label matters:\nwhile `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` carry a token, git and gh consult that token and\nnever reach the gh auth store, so a PAT you pasted into the store is not the\ncredential a push would use. To check or use the stored credential anyway, clear\nthe pair for that one command:"],[686,"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0.\n`SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap and stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."],[487,"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file. It is tracked in this\nrepository, and `probe_repo` (in `scripts/lib/workspace.sh`) treats a clone that tracks it\nas a **miss** — exit `3` — even when the same clone also tracks\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`."],[453,"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"],[358,"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."],[34,"`.env` keys it reads"],[482,"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit: `//:test-fast`, `//:lint`, `//:validate`, and the `docs-site/` Node checks."],[1098,"`.terraform.lock.hcl is missing` -> a note, not a failure"],[652,"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."],[654,"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Under a shared profile the\nlast-booted workspace's key wins for the whole fleet."],[755,"`/home` is **not** mounted in v1. Hermes cannot see your dotfiles, your SSH keys or\nyour shell history, and it should not need to."],[1087,"`/learn` missing from Grok Build on code-server web -> the golden image still pins an older CLI"],[750,"`/opt/data` is a **named volume on purpose**. Hermes keeps a SQLite database there in\nWAL mode, and WAL on a virtiofs bind mount is not safe. Bind-mounting that path is how\nyou corrupt the assistant's own memory."],[1190,"`/tmp/coder-startup-script.log` holds exactly one `curl: (28)` line when the\nstart-time download was what failed."],[762,"`<mount>` is the host directory of the Hermes data volume (what compose mounts at\n`/opt/data`). `--force` is how an existing volume picks up the seeded\n`dashboard.trusted_proxies`. Setting that key with `hermes config set` is the\nin-container alternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is not in the volume;\nrecreate the container and the env applies."],[698,"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"],[699,"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[696,"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"],[697,"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[310,"`~/.gitconfig` seeding reads that environment: an idempotent\n`credential.https://github.com.helper = !gh auth git-credential` entry (scoped to\n`github.com`) lets `git push`, `gh pr create` and `gh api` authenticate from the minted\ntoken rather than from a token stored in a file. It is skipped when your `gitconfig`\nalready names a helper, because git takes the **last** value it finds."],[1196,"`~/.grok/bin/grok` first on PATH is a leftover installer copy: move it aside\nso `/usr/local/bin/grok` wins."],[356,"`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is the one rendered target it does reseed, and\nit re-*renders* rather than copies: `${litellm_url}` and the `$${...}`\ndollar-brace unfoldings are rewritten, and that workspace's own `cline.apiKey`\nand `roo.openaiApiKey` are carried forward."],[1180,"`000` on the first probe means nothing listened on 4000. A `200 application/json`\nmeans LiteLLM answered, so the model lane is healthy and the app lane failed\nsomewhere else."],[1181,"`000` on the second probe means the private lane is down as well, so the public\nfailure is not a cloud-side one. `421` means the Host header matched no\n`server_name` here, which is what a rewritten Host looks like."],[1195,"`1.0.70` (or later) at `/usr/local/bin/grok` is the image that ships `/learn`.\nRebuild `images/dev` after the pin bump (`scripts/build-dev-image.sh`), re-push\nthe templates, and start a new workspace (or restart one so the **Grok Build**\nterminal profile execs the new binary)."],[1266,"`421` means this box has no vhost for that Host. Recreate `router` so it\nmounts the conf that lists `server_name hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`."],[1005,"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."],[387,"`AGENT_MODEL_URL` is the single `/v1/models` URL the \"Agent backend\" card uses\nfor both its browser link and its status probe. All three boxes serve the same\nmodel id, so point it at any reachable one; the per-box health keys are what tell\nyou which box answered."],[739,"`agent` also thinks hard by default: it bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`.\n**`agent-low`**, **`agent-medium`** and **`agent-xhigh`** are the thinking variants of\nit - the same Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on the same three Spark boxes, with real\n`tool_calls` and that level baked into the alias instead. Use one when a run needs a\ndifferent level; Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and `xhigh` and nothing else."],[880,"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."],[341,"`all` runs `env` and then `workspace`."],[866,"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."],[313,"`auth <container> - < PAT` installs a write-capable PAT into that workspace's gh auth\nstore. That is legitimate in two cases: the owner connected no Coder account, so\nnothing can be minted, or the minted (or GitHub App) credential is revoked, expired,\nor simply was never granted the repository you are pointed at."],[1053,"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The four interactive names carry it; LiteLLM must not."],[867,"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 30` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 5`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot."],[1103,"`bash: -c: line 32: syntax error near unexpected token '('` after \"Running code-server in the background\" -> HCL `$$((` in the editor wait loop"],[531,"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."],[1044,"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"],[449,"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."],[233,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."],[1243,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"],[1131,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."],[1221,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."],[88,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."],[1164,"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`. It separates engines that\nanswered, engines that were unresponsive, and an empty result page."],[378,"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` from the prefix on\neach entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` / `hermes-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. Compose sets\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the Chat tab's Host and\nOrigin)."],[1092,"`Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at tcp://dind:2375` -> the sidecar, or DinD off"],[206,"`cgroupns_mode = \"private\"` and `shm_size = 1024` (MB to the provider, so 1 GiB) are\nboth load-bearing; the sidecar is torn down with the workspace because it is gated on\n`start_count` as well as on the toggle."],[742,"`chat` - `qwen2.5:7b` on the Arc, and it returns real `tool_calls`. The reliable\ndegrade path when every Spark box is down."],[312,"`check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` names the credentials a workspace's\ngit and gh could present and answers \"can this credential WRITE to the repository you\npoint it at?\", by probing the receive-pack ref advertisement that endpoint `git push`\nnegotiates through (full usage:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- --help`). On the host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers with docker, or probes a named container\nalone. Inside a workspace it runs SELF mode with no docker: this environment's own\ncredential chain, one `self:` line per credential, and a named container there is\nrefused with a pointer to the host. `--source=chain` (the default) answers every\ncredential that could cover that repository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or\n`--source=mint` answers one slot and marks the answer shadowed while the env pair\nstands, because git and gh consult that pair first and never reach the store.\n`GH_TOKEN= GITHUB_TOKEN= <command>` is how you make them use the stored credential."],[110,"`coder login` on the host must reach the API *before* it has a credential, and in\npublic mode the only URL it will use is `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, which Authelia gates."],[1229,"`coder server` logs it; the host `coder` CLI does not."],[149,"`coder templates` is the canonical name; `template` is its alias."],[1227,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` itself stays the public name: browsers need it, and only CLI\ntraffic should not use it."],[1160,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` must be an `https://` URL: a LAN IP, or the public coder name in\npublic mode. Never `localhost` and never `127.0.0.1`."],[138,"`coder_agent_url` exists so agents keep dialing the local plain listener when\n`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is a public name."],[277,"`coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count`. `coder/preview` warns\n`Unexpanded count argument on block coder_app.*` when they are: that value is\nnot expanded at workspace-create preview. Scripts, metadata and the container\nstay gated."],[139,"`CODER_CONTAINER_SERVICE` (default `coder`), `CODER_DB_SERVICE` (default `db`),\n`CODER_CONTAINER_API_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`), `CODER_PUSH_USER`,\n`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, `DOCKER_BIN`, `PROBE_TIMEOUT` (20s) and `VERIFY_TIMEOUT`\n(60s) are the overrides."],[681,"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."],[362,"`CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX` is only for telling providers or hosts apart;\nomitted, it matches every `github.com` remote."],[272,"`coder_metadata.workspace_info` shows the image, the cloned repository (in `workspace`,\nor `none` when the Git repository field is empty), the LiteLLM URL, the default Grok\nmodel, whether DinD is `on (tcp://dind:2375)` or `off`, and - redacted - which key is\nwired in. A misconfigured workspace is therefore visible in the UI without shell\naccess. `devcontainer` adds a `devcontainer folder` tile and always reads\n`on (tcp://dind:2375)` for Docker-in-docker."],[229,"`coder_script.dind_bootstrap` is an ordering guard, not a starter: it waits up to 120s\n(180s cap) for the sidecar to answer `docker version`, because the agent runs its\n`run_on_start` scripts before it creates the dev containers."],[743,"`coder` / `coder-fast` - the Qwen2.5-Coder weights on the Arc. Chat is good, but\nthose weights write the tool call into the message body as plain text, so agent\nturns silently lose their tools. See\nlitellm-routing.md."],[256,"`connection_timeout = 300`."],[656,"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server. Grok launches\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch. The proxy config names\n`$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the workspace environment from\nthe sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled by the push from\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth, which is what\nfills `GH_TOKEN` above."],[1074,"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"],[570,"`custom.css` lives in `/app/config` and is served by the allow-listed\n`/api/config/custom.css` route. It styles the sign-in page too, so keep internal\nhostnames out of it."],[1004,"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."],[1003,"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."],[71,"`devcontainer` (`templates/devcontainer/`) is the DinD-backed one."],[703,"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."],[163,"`devcontainer` asks two questions instead: **Git repository** and **Build repo's dev\ncontainer** (`use_devcontainer`, Yes/No, immutable), plus the same three Grok fields."],[73,"`devcontainer` reuses `workspace`'s variables. `repo_url` exists in both; `dind` does\nnot exist in `devcontainer`."],[86,"`devcontainer`: **Devcontainer default** (the template default) and **Plain clone**."],[1093,"`did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` -> plan-time failure, not runtime"],[211,"`dind` is not a variable here. A devcontainer workspace without a daemon is nothing,\nand the plain-clone fallback still wants a daemon its `docker` commands can reach."],[642,"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."],[265,"`display_apps` gives: the **VS Code Desktop** helper (a locally installed VS Code /\nCursor / Windsurf plus the `coder.coder-remote` extension), port forwarding, and the\nSSH helper."],[197,"`docker build`, `docker run` and `docker compose` then work inside the workspace. The\n*AI workspace + DinD* preset is the usual way to get this shape, since the client\nbinaries ship only in the golden image."],[207,"`DOCKER_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and the `docker:29.8.1-dind` tag\nin `templates/workspace/main.tf` and `templates/devcontainer/main.tf` are the same\nengine line (29.8.1). Bump all three together."],[1205,"`DOCKER_HOST` is injected per template, not per toggle. `templates/workspace` sets\nit only while the toggle is on; `templates/devcontainer` sets it always, because\nthat template always provisions the sidecar and the `devcontainer` CLI has to\nreach its daemon either way. So a set `DOCKER_HOST` tells you the template; the\n*docker-in-docker* metadata tile tells you the state (the devcontainer template\nalways reads `on (tcp://dind:2375)`, the workspace one reads `off` when unset)."],[196,"`DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` is injected into the agent environment - absent when the\ntoggle is off."],[202,"`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` is deliberately **empty**, so the daemon serves plain\n`tcp://0.0.0.0:2375` with no TLS and no auth. (`DOCKER_TLS_CERTSIZE=0` does not do\nthis: the daemon would listen on 2376 with TLS and the client fails against 2375.)"],[1088,"`docker` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> an older image, or the wrong preset"],[339,"`env` reconciles one env file with its twin. It keeps every value you have set,\nfills a key the twin adds with the twin's default (or a reusable value already\nset in a sibling live file - `.env` filling `dev-stack.env`), and moves a key\nthe twin no longer declares into a commented trailer at the end of the file -\nparked, with its value and its explanation still readable, rather than deleted.\nComment lines always come from the twin, even when every value stays as you\nset it, so a pull that only rewrote an explanation still lands in `.env`. The\nrewrite lands in a staging file and replaces the live file only at the end,\nwith mode `600` kept. On a TTY the pass then walks every key that is new this\npull or still a placeholder (`change-me…`, `NOTSET`, `YOUR_LAN_IP`). An empty\nvalue whose example default is also empty is the intended value (LAN-only\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`, a parked MCP flag) and is not asked again. Enter keeps a\nsibling's value or the example default. A missing live file is created from\nthe twin only after a `Y/n` confirm. `--non-interactive` (and `--dry-run`)\nlist those keys and write no answers. A `summary:` line closes the pass."],[1097,"`Error: No configuration files` from `push-template` -> runfiles symlinks in the tar"],[865,"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes."],[1073,"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"],[17,"`fix/…` means a defect that is already on `main`. **A hotfix branches from `main` and lands\non `main` first** — every environment deploys *from* `main` (or from a tag cut on `main`),\nso there is no `staging` or `production` branch to patch and then remember to merge back."],[306,"`gh_token_refresh` (a `coder_script`) re-mints that pair on start and every 6 hours;\nthe minted token expires in about an hour, so between refreshes the gh auth store\ncan hold an expired credential for hours mid-day (check reports that staleness as\nREJECTED). `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the store goes\nstale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script\nprints its one note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App**\nwhen the injected App is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing.\nAfter a reconnect, restart the workspace (or wait for the next `gh_token_refresh`)\nso `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new grant; `GIT_ASKPASS` remints live\nwithout that. The refresh script unsets `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before\n`gh auth login --with-token`, because gh refuses stdin while those names are set."],[82,"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` is the shared GitHub-token refresh `coder_script`,\nrendered with `templatefile()` so `${github_auth_id}` and\n`${coder_github_app_install_url}` interpolate."],[655,"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."],[134,"`GITHUB_MCP`, `CONTEXT7_MCP` and `SEARCH_MCP` share one polarity: all three MCP blocks\nship from the template default (`true`), so the only value worth setting is `false`,\nand `true`/unset/empty pushes nothing at all."],[1165,"`grok mcp doctor searxng` inside the workspace probes the MCP hop. A healthy\nsearch-check plus a failed handshake points at the server, the URL or the port."],[76,"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the orchestration-protocol seed text the startup scripts\ninline into every workspace's `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` (read with `file()`, chomped)."],[657,"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."],[370,"`GROK_PROFILE_MODE` in the twin is the default for the template variable\n`grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also shows per workspace as the **Grok\nprofile** dropdown. Read once per push, so changing the default here needs\n`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. Overriding it for one workspace happens on\nthat dropdown and needs a rebuild, like `dind`."],[80,"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` and `coder-agents.md` are the Coder Agents\nskill and instruction seeds (chomped `file()`)."],[288,"`grok-build-terminal` keeps its own session, `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`, keyed off\nthe repository root found with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or the terminal's own\n`$PWD` outside a clone) and runs `grok --cwd <that root>` inside it."],[1086,"`grok` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> the CLI was never installed"],[753,"`group_add: ${DOCKER_GID}` plus the socket is the same credential the `coder` service\nalready holds: root-equivalent on the host. Nothing new is exposed here that the Coder\ncontainer does not already have - which is precisely why approvals, below, are the\ncontrol rather than the mount list. A public Hermes name has that same standing as\npublic Coder."],[1273,"`hermes config set` on `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is the in-container\nalternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` does not need that reseed."],[768,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` does not remove that requirement. Leave\n`approvals.mode` on `manual`."],[735,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a deprecated no-op. Setting it changes nothing and buys\nnothing, and it is not how the public name is opened."],[1262,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a no-op. Logs:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs hermes`."],[1271,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` has to be the public origin. Bootstrap writes\n`STACK_HERMES_URL` from a `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry, and compose copies\nthat into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`. The variable is environment: recreate\nthe container after bootstrap."],[754,"`HERMES_UID` / `HERMES_GID` are set to the host operator, so a file Hermes writes into\n`$STACK_HOST_PATH` is yours to commit and not owned by uid 10000."],[752,"`hermes/skills/` is tracked in git. Add a skill by editing the repo, never by editing\nthe volume; the volume copy is not the record."],[569,"`homepage/config/*.yaml` plus `custom.css` are bind-mounted read-only, so `git diff`\nstays the record of dashboard changes, and the directory itself has to stay writable\nbecause the app creates `logs/` inside it. A new config file (`kubernetes.yaml`, ...)\nneeds its own mount line in `docker-compose.yml` before the app sees it."],[254,"`host.docker.internal` mapped to `host-gateway`, so workspaces reach the Coder server\nand LiteLLM."],[566,"`https://<host>/` serves the dashboard behind a self-signed certificate that\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` issues, so expect a one-time browser warning. Port 80 redirects\nto HTTPS."],[1064,"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and Hermes are interactive\nsessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected."],[447,"`images/dev/tool-versions.env` is the single source of truth for every tool version:\nBazelisk, buildifier, shfmt, shellcheck (apt), bats, Terraform/tflint/terragrunt, the\nCoder CLI, the Docker *client* trio, Node, Python, Go, protoc and the pinned Grok CLI."],[1100,"`installed terraform version newer than expected` -> harmless"],[578,"`KASM_UI_PORT` has to match on **both** sides of the port mapping. The daemon-side\nlistener listens on that number, so the mapping is `4443:4443`."],[899,"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."],[1095,"`lookup registry.terraform.io on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving` from `push-template` -> coder cannot reach the Terraform registry"],[218,"`main.tf` therefore spells the editor's `folder` per hosting environment\n(`local.editor_folder`): `/workspaces/<repo-name>` inside the dev container,\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>` on the plain container, and\n`/home/coder` when no repository was named."],[14,"`main` is the only long-lived branch. It stays green and deployable: `main` is what a\nmachine is built from, so a merge to `main` is by definition deployable to the stack host."],[419,"`make test`/`bats`/`lint`/`fix`/`validate` and the `stack-*`/`prod-stack-*` shims are the\nsame commands;\n`make help` lists them. `//:test-fast` does not start Compose, pull models, or SSH to a Spark\nbox: the suites exercise those scripts against `tests/bats/tool_stubs`. `bazelisk run\n//scripts:<stem> -- <args>` is how a human runs one of them for real."],[994,"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."],[81,"`mcp-cache-proxy.json` is the context7 MCP cache-proxy blob the startup script\nwrites."],[863,"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."],[884,"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."],[1072,"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"],[78,"`notes-setup.sh` is the library behind each clone's private `notes.md`. The\nstartup script inlines it with `file()` the same way, writes it into\n`~/.grok/`, sources it, and calls `notes_materialize` per clone. That call also\nseeds the ignore entries that keep the file uncommitted; see \"What is shared\" in\n`docs/grok-build-and-agents.md` for what it writes into git config."],[665,"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`. The startup script copies that file here on every start and restores\nit when a recreate leaves the clone without one. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no\nshared volume, so those notes die with that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like\n`config.toml`, the last workspace started for a given user and repository wins."],[868,"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` matches the CLI's own streaming idle\nallowance, so a slow call may take a full twenty minutes before either end gives up."],[946,"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."],[1052,"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."],[1089,"`Permission denied` / `jq: error: Could not open file` on `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` -> empty named volume is root-owned"],[1096,"`pq: value too long for type character varying(256)` from `push-template` -> editor icon data URL"],[372,"`private`: no nested mount. Each workspace gets a private `~/.grok` on its own\nhome volume, so its `config.toml` - plus memory, skills and logs - differs per\nworkspace and dies with the workspace."],[661,"`private`: that workspace's `~/.grok` - `config.toml` included - lives on its own\nhome volume, differs from its siblings, and dies with the workspace (or survives a\nrebuild on the same volume)."],[963,"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."],[188,"`push-template.sh` re-sends both after every push, and `new-workspace.sh` names no\n`--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline either."],[363,"`push-template.sh` reads `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` from `coder.env` and\nforwards it as `--var github_auth_id=` so the templates declare the same id.\nChanging the id orphans existing GitHub account links."],[949,"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."],[891,"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."],[539,"`real_bin <tool>` resolves a tool with the stub directory removed from PATH. That is the\nintegration-tier gate: `if [ -z \"$(real_bin terraform)\" ]; then skip \"terraform not\ninstalled\"; fi` — a test that could talk to a real daemon skips when the tool is absent\ninstead of failing."],[1110,"`Refusing to bind dashboard to 0.0.0.0` -> empty Hermes basic-auth env"],[530,"`REPO_ROOT` — where the checkout is *for this run*. `tests/bats_runner.sh` exports it;\noutside Bazel it is whatever `bats_canonical_repo_root` resolves. Derive it, never\nhardcode a machine path, and treat \"cannot resolve\" as a `skip`, not a failure."],[136,"`repo_url` prefills the Git repository field on both create forms. The Terraform\ndefault is still no clone (`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`).\n`.env.sample` sets `REPO_URL` to this stack's GitHub URL, so a normal push prefills\n`https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git` and **VS Code Web** opens\n`~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. A blank `REPO_URL=` line stays unpassed."],[864,"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."],[425,"`rules_shell` only symlinks an `sh_test` or `sh_binary` source when that file is executable.\n`//tools:exec_shell.bzl` writes a generated launcher and runs the real `.sh` from runfiles\nwith bash, so a checkout whose scripts are mode `100644` still builds."],[1138,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in its logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[948,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[424,"`scripts/BUILD.bazel` declares one `exec_shell` launcher plus one `sh_binary` wrapper per\n`scripts/*.sh`, generated from the directory, so a new CLI is a Bazel target without a second\nedit there. `//:validate` is an alias for `//scripts:validate`; `//:fix` keeps the same\nlauncher pattern at the repo root."],[895,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (default),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. The four Spark aliases all drive tools, so all four are registerable - the\nthinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that picker."],[943,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (default),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`)."],[58,"`scripts/github-auth.sh`, the per-workspace doctor"],[462,"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker:"],[409,"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"],[670,"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."],[904,"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."],[280,"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`. Startup chowns\nthat volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume) to the home owner before\nthe write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`. A failed write logs a warning instead of running `jq`/`python`\nagainst a file that was never created."],[660,"`shared` (the default here, so a workspace gets the profile unless you turn the\nmode off): configure the CLI once and the rest of that account's workspaces\ninherit it."],[371,"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts that user's shared\ngrok-profile volume nested inside `/home/coder`, so `config.toml`, the MCP warm\ncaches, `memory-v2`, skills and a self-installed CLI are one profile shared by\nthat account's workspaces. It is re-rendered on every start and the\n**last-started workspace wins** for all of them."],[141,"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."],[765,"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."],[385,"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box. Empty is not a working value - LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at\nclient construction (`AuthenticationError`, before reaching the box) and `agent`\nfalls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark` is not a secret and is\nignored by a keyless backend."],[386,"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box."],[383,"`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` are the Ollama endpoints behind the `spark-<n>` aliases. The\nplaceholder hostnames are on purpose: replace them with real Ollama URLs or\nleave them as-is, and those aliases simply fail when used."],[384,"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` are the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoints. A workspace pins\nits session to one healthy box; fresh session-id-less traffic fans out by\n`least-busy`, shortest in-flight queue first."],[1046,"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL`,\nthe card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills `STACK_HERMES_URL`.\nCompose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the Host and Origin\nthe Chat tab checks;"],[1045,"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"],[751,"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find. Empty (CI\n`compose config`) falls back to `.:/opt/stack`. Seeded `terminal.cwd` is\n`${STACK_HOST_PATH}`, expanded from the same env."],[1268,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from\n`proxy/router.conf`."],[375,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch. Empty keeps everything below LAN-only and\nevery derived `STACK_*_URL` stays empty; otherwise set the comma-separated public\nDNS names in the hyphenated one-label form the rules below define; `.env.sample`\nshows a worked example."],[255,"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."],[1184,"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."],[1202,"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."],[223,"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."],[971,"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."],[533,"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."],[538,"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."],[283,"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."],[100,"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."],[75,"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."],[1001,"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `coder-agents.md`,\n`mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown and JSON are\nread with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must not\nre-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."],[758,"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."],[285,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[638,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[99,"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."],[519,"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."],[515,"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."],[516,"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."],[518,"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."],[990,"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."],[520,"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"],[517,"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."],[1054,"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."],[79,"`tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`, and `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the\nterminal payloads the startup script writes. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."],[1102,"`Unexpanded count argument on block \"coder_app.coder_ui\"` -> apps were gated on start_count"],[1099,"`unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"` -> Authelia answered the CLI"],[883,"`unverified` on the `spark-*` rows means exactly that: this box never ran them\nagainst a live Spark, so the column records the absence of a measurement, not a\n\"no\"."],[461,"`updateRemoteUserUID` is on, so the container's `coder` uid matches the host user and the\nbind-mounted checkout stays writable without a `chmod` dance."],[77,"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."],[287,"`vscode-terminal-tmux` re-joins or starts one shared session per folder, named\n`vscode-<folder>-<hash>`; with no `tmux` on PATH it falls back to a plain login shell."],[534,"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."],[682,"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."],[70,"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."],[351,"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\". The pass now prints which\ndaemon it addressed and tells you to use a host shell, or to name the target:"],[72,"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."],[296,"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."],[340,"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name.\nThe reseed set is the seven home-relative targets the templates seed -\n`~/.tmux.conf`, `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.coder/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`, the two `grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md` copies and\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` - and the same rule applies as in\n`refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it still carries its\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker (or, for the settings file, its\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key), so a config you hand-edit in a terminal is your\nfile and this tool will not move it. `--force` overrides that, per run, and says\nso."],[85,"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."],[1175,"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."],[1176,"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."],[790,"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"],[787,"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"],[788,"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"],[789,"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"],[871,"| Alias          | Model                | Backend                                                                             | Structured tool calls                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-medium` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `medium` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |"],[872,"| Alias         | Model                | Backend                                                                            | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-xhigh` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |"],[876,"| Alias         | Model               | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `SPARK1_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |"],[874,"| Alias        | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder-fast` | `qwen2.5-coder:14b` |         | no (measured)         |"],[877,"| Alias        | Model        | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------ | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` | `SPARK2_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |"],[870,"| Alias       | Model                | Backend                                                                          | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ----------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-low` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `low` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |"],[869,"| Alias   | Model                | Backend                 