e CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."],[116,"Same trust model as `coder reset-password`, which also bypasses the API and talks to\nthe database directly."],[510,"Sandbox-safe repo-root probing"],[504,"Sandbox-safe: probe for the repo root, then say so"],[6,"Scope"],[395,"Script targets and runfiles"],[400,"Script targets, runfiles, and why a new `scripts/*.sh` is a target automatically"],[684,"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"],[978,"Scripts"],[620,"Search lane and MCP servers"],[1080,"Search never answers (or answers empty) -> prove the search lane before blaming the MCP hop"],[1163,"Search rides Grok's native `web_search` tool by default (`disable_web_search =\nfalse`). The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the fallback, declared by\ndefault (`search_mcp` defaults to true). Two probes split a SearXNG fault in half:"],[1057,"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."],[769,"Secrets live in gitignored `.env` and in the `hermes-data` volume. Never commit them,\nand never paste `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` into a chat to prove a route works."],[716,"Security notes"],[431,"Seeding the contributor home"],[261,"Seeds the shared code-server User settings and the Coder Agents context files, only\nfor files that are absent."],[475,"SELF means no docker at all and no `DOCKER_HOST`: one probe of this environment's own\ncredential chain, printed as one `self:` line per credential. `docker` in here addresses\nthe workstation daemon mounted in, never the stack host's workspace fleet."],[474,"SELF mode applies when `CODER_WORKSPACE_ID` is set **or** `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` is. The\ncontainer sets the latter; a Coder workspace sets the former."],[1011,"Serving the same five services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."],[146,"Set a token first. `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN` is any token from the Coder **Tokens** page or\nfrom `coder tokens create`."],[662,"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared profile while its siblings stay inside it."],[1050,"Set the five names in `.env` (`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`; `.env.sample` lists them, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`), and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs\nbeside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap` and recreate the stack you switched. The\ncommands are in the README."],[381,"Set the names in `.env`, and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside\nthe stable one, then re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and recreate the\nstack you switched. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is part of that list:\nbootstrap writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`, and the LAN `:9119` listener stays either\nway. Leave the hermes name out and the Homepage card keeps the LAN URL.\npublic-access.md owns the naming rule, the measured\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` on the dotted form, the request path and the\nchecks to run on the Traefik box."],[43,"Shape"],[987,"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."],[854,"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[1261,"Sign-in is `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.env`\n(default `admin` / `change-me-hermes` until bootstrap rotates the\nplaceholders). To fill missing keys and replace those placeholders:"],[731,"Sign-in is HTTP basic auth from `.env`: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` (default `admin`) and\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`. Bootstrap generates the password; it is **not** the\nHomepage password, and `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` does not rotate it.\nThat basic auth stays on every door. Hermes v0.22.5 refuses to bind `0.0.0.0` with no\nprovider. On the public URL Authelia challenges first and basic auth challenges\nsecond; two logins are expected. LAN `:9119`, and LAN `:8080` with this name's Host\nheader, never see Authelia, so basic auth is the credential on those paths."],[719,"Sign-in: HTTP basic auth, with Authelia in front of the public name"],[1183,"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."],[944,"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."],[543,"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails:"],[214,"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."],[892,"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."],[1209,"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."],[952,"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."],[326,"Spark agent model group"],[897,"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."],[772,"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"],[1139,"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"],[1071,"Stack bring-up"],[1115,"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"],[392,"Stack-host bootstrap"],[396,"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"],[778,"Start here"],[601,"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"],[1120,"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"],[744,"Switch it inside the container with `hermes config set model.model chat` (or\n`agent-medium` for a thinking level), which edits the live config under `/opt/data`."],[374,"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the shared\nprofile holds and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."],[1070,"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."],[1211,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."],[1215,"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."],[1218,"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."],[333,"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"],[1026,"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"],[445,"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"],[119,"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."],[39,"Template variables"],[164,"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace."],[284,"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."],[979,"Templates"],[55,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[613,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[63,"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"],[627,"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"],[1094,"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI"],[496,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[976,"Tests"],[934,"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."],[489,"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."],[329,"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"],[225,"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."],[291,"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."],[144,"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."],[208,"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."],[480,"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."],[109,"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."],[726,"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."],[369,"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."],[1207,"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."],[917,"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."],[1158,"The **app lane**: the **LiteLLM** button in the Coder dashboard, at\n`https://<coder name>/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/litellm`. `coder_app.litellm` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so that path rides the *coder* name and `coder:3000`, and\n`litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted. See the next heading."],[732,"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."],[1172,"The **LiteLLM** and **Open VS Code Web** buttons are Coder *apps*: their URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` on the coder hostname, because both `coder_app`\nblocks set `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane failure, and a 200\nfrom `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."],[1157,"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."],[234,"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."],[522,"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."],[544,"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."],[1275,"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."],[1151,"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."],[885,"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."],[382,"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"],[878,"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."],[675,"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which rides the owner's shared\nprofile volume in `shared` mode, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs both network and `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`\n(optional - empty means the public rate limit), while an identical lookup inside the\nTTL is answered locally. Two tools, in order -\n`resolve-library-id`, then `query-docs` on the id it returns. Prove it with\n`grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."],[278,"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."],[275,"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs fresh under `/tmp/code-server` on\nevery start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."],[42,"The `devcontainer` template"],[540,"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."],[674,"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."],[180,"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `coder`, `coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the\nvariable names another one."],[1137,"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."],[882,"The `spark-*` rows are a different thing: the same Qwen2.5-Coder and Qwen2.5\nweights, served over Ollama on the Spark boxes instead of on the Arc."],[27,"The `templates/common/` payloads"],[639,"The `tmux` profile re-joins (or starts) one session per folder, named\n`vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A second terminal window, a browser reload, or\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands in that same session, and a long job\nsurvives closing the tab. `tmux ls` lists the names."],[274,"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when it is missing** from\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the folder and, when\nthe folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for `<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings live on the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume seed, not\nin a per-start merge."],[929,"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."],[836,"The Agent backend card"],[822,"The alias table"],[846,"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"],[805,"The assistant on the host"],[484,"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."],[786,"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."],[365,"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."],[928,"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, so they follow the LAN, and they answer without a key."],[680,"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."],[1263,"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."],[1159,"The checks that apply to either lane:"],[242,"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."],[594,"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."],[802,"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."],[473,"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"],[391,"The contributor path"],[397,"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"],[857,"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."],[919,"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."],[279,"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."],[204,"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"],[201,"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."],[398,"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"],[926,"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."],[1028,"The default shape is LAN-only. Setting `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` additionally serves the same\nfive services on public names over real TLS. It is purely additive: every LAN route\nkeeps working."],[624,"The delegation sketch"],[1250,"The denial is about the **grant**, not the credential format. A\nconnected-but-unscoped credential authenticates and is still refused: 403, or 404\nwhere GitHub hides a private repo's existence (a repo hidden from the credential\nanswers 404, or 403, on the read lane and the write lane alike). And reads prove\nnothing about push: a public repo answers read probes for any authenticated\ncaller, so a credential that READS a repo can still be refused every WRITE to it."],[1241,"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."],[942,"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"],[213,"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."],[251,"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."],[150,"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."],[574,"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."],[1253,"The doctor, from a host shell or from inside a workspace, names the credentials a\nworkspace's git and gh could present and answers \"can this credential WRITE to the repo you\npass?\". It probes the endpoint `git push` negotiates through: one GET of\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`. A REFUSED-GRANT answer means the\ncredential authenticates while every write to that repo is denied; NOT VISIBLE\nmeans the repo is hidden from the credential (or absent); REJECTED means github.com\nrefuses the credential outright, as it does an expired mint:"],[644,"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."],[486,"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."],[295,"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."],[981,"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"],[545,"The file probe (`-f docker-compose.yml`) says \"this is a repo root\"; the `command -v` probe\nof a tool that cannot exist inside the sandbox says \"and it is the real checkout, not a\ncopy\". Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case."],[760,"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."],[1278,"The first registered account is the site admin."],[1013,"The five names"],[1021,"The five names, and how to choose them at bootstrap time"],[252,"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."],[614,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image"],[628,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"],[911,"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."],[558,"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput"],[733,"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."],[548,"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."],[645,"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."],[579,"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."],[450,"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."],[53,"The inlined code-server fork"],[524,"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"],[945,"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."],[740,"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."],[596,"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."],[575,"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page."],[505,"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"],[331,"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"],[658,"The managed block is guarded by a `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker, so a\nhand-edited config is left alone."],[838,"The measured burst"],[668,"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."],[441,"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"],[526,"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"],[478,"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"],[804,"The model lane"],[1116,"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"],[817,"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."],[890,"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."],[720,"The models it calls through LiteLLM"],[230,"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."],[379,"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."],[709,"The on-host assistant with the Web Dashboard on :9119: how to reach it on the LAN and on its public name, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command."],[311,"The one copy on disk is the per-workspace gh auth store (0600) that `gh_token_refresh`\nmaintains."],[845,"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"],[429,"The one image, the one pin file"],[428,"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it is seeded, and which path is the merge gate."],[844,"The one-front-door rule"],[588,"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"],[808,"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"],[1060,"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."],[567,"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."],[559,"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"],[1269,"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[1012,"The path a public request takes"],[1020,"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"],[302,"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."],[829,"The picker: least-busy"],[906,"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."],[918,"The pin itself is kept."],[830,"The pin: session_affinity"],[181,"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."],[931,"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."],[1265,"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"],[766,"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`). It does not change\nwhat the container can do: local terminal, host mounts and the docker socket still\nmean this agent can affect the real host. Treat its approvals the way you treat a\nroot shell. That is the same standing as public Coder."],[886,"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."],[1248,"The refresh script inherits `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` from the agent. `gh auth\nlogin --with-token` refuses stdin while those names are set. Current templates\nunset the pair, log out, then log in with `--git-protocol https`, and print gh's\nstderr (token redacted) if that still fails."],[629,"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"],[615,"The rendered `~/.grok` config"],[264,"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."],[737,"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."],[1031,"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."],[806,"The rules a change follows"],[1140,"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"],[1127,"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."],[1136,"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."],[1189,"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."],[36,"The same push, by hand"],[115,"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."],[631,"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"],[289,"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."],[1272,"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"],[756,"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."],[706,"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume. They are *not*\non the shared `~/.grok` profile volume above, and seeding does not care whether\nthat mount exists."],[488,"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."],[145,"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."],[653,"The seven aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field."],[973,"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."],[800,"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."],[437,"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"],[775,"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."],[503,"The stub-PATH recipe"],[509,"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"],[143,"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."],[121,"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."],[702,"The text is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced). It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."],[879,"The three `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter."],[1167,"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default. Park one\nwith `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` / `SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` and\na template re-push. `grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared\nservers and their handshake."],[199,"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."],[117,"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."],[405,"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"],[909,"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."],[1023,"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"],[380,"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`proxy/router.conf` picks the service from `$http_host` and listens on\n`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back as `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the four interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[955,"The two commands"],[959,"The two commands an operator runs most"],[506,"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"],[617,"The two modes"],[319,"The two passes"],[328,"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"],[502,"The two paths a suite may use"],[45,"The two questions"],[1223,"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"],[1276,"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."],[195,"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."],[1161,"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."],[1252,"Then check the URL scheme. That credential covers `https://github.com/...` remotes\nonly, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets neither helper nor askpass token\nand needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this stack provisions nowhere."],[364,"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."],[304,"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."],[606,"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."],[599,"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."],[1192,"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."],[147,"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"],[153,"Then re-pin both values:"],[1203,"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."],[122,"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."],[925,"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."],[137,"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."],[335,"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."],[848,"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"],[1225,"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."],[1058,"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."],[785,"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."],[420,"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."],[881,"Those three groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not three\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."],[896,"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."],[1206,"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"],[1029,"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."],[761,"To reseed deliberately:"],[1178,"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."],[824,"Tool calls: the measured limitation"],[847,"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"],[1274,"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."],[1016,"Traefik configuration"],[1069,"Troubleshooting"],[609,"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."],[2,"Trunk-based branch rules: main is the only long-lived branch, work branches are short-lived, and a pull request is the only way back in."],[13,"Trunk-based, GitHub-Flow shaped: one long-lived branch, short-lived work branches, and a pull\nrequest as the only way back into `main`."],[819,"Two doors, one key"],[1019,"Two follow-ups, both by hand"],[499,"Two gates, one rule"],[286,"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`."],[1173,"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"],[334,"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."],[1156,"Two lanes reach that port and they share nothing but the destination, so a probe on one\nanswers nothing about the other."],[469,"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"],[563,"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."],[69,"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."],[811,"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."],[666,"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."],[1109,"UI and logins"],[809,"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"],[148,"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."],[452,"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."],[571,"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"],[947,"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."],[1104,"VS Code Web opens `/home/coder` instead of the cloned repository -> last workspace won"],[1063,"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."],[1022,"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"],[1015,"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"],[7,"What `main` is allowed to be, and what may not be committed to it"],[439,"What `seed-dev-env` materializes into that home, and what it refuses to touch"],[155,"What a create form shows, in `workspace` order."],[826,"What agent mode needs"],[820,"What an alias is"],[394,"What Bazel brings"],[399,"What Bazel brings over running the scripts directly"],[831,"What carries a session id"],[434,"What CI does"],[442,"What CI does, and why this repo's own Coder workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[10,"What CI runs, what branch protection should require, and where secrets go"],[557,"What each dashboard card shows and which API it reads"],[832,"What floats freely"],[318,"What goes stale"],[317,"What goes stale after a `git pull`, what `update-configs` refreshes, and what it deliberately will not touch."],[618,"What is shared"],[49,"What it decides"],[30,"What it does and does not do"],[47,"What it reuses, and the trust warning"],[550,"What it shows"],[322,"What it will not do"],[468,"What it writes: `~/.tmux.conf`, the shared `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` (with the\ncode-server and desktop VS Code User settings symlinked to it), `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`, the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, the two terminal helpers in\n`~/.grok/bin`, `~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` plus this clone's `notes.md`, and the\n`~/.grok/bin` line in `~/.bashrc`."],[779,"What runs here"],[612,"What runs inside a workspace and in the Coder control plane: terminal profiles, the shared `~/.grok` profile, and where models come from."],[780,"What runs on the box and which door each service answers on"],[625,"What seeds the agent's context"],[721,"What the container mounts, and what \"be the host\" costs"],[51,"What the startup script does, in order"],[26,"What the two templates make"],[59,"What the two templates make, and when the `devcontainer` toggle matters"],[436,"What this is not"],[626,"What this is not"],[587,"What to read next"],[825,"What was measured"],[833,"When a pinned box cools or goes unhealthy"],[920,"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."],[495,"When the container itself is the suspect, `bazelisk run //:validate` from inside it is the\nfastest answer, and ../troubleshooting.md covers the stack-side\nfailures it rules in or out."],[9,"Where a hotfix branches from and where it lands first"],[840,"Where an alias edit lands"],[32,"Where it runs, and why"],[330,"Where the command may run, and where it refuses to"],[956,"Where to look"],[321,"Where to run it"],[781,"Where to start depending on what you came to do"],[837,"Which box answered"],[327,"Which files go stale and why `git pull` cannot fix them"],[958,"Which page answers which operational question"],[807,"Which page explains which part of the stack"],[508,"Which runtime actually gates a merge, and which one is a convenience"],[501,"Which runtime gates"],[44,"Why the shared bind path is load-bearing"],[435,"Why this repo's own workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[841,"Why this shape"],[604,"Wire the control-plane chat agent — print the intended end state, then apply it:"],[930,"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."],[193,"With it on, per workspace:"],[936,"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."],[641,"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."],[226,"With the toggle off - or on a repo that carries no `devcontainer.json`, where the build\nhas nothing to build - the editor attaches to the plain workspace agent instead, opened\non the same clone path."],[224,"With the toggle on, `coder_devcontainer.repo` then builds the repo's own\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` on the sidecar through the Coder agent\n(`@devcontainers/cli` is installed into the parent workspace container by a\n`run_on_start` module, and the golden image does not ship it)."],[294,"Without the flag the script passes exactly the parameters it used to pass, and the create\nform's **Git repositories (additional)** textarea stays empty."],[4,"Work branches"],[759,"Working agreement with the assistant: it explains the plan in chat, then waits for the\napproval card in the Chat tab before running anything mutating. If a run looks like it\nis acting without one, stop it and re-read the mode."],[1066,"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."],[1079,"Workspace cannot reach LiteLLM -> `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, or `host.docker.internal`"],[1084,"Workspace stuck in *starting* -> startup is blocking"],[856,"Workspaces and desktops always talk to one base URL,\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, and pick a model alias. That is the\nhost-gateway door."],[1067,"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."],[951,"Workspaces see a stable OpenAI-compatible URL and stable alias names."],[1083,"Workspaces: startup, images, the toolchain inside them"],[1117,"Workspaces: startup, the golden image, Grok Build (`/learn`, MCP), the toolchain"],[260,"Writes the two terminal-profile tmux helpers (see below)."],[985,"Writing a script that passes `//:lint` and `//tests:doc_coverage` first try"]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":25,"2":26,"3":27,"4":28,"5":29,"6":30,"7":31,"8":32,"9":33,"10":34,"11":35,"12":36,"13":37,"14":38,"15":39,"16":40,"17":41,"18":42,"